\begin{abstract}
This paper addresses the challenge of discrete phase-shift optimization in reconfigurable intelligent surface (RIS)-aided multiuser communication systems.
Practical RIS hardware often utilizes low-resolution phase shifters, leading to challenging combinatorial optimization problems.
To address this challenge, we propose a Bussgang-based latent lifting (BuLL) method that represents discrete phase shifts as quantized outputs of continuous latent variables.
Our BuLL method calibrates the Bussgang gain and distortion covariance under a reference distribution and keeps these moments fixed during deterministic latent optimization.
These fixed moments are then used to construct a distortion-aware continuous surrogate that incorporates reference-averaged discretization effects without penalty terms.
For uplink channel estimation, we develop a matrix quadratic transform-based algorithm to optimize a mean-square-error  surrogate.
For downlink beamforming, we develop a generalized power iteration-based RIS phase-shift optimization algorithm for a spectral efficiency (SE) surrogate.
Numerical results demonstrate improvements in channel estimation accuracy and sum SE over conventional benchmarks in the evaluated low-resolution RIS settings.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Reconfigurable intelligent surface, discrete optimization, Bussgang theorem, channel estimation, beamforming.
\end{IEEEkeywords}
\vspace{-0.55cm}
\section{Introduction}
Reconfigurable intelligent surface (RIS) has emerged as a key enabling technology for next-generation wireless communication systems \cite{kumar2025ris}. 
Composed of numerous low-cost passive reflecting elements, RIS can reshape the wireless propagation environment to enhance signal quality and suppress interference without requiring active radio-frequency chains \cite{di2020smart}. 
Consequently, RIS-aided communications have attracted research attention across various applications \cite{wu2019intelligent, kundu2021channel, zheng2022survey}.
While many existing studies assume ideal, continuous (infinite-resolution) phase shifts for theoretical simplicity, hardware implementations impose stringent constraints. 
To maintain low hardware cost and minimal power consumption, practical RIS prototypes are predominantly developed with extremely low-resolution phase shifters, typically 1-bit \cite{pei2021ris} or 2-bit \cite{zhao20242}. 
Therefore, it is  more practical and realistic to design RIS-aided systems under the assumption of discrete phase shifts rather than idealized continuous ones \cite{wu2019beamforming, gao2021robust, ramezani2024joint}.
However, considering discrete phase-shift optimization leads to significant technical challenges.
The discrete nature of the hardware fundamentally transforms the RIS optimization into mixed-integer nonlinear programming (MINLP), which is generally NP-hard \cite{parker2014discrete, burer2012non}. 
In particular, since an exhaustive search over all possible configurations is computationally prohibitive for practical RIS sizes, developing efficient algorithms that effectively handle discrete phase-shift constraints is of paramount importance.
\subsection{Related Works}
Existing approaches for discrete RIS optimization generally fall into three categories: relaxation-based, penalty-based, and combinatorial or heuristic search methods.
The most widely adopted strategy is the relaxation-then-quantization approach \cite{wu2019beamforming_icassp, so2007approximating, di2020hybrid}. 
In this paradigm, discrete phase-shift constraints are relaxed to continuous unit-modulus constraints, solved via techniques such as semidefinite relaxation \cite{gao2021robust}, successive convex approximation \cite{pan2020multicell}, and  manifold optimization \cite{manifold_ref, guo2020weighted, pan2022overview}, then projected to the nearest discrete values.
While computationally tractable, these methods perform a 
\emph{deterministic relaxation} in which the relaxed continuous variable 
bears no modeled statistical relationship to its eventual discrete value. 
Consequently, the continuous optimization is entirely agnostic to the 
subsequent quantization, leading to performance degradation that is particularly definite in low-resolution regimes where the discretization error is severe.
To mitigate discretization loss, the \textit{penalty-based} approach \cite{zhu2025discrete} augments the objective function to enforce discrete constraints during optimization. 
While yielding improved performance, it suffers from high computational complexity due to double-loop iterations and relies heavily on empirical parameter tuning.
Alternatively, direct discrete optimization relies on \textit{combinatorial search} techniques. 
For instance, element-wise coordinate descent \cite{wu2019beamforming} sequentially optimizes each RIS element, while phase intercept methods \cite{ren2022linear} evaluate a reduced set of candidate phase shifts to reduce complexity. 
Nevertheless, most of these existing strategies lack strict theoretical performance guarantees and are susceptible to being trapped in local optima. 
Furthermore, a common drawback of these strategies is the difficulty of seamlessly integrating them with well-established continuous optimization theories, often necessitating the development of specialized, problem-specific solvers. 
These observations motivate our work.
Discrete optimization is closely related to the quantization operation, which is inherently a nonlinear mapping from continuous to discrete values. 
For handling nonlinear quantization operation, the Bussgang decomposition \cite{bussgang1952crosscorrelation,demir2020bussgang} provides a powerful tool for statistically linearizing nonlinear operations by decomposing the output of a nonlinear function into a linear signal component and an uncorrelated distortion noise term. 
In particular, this approach has been extensively employed in the context of low-resolution quantizers for massive multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) systems, where it enables tractable performance analysis and receiver/precoder designs by effectively capturing the quantization effects \cite{mezghani2012capacity,aghdam2019distortion,wan2020generalized,choi2022energy}. 
These prior works primarily adopt the Bussgang decomposition as an analysis tool to characterize the distortion introduced by hardware-imposed quantization on physical signals.
In contrast, rather than modeling the quantization effect on an observed signal, we introduce continuous latent variables and establish their statistical connection to the discrete optimization  via the Bussgang decomposition under a reference distribution.
This latent variable formulation replaces the original discrete optimization problem with a distortion-aware continuous surrogate, where well-established continuous optimization techniques become readily applicable.
\subsection{Contributions}
In this paper, we propose a \emph{Bussgang-based latent lifting (BuLL)} method that provides a principled approach to solving discrete optimization problems arising in RIS-aided communication systems.
The main contributions  are summarized as
\begin{itemize}
 \item 






 We develop the BuLL method for discrete optimization that represents discrete variables as quantized outputs of continuous latent variables.
 Using the Bussgang decomposition~\cite{demir2020bussgang}, we derive closed-form Bussgang moments for the considered discrete mapper under a Gaussian reference distribution.
 These moments remain fixed during latent optimization and incorporate reference-averaged discretization effects into a distortion-aware continuous surrogate.
 Our BuLL method extends the role of the Bussgang decomposition from an analysis tool to the construction of a continuous optimization surrogate.
 \item 
 We apply the BuLL method to RIS beam pattern design for uplink channel estimation in a discrete RIS-aided system.
 By lifting the discrete variable to a continuous latent variable, the BuLL framework replaces the discrete-constrained design with a continuous mean square error (MSE) surrogate.
 The surrogate is optimized via the matrix quadratic transform (QT) \cite{shen2018fractional}.


 \item 
 We further extend our BuLL method to RIS-aided beamforming design for downlink sum spectral efficiency (SE) maximization in a multiuser multiple-input single-output (MU-MISO) system.
 We propose a generalized power iteration (GPI)-based phase-shift optimization method for the distortion-aware SE surrogate.
 This eliminates the penalty-parameter tuning required by the existing GPI-based RIS phase-shift optimization in~\cite{oh2026scalable}.

\end{itemize}
