\section{System Model} \label{sec:sys_model}
We consider a RIS-aided multiuser communication system in which a base station (BS) is equipped with $N$ antennas and serves $K$ single-antenna users.
We assume that direct links between the BS and users are blocked.
The RIS comprises  $M$ reflecting elements characterized by the diagonal phase-shift matrix ${\boldsymbol{\Phi}} = {\rm diag}(\bphi)$ where $\bphi = [\phi_{1}, \phi_{2}, \ldots, \phi_{M}]^{\sf T}$ and $\phi_m = e^{j\theta_m}$ for all $m \in \{1, 2, \ldots, M\}$.
We consider discrete RIS elements with $b$-bit phase resolution. 
To model this discrete nature, we define the set of feasible phase values $\bbF$ with cardinality $L=2^b$, which are uniformly distributed on the unit circle. 
The discrete set is given by
\begin{align}
 \bbF = \left\{ e^{j \frac{2\pi l}{L}} \; \bigg| \; l = 0, 1, \dots, L-1 \right\}. 
\end{align}
For a given $b$-bit, each phase shift is restricted to take one of the discrete values in $\bbF$, i.e., $\phi_m \in \bbF$ for all $m \in \{1, 2, \ldots, M\}$.
In the channel model, we assume a time-division duplexing (TDD) protocol.
Let $\bH_{1} \in \mathbb{C}^{N \times M}$ denote a channel matrix from the RIS to the BS, and let $\bh_{2,k} \in \mathbb{C}^{M}$ denote a channel vector from the $k$-th user to the RIS.
The RIS-user channel matrix is defined as $\bH_{2} = [\bh_{2,1}, \dots, \bh_{2,K}] \in \mathbb{C}^{M \times K}$.
Under the assumption of quasi-static block fading, an effective channel matrix $\bH \in \bbC^{N \times K}$ encompassing RIS phase shifts is given by
\begin{align}
 \label{eq:ch}
 {\bH = \bH_{1} {\boldsymbol{\Phi}} \bH_{2} = [{\bf h}_1, \dots, {\bf h}_K],}
\end{align}
where $\bh_k \in \mathbb{C}^{N}$ represents the effective channel vector of the $k$-th user.
\section{BuLL Discrete Optimization} \label{sec:bussgang}
In this section, we establish a novel discrete optimization framework to address the severe intractability arising from the discrete RIS phase shifters.
The core idea is to lift the target discrete optimization variable into a continuous latent space by exploiting the statistical relationship.
We remark that  such a latent variable does not physically exist in the system.
Specifically, we view each discrete variable as arising from the discretization of an underlying continuous latent variable, which allows the original objective function to be reformulated in terms of the latent variable and discrete mapping. 
\subsection{BuLL Framework \label{subsec:latent_bussgang}}
We begin by reviewing the Bussgang decomposition \cite{bussgang1952crosscorrelation, demir2020bussgang}, which provides a principled framework for statistically linearizing nonlinear operations.
\begin{theorem}[Bussgang decomposition \cite{bussgang1952crosscorrelation, demir2020bussgang}]
 \label{th:Buss_decom}
 Let $\bx$ be a zero-mean complex Gaussian random vector with nonsingular covariance. 
 The output  of a nonlinear  function $\cU(\cdot)$ can be decomposed into a linear signal component of $\bx$ and a distortion noise term $\bd$ as 
 \begin{align}
 \label{eq:general_Bussgang_decom}
 {\bz} = \cU(\bx) = \bB \bx + \bd,
 \end{align}
 where $\bB = \bC_{\bz\bx}\bC_{\bx\bx}^{-1}$ is the Bussgang gain matrix determined in the minimum mean square error (MMSE) sense between $\bz$ and $\bB\bx$, and $\bd$ is the distortion noise satisfying $\bbE[\bd\bx^{\sf H}] = \mathbf{0}$.
\end{theorem}
To understand the conventional use of the Bussgang decomposition, we first present a typical application:
\begin{example}[Low-resolution quantization in massive MIMO]
 \label{ex:mimo_quantization}
 \normalfont
 Theorem~\ref{th:Buss_decom} is widely used to model low-resolution analog-to-digital converters (ADCs) in MIMO receivers \cite{mezghani2012capacity, wan2020generalized, choi2022energy}.
 Consider the received signal $\by_{\rm rx} = \bar{\bH}\bx_{\rm tx} + \bar{\bn} \in \bbC^{N}$, where $\bx_{\rm tx} \sim \cC\cN({\bf 0}, \bR_{\bx\bx})$ and $\bar{\bn} \sim \cC\cN({\bf 0}, \bR_{\bn\bn})$ are independent.
 Since $\by_{\rm rx}$ is a physical signal, its distribution is determined by the signal and noise statistics as $\by_{\rm rx} \sim \cC\cN(\mathbf{0}, \bar{\bH}\bR_{\bx\bx}\bar{\bH}^{\sf H} + \bR_{\bn\bn})$.
 Applying Theorem~\ref{th:Buss_decom} to the ADC output yields
 \begin{align}
 \label{eq:conventional_bussgang}
 \br = \cQ_{\sf adc}(\by_{\rm rx}) =
 \bB_{\sf adc}\by_{\rm rx} + \bd_{\sf adc},
 \end{align}
 where $\bB_{\sf adc}$ is the Bussgang gain matrix and $\bd_{\sf adc}$ is the distortion noise satisfying $\bbE[\bd_{\sf adc}\by_{\rm rx}^{\sf H}] = \mathbf{0}$.
 Here, $\br$ serves as an observation for estimating $\bx_{\rm tx}$ (or $\bar{\bH}$), and the Bussgang decomposition acts as an {\it analytical tool} for characterizing hardware-induced quantization distortion.
\end{example}
Unlike the conventional usage discussed in Example~\ref{ex:mimo_quantization}, we now propose a {BuLL method}; we introduce a continuous latent variable $\breve{\bphi} \in \bbC^{M}$ that does not physically exist in the system and deliberately interpret the discrete phase-shift vector $\bphi \in \bbF^{M}$ as if it were the quantized output of this latent variable.
The detailed steps are presented as follows:
\subsubsection{Introduction of Latent Bussgang Variable} 
 Introducing a continuous latent variable $\breve{\bphi} \in \bbC^M$, we consider the discrete phase-shift vector ${\bphi}$ as an output of a continuous-to-discrete mapper. 
 For the following calibration, $\breve{\bphi}_{\rm ref}$ is a reference draw with i.i.d. $\mathcal{CN}(0,1)$ entries.
 We write $\breve{\bphi}$ for $\breve{\bphi}_{\rm ref}$ for brevity, and all expectations refer to this reference law.
 Accordingly, Theorem~\ref{th:Buss_decom} gives
 \begin{align}
 \label{eq:Bussgang_decom}
 {\bphi}_{\rm ref} = \cQ(\breve{\bphi}) = \bA \breve{\bphi} + \bq,
 \end{align}
 where $\cQ(\cdot)$ denotes a nonlinear continuous-to-discrete mapper that maps each input entry to a feasible value in $\bbF$, $\breve{\bphi} = [\breve{{\phi}}_1, \ldots, \breve{{\phi}}_M]^{\sf T} \in \bbC^{M}$ denotes the continuous latent variable, $\bA \in \mathbb{C}^{M \times M}$ is the corresponding Bussgang gain matrix, and $\bq \in \mathbb{C}^{M}$ is the distortion noise satisfying $\bbE[\bq\breve{\bphi}^{\sf H}] = \mathbf{0}$.

 In addition, $\bbE[\bq] = {\bf 0}$ holds because $\bbE[\bphi_{\rm ref}] = \bbE[\breve{\bphi}] = {\bf 0}$.
 The Bussgang decomposition essentially models the nonlinear output as a linear transform of the input plus an error term. 
 To ensure that the linear model $\bA\breve{\bphi}$ linearly expresses ${\bphi}_{\rm ref}$ in the MMSE sense, $\bq = {\bphi}_{\rm ref} - \bA\breve{\bphi}$ must be uncorrelated to the input $\breve{\bphi}$.
 Thus, imposing $\bbE[\bq\breve{\bphi}^{\sf H}] = \mathbf{0}$ yields:
 \begin{align}
 \bbE[({\bphi}_{\rm ref} - \bA\breve{\bphi})\breve{\bphi}^{\sf H}] = \bC_{{\bphi}\breve{\bphi}} - \bA\bC_{\breve{\bphi}\breve{\bphi}} = \mathbf{0},
 \end{align}
 which implies $\bA = \bC_{{\bphi}\breve{\bphi}}\bC_{\breve{\bphi}\breve{\bphi}}^{-1}$. 




 The distortion noise covariance matrix is given by
 \begin{align}
 \bC_{\bq\bq} = \bbE[(\bphi_{\rm ref} - \bA\breve{\bphi})
 (\bphi_{\rm ref} - \bA\breve{\bphi})^{\sf H}].
 \end{align}
 Using the uncorrelation $\bbE[\bq\breve{\bphi}^{\sf H}] = \mathbf{0}$, 
 this simplifies to
 \begin{align}
 \label{eq:Bussgang_Cqq}
 \bC_{\bq\bq} = \bC_{\bphi\bphi} - \bA\bC_{\breve{\bphi}\breve{\bphi}}
 \bA^{\sf H}.
 \end{align}
