\subsection{Validation of the BuLL Metrics and Moments.}
\label{subsec:surrogate_validation}
\begin{figure}[t] 
 {\centerline{\resizebox{0.85\columnwidth}{!}{\includegraphics{Figures/Fig_Actual_vs_Surrogate.pdf}}}
 \vspace{-0.8em}
 \caption{The actual and surrogate metrics versus the number of RIS elements $M$: sum MSE (left axis) and sum SE (right axis).}
 \label{fig:surrogate_validation}} 
\end{figure}
To assess how well the proposed surrogates represent the actual
quantized-design metrics, we compare the sum MSE and sum SE as the
number of RIS elements $M$ varies for $b=1$ bit.
For channel estimation, the Jensen-derived surrogate in \eqref{eq:LB_Cov} is compared with the actual MSE evaluated using the corresponding quantized pattern.
For beamforming, the RIS latent vector is optimized with a fixed normalized
random precoder, which is used in both the surrogate in \eqref{eq:R_k_Cqq} and actual quantized-phase SE evaluations.
As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:surrogate_validation}, the Jensen surrogate
provides MSE values comparable to the actual metric, although the
latter exhibits more variation with $M$.
The SE surrogate closely follows the increasing trend of the actual
sum SE, with a remaining approximation gap.
These results illustrate the empirical correspondence between the
surrogates and the implemented metrics under the considered settings.
