\subsection{Performance of Precoding with Channel Estimation}
We now evaluate the beamforming algorithms under imperfect CSIT by incorporating the MSE resulting from the channel estimation algorithms.
To this end, we generate cascaded channel error vectors that preserve the second-order statistics of the LMMSE estimation error, which is the key quantity minimized by the MSE-based RIS pattern design in \eqref{eq:main_problem}.
Specifically, letting $\bR^{\sf e}_k = \bU^{\sf e}_k \boldsymbol{\Lambda}^{\sf e}_k \bU^{\sf e\,{\sf H}}_k$ denote the eigendecomposition of the error covariance matrix, we construct $\be_k = \bU^{\sf e}_k (\boldsymbol{\Lambda}_k^{\sf e})^{\frac{1}{2}}\,\bz_k$ with $\bz_k \sim \cC \cN({\bf 0}, \bI_{NM})$, so that $\bbE[\be_k\be_k^{\sf H}]=\bR^{\sf e}_k$, which yields the user-wise MSE, i.e.,  ${\rm MSE}_k = {\rm tr}(\bR^{\sf e}_k)$ for all $k$.
The error covariance matrices are computed via the LMMSE estimator with the RIS pattern designed by each algorithm.
Fig.~\ref{fig:PvsSE_imperfect} shows  the sum SE versus the maximum transmit power $P$ for $N=16$, $K=4$, and $M=30$.
To isolate the individual contributions of the BuLL method, we consider four combinations of channel estimation (BuLL-QT vs.\ QT) and beamforming (BuLL-GPI vs.\ GPI) schemes.
It is important to emphasize that each pair employs an \emph{identical} optimization algorithm; the \emph{sole} difference is whether the BuLL method is incorporated.
Therefore, every performance gap observed in Fig.~\ref{fig:PvsSE_imperfect} is entirely attributed to our BuLL method in discrete optimization.
With a fixed beamforming method, replacing QT with BuLL-QT yields a substantial estimation gain, while with fixed channel estimation, employing BuLL-GPI provides a distinct beamforming gain over GPI.
These two gains are complementary: the fully BuLL-based pipeline (BuLL-GPI with BuLL-QT) achieves a significant total gain over its counterpart without the proposed approach (GPI with QT).
These results confirm that our BuLL method serves as a plug-in enhancement that can be readily embedded into existing algorithms to unlock substantial performance improvements in various discrete optimization problems.
\section{Conclusion} \label{sec:conclusion}
In this paper, we proposed the BuLL-based discrete optimization approach and applied it to discrete RIS-aided multiuser communication systems.
The core idea is to introduce a continuous latent variable and characterize its statistical relationship with the discrete variable through the Bussgang decomposition under a Gaussian reference distribution. The resulting Bussgang moments are used to construct a distortion-aware continuous surrogate for the discrete optimization problem.
A distinct advantage of BuLL is its ability to account for discretization errors within the surrogate without relying on penalty terms.
Using this approach, we developed BuLL-QT for uplink RIS pattern design and BuLL-GPI for downlink RIS beamforming optimization.
Numerical results demonstrated competitive channel estimation
and SE performance for BuLL-QT and BuLL-GPI, respectively.
These findings confirm that incorporating our BuLL method offers a principled and efficient solution for discrete RIS designs.
