\section{Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:Bussgang_gain}} 
\label{appen:Buss_gain}
The Bussgang gain is defined as $\beta = \frac{\bbE[\mathcal{Q}(\breve{\phi}_m)\breve{\phi}_m^*]}{\bbE[|\breve{\phi}_m|^2]}$. Since $\breve{\phi}_m \sim \mathcal{CN}(0,1)$, the denominator is $\bbE[|\breve{\phi}_m|^2] = 1$.
Let the latent variable be expressed in polar coordinates as $\breve{\phi}_m = r e^{j\breve{\theta}}$, where the amplitude $r$ follows a Rayleigh distribution with $\bbE[r] = \frac{\sqrt{\pi}}{2}$, and the phase $\breve{\theta}$ is uniformly distributed in $[-\pi, \pi)$.
The quantizer $\mathcal{Q}(\cdot)$ maps the continuous phase $\breve{\theta}$ to the discrete phase ${\theta}$, such that the phase error $\delta = {\theta} - \breve{\theta}$ is uniformly distributed in $\left[-\frac{\pi}{L}, \frac{\pi}{L}\right)$.
The numerator can be computed as:
\begin{align}
 \nonumber
 \bbE[\mathcal{Q}(\breve{\phi}_m)\breve{\phi}_m^*] &= \bbE\left[ e^{j{\theta}} \cdot r e^{-j\breve{\theta}} \right] 


 &= \bbE[r] \cdot \bbE\left[ e^{-j(\breve{\theta} - {\theta})} \right] = \frac{\sqrt{\pi}}{2} \cdot \bbE\left[ e^{j\delta} \right].
\end{align}
The expectation of the phase error term is:
\begin{align}
 \bbE[e^{j\delta}] \!=\! \int_{-\frac{\pi}{L}}^{\frac{\pi}{L}} e^{j\delta} \frac{L}{2\pi} d\delta = \frac{L}{2\pi} \left[ \frac{e^{j\delta}}{j} \right]_{-\pi/L}^{\pi/L} 
 \!= \frac{L}{\pi} \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{L}\right)\!.
\end{align}
Multiplying the amplitude and phase expectations yields the generalized gain $\beta$ in \eqref{eq:Bussgang_gain}. 
This completes the proof.
\hfill $\blacksquare$
\section{Proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:NEP_RIS}}
\label{appen:GPI}
According to the stationary condition, the stationary points need to satisfy $\partial \cL(\bw)/\partial \bw^{\sf H} = 0$.
Thus, we take the partial derivative of $\cL(\bw)$ with respect to $\bw$ and set it to be zero. 
By using the derivative of the Rayleigh quotient form as
\begin{align}
 \label{eq:derivative_matrix}
 \frac{\partial \left( \frac{{\bw}^{\sf H} {\bJ}_k \bw}{{\bw}^{\sf H} {\bK}_k \bw}\right)}{\partial \bw^{\sf H}} = \left( \frac{\bw^{\sf H} {\bJ}_k \bw}{\bw^{\sf H} {\bK}_k \bw} \right) \left[\frac{{\bJ}_k \bw }{{\bw}^{\sf H} {\bJ}_k \bw} - \frac{{\bK}_k \bw }{{\bw}^{\sf H} {\bK}_k \bw} \right],
\end{align}
we can calculate the partial derivative of $\cL(\bw)$ in \eqref{eq:lamb_obj} as
\begin{align}
 \label{eq:partial_L1}
 \frac{\partial \cL (\bw)}{\partial \bw^{\sf H}} = \sum_{k=1}^K \frac{1}{\ln 2} \left( \frac{{\bJ}_k \bw }{{\bw}^{\sf H} {\bJ}_k \bw} - \frac{{\bK}_k \bw }{{\bw}^{\sf H} {\bK}_k \bw} \right).
\end{align}
Using \eqref{eq:partial_L1}, the stationary condition holds if 
\begin{align}
 \label{eq:kkt_cond_rearrange}
 \sum_{k=1}^{K}  \left(\frac{\bJ_k}{ {\bw}^{\sf H} \bJ_k \bw} \right)\bw
 =\sum_{k=1}^{K} \left(\frac{\bK_k}{ {\bw}^{\sf H} \bK_k \bw} \right)\bw.
\end{align}
The stationary condition can be regarded as the generalized eigenvalue problem with its corresponding matrices $\bar{\bJ}(\bw)$ and $\bar{\bK}(\bw)$ defined in \eqref{eq:CD_KKT}: $\bar{\bJ}(\bw) \bw = \lambda(\bw) \bar{\bK} (\bw) \bw.$

Here, $\bar{\bK}(\bw)$ is positive definite for $\sigma^2/P>0$ and positive $\lambda_{\sf 2}(\bw)$.
\hfill $\blacksquare$
