\begin{abstract}
Rotatable semi-passive intelligent reflecting surfaces (SIRSs) provide an additional spatial degree of freedom (DoF) by proactively adjusting the orientation, while dedicated sensors directly receive target echoes. To exploit this DoF for integrated sensing and communication (ISAC), we investigate the joint optimization of SIRS rotation, base station (BS) transmit beamforming, and SIRS phase-shifts to minimize the Cramér–Rao bound (CRB) for target's spatial-frequency estimation subject to per-user communication signal-to-interference-plus-noise ratio (SINR) constraints. We first establish an orientation-dependent system model that characterizes the effective aperture gains, based on which the CRB is derived. To gain useful insights, closed-form expressions of the beamforming and rotation designs are derived in the sensing-only and communication-only cases. For the co-located user and target case, we further characterize the condition under which the sensing-oriented rotation design also satisfies the communication requirement. In the single-user single-target case with ideal unit aperture gains, we analyze the channel correlation and its impact on ISAC performance. For the general multi-user case, an effective method based on the genetic algorithm is developed. Numerical results demonstrate the superiorities of the rotatable SIRS over the fixed-orientation SIRS and show that jointly optimizing the SIRS orientation and beamforming can improve the effective aperture gains and the correlation between communication and sensing channels.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Semi-passive intelligent reflecting surface (SIRS), rotation optimization, integrated sensing and communication (ISAC), alternating optimization.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
Integrated sensing and communication (ISAC) has been recognized as a key usage scenario for sixth-generation (6G) wireless networks \cite{luo2026ISACSurvey}. By sharing spectrum, hardware, and transmit waveforms, ISAC supports communication and sensing on an integrated platform, enabling applications such as autonomous driving, intelligent transportation, and industrial automation \cite{huangUAV}. However, communication and sensing generally impose distinct requirements on transmitted signals. Specifically, communication requires reliable signal reception at users and the suppression of inter-user interference, whereas sensing requires sufficient target illumination power \cite{niu2025InterferenceSurvey}. The resulting trade-off is governed by the correlation between communication and sensing channels, which motivates the use of additional spatial degrees of freedom (DoFs) \cite{bian2025Correlation,hua2024MIMOTradeoff,ren2024MultitargetTradeoff,feng2026ISAC}.
The performance of ISAC systems is critically dependent on the wireless propagation environment. In the absence of direct line-of-sight (LoS) links, both the received communication signal power and target illumination may be severely degraded, making it difficult to ensure reliable communication and ubiquitous sensing through base station (BS) transceiver design alone. To address these challenges, intelligent reflecting surface (IRS) has emerged as a cost-effective technology for reconfiguring wireless propagation environments by adjusting their reflection coefficients \cite{tishchenko2025HybridSurvey,rihan2024PassiveActive,ma2024RISISAC,ji_RDARS_model_driven}. By creating additional reflected paths and providing passive beamforming gains, IRSs can enhance the received communication power and target echo signals. 
Motivated by these benefits, IRS-aided ISAC has been investigated in prior studies. Depending on their hardware configurations, IRS architectures for ISAC can be categorized into passive IRS, active IRS, and semi-passive IRS (SIRS). For passive IRS-assisted ISAC with sensing echo received at the BS, the sensing signal propagates along the BS-IRS-target-IRS-BS path, thereby suffering severe cascaded propagation loss. A multi-hop IRS-enabled ISAC system was studied in \cite{luo2026MultihopPositioning}, where transmit beamforming and IRS phase-shifts were jointly designed to enhance target positioning accuracy while satisfying user communication constraints. For single-IRS and dual-IRS architectures, \cite{sankar2024RISBeamforming} investigated joint beamforming and reflection design for radar beampattern matching and worst-case target illumination power maximization, respectively, subject to user signal-to-interference-plus-noise ratio (SINR) constraints. Furthermore, in \cite{liu2024SNRCRB}, the BS beamforming and IRS reflection were jointly optimized to maximize the communication sum-rate under sensing signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) or Cramér–Rao bound (CRB) constraints. To compensate for path loss, active IRS is equipped with active reflecting elements to amplify incident signals \cite{peng2026ActiveULDL}. Thanks to its reflection amplification capability, it has been exploited for local sensing \cite{han2025MultifunctionalRIS}, secure transmission \cite{hua2024SecureIRSISAC}, and ISAC \cite{zhu2024ActiveCRB,yigit2025HybridSTARISAC}. However,  it may also amplify undesired noise and interference. Another type of active IRS, also known as a self-sensing IRS, integrates passive reflecting elements with active sensors and controllers to support signal transmission, reception, and processing. A self-sensing IRS-aided mmWave ISAC system was investigated in \cite{selfSensingIRS}, where a two-phase transmission scheme was developed for joint location sensing and channel estimation, and IRS beamforming was optimized for CRB minimization. Despite these benefits, both types of active IRSs introduce additional power consumption and hardware complexity due to the active components.
Considering both the path loss and hardware cost, a SIRS is equipped with dedicated sensors that receive and process the target echo signal for parameter estimation via the BS-IRS-target-IRS link. In the literature, SIRS-enabled sensing has been studied through CRB analysis and joint beamforming design \cite{peng2024SemiPassive,chen2026ISAC}, joint time-of-arrival and direction-of-arrival estimation \cite{hua2024SemiPassiveLocalization}, as well as integrated user localization and communication \cite{hu2023LocationISAC}. In addition, for SIRS-assisted ISAC, energy efficiency maximization with communication and sensing SNR constraints was studied in \cite{rojith2026SemiPassiveEE}. The authors of \cite{dong2026AngleVelocity} considered joint angle and radial velocity estimation. Furthermore, CRB optimization with and without prior angle information were investigated in \cite{dong2025SemiPassiveNearField}. In these studies, beamforming and reflection coefficients are optimized with pre-defined IRS orientations. However, a small effective aperture caused by undesired incidence or reflection angles cannot be fully compensated for by phase alignment alone. This limitation motivates exploiting the IRS orientation as an additional spatial DoF.
To address the deployment issue and motivated by the rotatable antenna \cite{qu2025RotatableCooperativeISAC,wang2026RotatableHybridISAC,zhang2026RotatableNearField,sun2025RotatableMovableISAC,luRA}, joint IRS position and rotation optimization has been studied under angle-dependent channel models \cite{rotation_IRS,feng2025SixDIRS,peng2026CooperativeRotatableIRS,liu2025RotatableIRS,wang2025rotate}. Specifically, an IRS at a fixed location can adjust its incidence and reflection geometry through mechanical rotation. In \cite{peng2026CooperativeRotatableIRS}, cooperative IRS beamforming and orientation were jointly designed, with closed-form expression of solutions derived under line-of-sight (LoS) channels. Furthermore, rotatable IRSs have been experimentally evaluated for max-min area coverage in \cite{liu2025RotatableIRS}. The work \cite{wang2025rotate} investigated an unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV)-mounted passive IRS-enabled ISAC system, where the IRS location, orientation, and reflection coefficients were optimized jointly with the BS transmit beamforming to maximize the sensing SNR subject to a communication SNR requirement. These results highlight the importance of jointly designing IRS beamforming and orientation to improve the effective reception and reflection gains, and demonstrate the benefits of geometric reconfiguration beyond conventional phase-shift design. Nevertheless, the work \cite{wang2025rotate} considered a single-user ISAC system with the objective of sensing SNR, where the target echo signals are received at the BS through a fixed antenna array. In contrast, a SIRS directly collects the target echo signal through its sensors, whose orientation changes with the reflecting panel. As such, SIRS rotation affects not only target illumination but also the receive array response, introducing an additional design dimension for target parameter estimation. Moreover, in a multi-user ISAC system, the SIRS needs to assist the BS to serve users located in different directions while mitigating inter-user interference.
Regarding the deployment of SIRS in an ISAC system, several fundamental issues remain unaddressed. First, how to design a rotation scheme to flexibly balance between communication and sensing performance? SIRS rotation changes the effective aperture gains of the BS-IRS-user and BS-IRS-target links. An orientation that improves target performance may reduce the user signal strength, requiring the BS and SIRS beamforming to be adjusted to satisfy the communication requirement. Conversely, adjusting the SIRS rotation to strengthen the communication link may reduce the target illumination gain and degrade sensing performance. Second, how to characterize the impact of channel alignment on CRB for target's spatial-frequency estimation? Note that the CRB depends not only on the received echo power but also on the derivative of the target response with respect to (w.r.t.) the estimated parameter. SIRS rotation changes the array responses and the correlation between the effective communication and sensing channels. Favorable channel alignment allows a common reflection configuration to support both functions, consistent with the subspace rotation principle in \cite{S&C}. With multiple users, rotation additionally changes the users’ directions relative to the surface and their effective channel correlations. An orientation that improves the alignment between one user and the target may also increase inter-user channel correlation. To this end, it is challenging to jointly design the BS beamforming, SIRS orientation, and passive beamforming to minimize the CRB while satisfying the per-user communication constraints.
Motivated by the above considerations, we investigate a rotatable SIRS-assisted ISAC system, where a multi-antenna BS serves multiple single-antenna users and a point target in the non-line-of-sight (NLoS) region of the BS. In the considered system, the reflecting elements establish reflected links from the BS to the users and the target, while the sensors at the SIRS directly receive and process target echoes. Specifically, we consider a target-tracking scenario with prior target information, where the angle of the point target w.r.t. the SIRS is refined. By jointly adjusting the orientation of the reflecting panel and that of the sensors, SIRS rotation changes both the effective aperture gains and the array responses, thereby providing additional spatial DoFs for communication and sensing. To exploit these benefits, we aim to minimize the CRB for the target's spatial-frequency estimation by jointly optimizing the BS beamforming, SIRS beamforming, and rotation angles, subject to user SINR, transmit power, and reflection constraints. The rotation angles are nonlinearly coupled with the BS and SIRS beamformers, and the feasible orientations are restricted by the half-space reflection requirement, which requires joint beamforming and rotation designs. The main contributions are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
 \item First, we propose angle-dependent communication and sensing models and derive the sensing CRB. For the single-path sensing-only and communication-only cases, we derive closed-form expressions for the beamforming and rotation designs, and show that two rotation angles are sufficient to achieve the performance of three-dimensional orientation under the considered setup. For a co-located user and target, we characterize the feasibility condition under which the sensing-optimal orientation also satisfies the user requirement.
 \item Second, we analyze the single-user case with unit effective aperture gains and characterize the impact of channel alignment on the performance of the ISAC system. We provide an SIRS orientation and beamforming design scheme to support communication and sensing, thereby distinguishing the spatial correlation benefit from the effective aperture gain. This analysis explains how rotation can balance the trade-off between the two functions through a proper reflection geometry. Furthermore, we develop an effective optimization framework for the multi-user case. A genetic algorithm is proposed for rotation optimization, while the BS and SIRS beamforming are optimized through semidefinite relaxation and Schur’s complement. 
 \item Finally, comprehensive numerical results are conducted to verify our analytical findings and demonstrate the effectiveness of the rotatable SIRS over the fixed-orientation SIRS. Moreover, the proposed rotation designs can achieve CRB performance close to that of exhaustive search and outperforms the communication-orientation scheme. The results further characterize the impact of transmit power, communication threshold, as well as the number of BS antennas, reflecting elements, and sensors on the ISAC performance.
\end{itemize}
The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section II presents the system model and the sensing CRB. Section III formulates the joint optimization problem, presents analytical designs for special cases, and develops effective methods for the single-user and multi-user scenarios. Section IV provides numerical results to evaluate the proposed designs and illustrate the trade-off between communication and sensing. Section V concludes the paper.
