As shown in Fig. \ref{fig:systemmodel}, we consider a rotatable SIRS-aided ISAC system, where the SIRS is deployed to assist a multi-antenna BS to sense a point target and serve multiple single-antenna users. We focus on a challenging scenario where the direct BS-target and BS-user links are blocked by obstacles, and thus communication and target illumination rely on SIRS reflection. The SIRS and the BS are equipped with $N$ passive reflecting elements and $L$ active sensors, as well as $M$ transmit antennas, respectively. Let the set of the reflecting elements and users are denoted by $\mathcal{N} = \{1,\cdots, N\}$ and $\mathcal{K} = \{1,\cdots, K\}$, respectively, where the number of users is represented as $K$. In a global Cartesian coordinate system (CCS), the coordinates of the BS, SIRS, target, and user $k \in \mathcal{K}$ are given by ${{\mathbf{p}}_{\mathrm{B}}} = {\left[ {0,0,0} \right]^T}$, ${{\mathbf{p}}_{\mathrm{I}}} = {\left[ {{x_{\mathrm{I}}},{y_{\mathrm{I}}},{z_{\mathrm{I}}}} \right]^T}$, ${{\mathbf{p}}_{\mathrm{T}}} = {\left[ {{x_{\mathrm{T}}},{y_{\mathrm{T}}},{z_{\mathrm{T}}}} \right]^T}$, and ${{\mathbf{p}}_k} = {\left[ {{x_k},{y_k},{z_k}} \right]^T}$, respectively.


The 3D orientation of the SIRS can be represented by $\bm{\varphi} = \left[\varphi_x, \varphi_y, \varphi_z\right]^T$, where $\varphi_x \in \left[-\pi/2,\pi/2\right]$, $\varphi_y \in \left[-\pi/2,\pi/2\right]$, $\varphi_z \in \left[-\pi/2,\pi/2\right]$ are Euler angles denoting the SIRS’s degree of orientation around $x'$-, $y'$-, and $z'$-axis, respectively. The relationship between the global CCS and the local CCS is characterized by the orientation matrix expressed in \eqref{gCCS}.
