To further discuss the impact of the spatial correlation on the ISAC performance, we compare the proposed scheme based on Proposition \ref{proposition:subspace} and the fixed scheme by plotting the CRB versus the communication SNR threshold with $F_{\mathrm{T}}(\bm{\varphi}) \approx 1$ and $F_{\mathrm{U}}(\bm{\varphi}) \approx 1$ in Fig. \ref{fig:subspace}. First, one can observe that the CRB of the fixed scheme is close to that of the proposed scheme when the SNR threshold is low. This is because the SNR constraints are readily satisfied and thus most of the available spatial DoFs and transmit power can be allocated to sensing. However, the performance gap becomes larger as $\Gamma _\mathrm{U}$ increases, since the correlation between the effective sensing and communication channels is limited by the fixed orientation. Thus, the SIRS phase-shifts and rotation must be adjusted to improve the user signal strength at the expense of the beamforming gain towards the target, resulting in a higher CRB. In contrast, the CRB of the proposed scheme remains almost constant. Based on \eqref{lemma}, the rotation angles are optimized to increase $\rho$, thereby improving the effective sensing and communication channel gains through channel subspace rotation. The results demonstrate that the proposed design enlarges the region of the achievable  sensing and communication performance and facilitates high-quality sensing and communication services.
