\subsection{Multi-User Case}
Finally, we study the case where the BS serves one target and two users in the ISAC system. Fig. \ref{fig:STMU_M} compares the CRB versus the number of BS antennas under the following SIRS rotation schemes: 1) Communication-oriented design: the SIRS rotation angles are set to maximize the minimum user SINR; 2) GA:  the optimized SIRS rotation angles are obtained via the genetic algorithm; 3) ES: the optimized SIRS rotation angles are obtained via the two-dimensional search. First, it is observed that the CRB of all the schemes monotonically decreases with the number of BS antennas due to the increased BS transmit beamforming gain. Second, we observe that the proposed GA scheme achieves a CRB close to that of the ES scheme in the whole range of $M$. Third, the CRB performance gap between the proposed scheme and the communication-oriented design scheme is pronounced when $M$ is relatively small. As $M$ increases, the transmit beamforming gain becomes dominant in achieving a balance between communication and sensing. Nevertheless, the gap remains noticeable at larger $M$. This is because the communication-oriented rotation scheme is aim to improve the communication performance to satisfy the SINR constraint, however, it does not account for the sensing information in the CRB. In contrast, the BS beamforming, the SIRS beamforming, and the SIRS rotation are jointly optimized under the GA and ES schemes to improve the ISAC performance. The results demonstrate the effectiveness of the GA scheme and highlight the importance of the SIRS rotation design and beamforming.
