To illustrate the fundamental trade-off between communication and sensing performance, we plot the CRB versus the communication SINR threshold in Fig. \ref{fig:STMU_C}. One can observe that the CRB increases with $\Gamma_k$. In particular, it increases more rapidly when the SINR threshold exceeds 10 dB. This is because the feasible region for the beamforming and rotation designs becomes more limited as the per-user communication SINR constraints become more stringent. In this case, more resources must be allocated to the communication signals to ensure sufficient received signal strength and suppress inter-user interference. Moreover, it is observed that increasing the number of scatterers leads to a higher CRB. Based on \eqref{path}, increasing $C$ reduces the weight of the LoS component, while the additional scattering paths experience cascaded propagation losses. However, the resulting path loss cannot be effectively compensated for by the additional spatial DoFs, especially when the SINR threshold is sufficiently large.
