In the following, given the channel conditions, e.g., $C=5$, we investigate how to further improve the ISAC performance by exploiting the available system resources, including the numbers of SIRS reflecting elements and SIRS sensors. In Fig. \ref{fig:STMU_L}, we plot the CRB versus the number of sensors under different numbers of reflecting elements. We observe that the CRB monotonically decreases with $L$. This is because deploying more sensors at the SIRS provides higher spatial diversity gains, thereby increasing the information available for target parameter estimation. Different from the sensing-only case with ideal unit aperture gain, where the CRB is inversely proportional to $L^3-L$ and $N^2$ as shown in \eqref{sensing_crb}, increasing the number of reflecting elements is more beneficial for the considered system with multiple users. Specifically, the CRB decreases from -89.47 dB to -92.52 dB when $L$ increases from 4 to 8 with $N=128$, whereas the CRB decreases from -77.02 dB to -93.37 dB when $N$ increases from 64 to 128 with $L=10$. The reasons are as follows. In this case, the sensing performance depends not only on the improvement in target echo signal strength provided by the additional sensors but also on the communication constraints and the resulting joint beamforming and rotation designs. Moreover, additional reflecting elements provide a higher passive beamforming gain for both sensing and communication. With the joint optimization of beamforming and rotation, the spatial resources can be fully exploited to improve the sensing performance while satisfying the per-user communication SINR constraints. The results highlight the importance of determining the numbers of reflecting elements and sensors according to the specific sensing and communication constraints.
