\section{Conclusion}
This paper has studied a rotatable SIRS-assisted ISAC system under an orientation-dependent model. Based on the proposed model, the sensing CRB was derived to characterize the effects of SIRS rotation and joint beamforming on target estimation. Closed-form expressions of the rotation angles were obtained for the sensing-only and communication-only cases, where two-dimensional orientation were sufficient to achieve same performance as three-dimensional orientation. For co-located user and target case, the sensing-oriented design satisfied the communication requirement. For the single-user single-target case with ideal unit aperture gains, the rotation angles were designed to improve the correlation between the effective communication and sensing channels. An effective method was developed to address the CRB minimization problem in the multi-user case. Numerical results demonstrated the effectiveness of the rotatable SIRS over the fixed-orientation SIRS in terms of reducing the requirement of transmit power and number of reflecting elements and improved the ISAC performance due to subspace rotation gain, and showed that it can improve the ISAC performance due to subspace rotation gain. Moreover, in the multi-user case, increasing the number of reflecting elements yields a larger CRB reduction than increasing the number of sensors. These findings provide useful guidelines for the joint design of surface orientation and beamforming in SIRS-aided ISAC systems.

