\begin{abstract}
Medical Vision-Language Pre-training (VLP) leverages the semantic richness of radiology reports to learn 3D representations, yet current architectures remain fundamentally anatomy-agnostic. While clinical diagnostics rely on organ-specific context, standard encoders apply a uniform, dense parameterization to all volumetric patches, and existing Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) routers gate on isolated local features without explicit access to the organ composition of the scan or crop.
We propose Anatomy-Activated Mixture-of-Experts (A$^{2}$MoE), a framework that incorporates anatomical inductive biases within the model's computational path.  A$^{2}$MoE introduces two key components: (1) a Histogram Representation Router (HRR) that conditions expert selection on global visual context and a predicted anatomical
composition histogram, encouraging anatomy-dependent expert weighting; and (2) an Organ-Query Multi-Scale Projector (OQMSP) that utilizes learnable queries to extract organ-level embeddings for fine-grained alignment without requiring manual annotations at inference. By routing at the patch level rather than the token level, A$^{2}$MoE reduces routing complexity by orders of magnitude while satisfying load balancing by construction. Pre-trained on over 60,000 multi-phase CT scans, A$^{2}$MoE achieves state-of-the-art performance across segmentation, classification, and detection benchmarks. Furthermore, we demonstrate that our anatomically grounded inductive biases support robust generalization to MRI and ultrasound, establishing a scalable foundation for medical representation learning. The code is available at \href{https://github.com/gmum/A2MoE}{github.com/gmum/A2MoE}.
\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
Vision-Language Pre-training (VLP) has redefined multimodal representation learning by aligning visual features with semantic language cues in a shared embedding space~\cite{bao2022vlmo,yang2022vision,chen2023vlp,dou2022coarse,li2022blip,alayrac2022flamingo,moor2023med,li2023blip,su2024boost,zhang2025medunifier,chu2026medtri,liang2025medfilip}. 
In the medical domain, Medical Vision-Language Models (MedVLMs)~\cite{shuilarge,pmlr-v306-shi26b,cao2025boosting,hamamci2025better,zhao2026towards,claessens2025scaling,radford2021learning,zhang2022contrastive,moon2022multi,yan2025local,lei2026versatile,li2023lvit} leverage radiology reports to supervise 3D visual encoders, reducing dependence on scarce voxel-level annotations. 
Moreover, 3D medical images have an anatomical hierarchy that current encoders rarely exploit: local image patterns arise within organs, organs appear within broader scan-level anatomical compositions, and reports describe findings at corresponding organ-specific and global levels. 
Current MedVLM backbones largely ignore this structure, applying the same dense parameters to all volumetric patches regardless of their anatomical context. 
This is suboptimal because visually similar patterns can carry different clinical meanings in different organs or anatomical neighborhoods. 
We argue that medical VLP should therefore move beyond post-hoc image--text alignment and make anatomical context part of the model's computation path during feature extraction.
Recent advances in MedVLMs have recognized the importance of anatomical grounding, but have primarily addressed it through refined \textit{alignment objectives}. For instance, fVLM~\cite{shuilarge} matches CT regions to specific report snippets, while ViSD-Boost~\cite{cao2025boosting} enhances semantic density via organ-aware contrastive loss. However, these methods focus primarily on \textit{what} is aligned to text, but they do not fundamentally change \textit{how} visual features are computed inside the encoder. Current 3D backbones~\cite{xin2025med3dvlm, blankemeier2026merlin, lee2026read} treat every voxel with the same dense parameterization, regardless of whether a feature arises in the liver, kidney, spleen, bowel, or background. Explicit anatomical conditioning offers a complementary direction; in clinical practice, identical visual patterns often hold divergent meanings depending on their anatomical context. A "dark spot" (hypodensity), for example, carries distinct diagnostic weight in the liver versus the spleen or kidney, motivating an explicit anatomical input alongside learned visual features.
