 \caption{\textbf{Comparison of MoE routing strategies.} (a) MoE routing: The input feature map $x$ is pooled to form a global visual context, which is passed through an MLP router to produce logits over $K$ experts. Softmax-normalized weights are then used to compute a convex combination of expert outputs $E_k(x)$. Routing depends solely on visual features. (b) In addition to visual context, a foreground-normalized organ-composition histogram $\widetilde{\mathbf h}$ is embedded and fused with visual features to jointly condition the router. Each foreground entry represents the corresponding organ's share of the total foreground voxel mass in the input crop. A learnable class-to-expert affinity matrix $\mathbf A^{(s)}$ introduces a structural prior via a log-probability term $\log(\widetilde{\mathbf h}\widetilde{\mathbf A}^{(s)}+\epsilon)$, biasing expert selection toward anatomically consistent assignments. The resulting logits are softmax-normalized to obtain routing weights, and the final output is computed as a weighted sum of expert responses.}
 \label{fig:aamoe_routing}
\end{figure}
Mixture-of-Experts (MoE)~\cite{jacobs1991adaptive,shazeer2017,fedus2022switch} provides a principled mechanism for conditional computation, yet its standard formulation in computer vision remains "context-blind." Current MoE routers typically dispatch tokens based on isolated local patch embeddings, ignoring the global anatomical topology of the 3D volume. This leads to a suboptimal "semantic collision": patches with high textural similarity but distinct anatomical identities, such as a liver--kidney boundary versus a liver--stomach interface, are frequently mapped to the same expert parameters. Such interference dilutes the model’s capacity to learn specialized, organ-specific feature transforms. In addition to MoEs, existing cross-modal projection heads either rely on global pooling, which collapses local anatomical nuances, or depend on ground-truth masks to isolate organ-level evidence, a dependency that renders them impractical for real-world inference where such masks can be unavailable.
Motivated by these limitations in both expert routing and cross-modal projection, we propose \textbf{A}natomy-\textbf{A}ctivated \textbf{M}ixture-\textbf{o}f-\textbf{E}xperts (\textbf{A$^{2}$MoE}), a volumetric medical VLP framework that incorporates anatomical conditioning into the encoder and organ-indexed aggregation into the projector. Our central hypothesis is that \textit{explicitly conditioning model capacity on global anatomical composition allows the network to learn more discriminative, organ-specific representations than dense or context-blind backbones. By grounding the computation path in structural priors, the model can effectively decouple the complex, overlapping signals present in 3D medical scans}. 
To realize this, we introduce two core components. First, our Histogram Representation Router (HRR) jointly embeds global visual context with an anatomical composition histogram $\mathbf{h}$ and a learnable class-to-expert affinity prior (see Figure \ref{fig:aamoe_routing}). This conditions expert weighting on crop-level anatomical
context. Second, we address the challenge of mask-free inference with an Organ-Query Multi-Scale Projector (OQMSP). Instead of requiring explicit segmentation, OQMSP utilizes a set of learnable organ queries that cross-attend to MoE-refined features across multiple scales. This allows the model to "probe" for organ-specific evidence and extract per-organ embeddings based on learned attention weights, providing a scalable mechanism for fine-grained image–text alignment without masks at
inference. While pre-trained on CT, the structural inductive biases of A$^{2}$MoE are modality-agnostic, enabling seamless transfer to MRI and ultrasound where anatomical consistency remains a primary diagnostic pillar. Our main contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
 \item \textbf{Anatomy-Activated MoE Routing.} We introduce the HRR, a novel routing mechanism that uses an anatomical composition histogram to guide conditional computation, enabling the model to allocate specialized parameters to specific anatomical structures.
 \item \textbf{Mask-Free Organ-Level Alignment.} We propose OQMSP, a projector that extracts per-organ visual embeddings through learned queries, allowing for fine-grained VLP alignment without the need for segmentation masks during inference.
 \item \textbf{Empirical Validation of Conditional VLP.} We show that anatomy-conditioned routing and organ-level alignment improve 3D medical representation learning and support robust transfer from CT pre-training to MRI and ultrasound.
\end{enumerate}
We demonstrate that A$^{2}$MoE serves as a robust foundation for multimodal representation learning. 
By pre-training on over 60,000 CT-report pairs, we achieve state-of-the-art performance across segmentation, classification, and detection tasks. Notably, A$^{2}$MoE exhibits scalability across diverse structural granularities, from fine-grained anomalies to large-scale anatomical systems, and maintains high generalization performance on out-of-distribution benchmarks. Ultimately, A$^{2}$MoE provides a scalable mechanism for aligning unstructured semantic priors with dense 3D representations, facilitating effective cross-modal knowledge transfer.
\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}
\noindent
\textbf{Mixture-of-Experts.} MoE enables conditional computation by routing inputs to specialized sub-networks, scaling model capacity without proportionally increasing computational cost~\cite{shazeer2017,fedus2022switch,riquelme2021scaling,Kada_2026_CVPR}. In vision, V-MoE~\cite{riquelme2021scaling} and Soft
MoE~\cite{puigcerversparse} route image tokens to experts based on
local patch embeddings, while LIMoE~\cite{mustafa2022multimodal}
extends sparse MoE to contrastive language-image pre-training.
In medical imaging, HoME~\cite{plotka2026mamba} combines Mamba
state-space blocks with soft expert routing for linear-time 3D
segmentation, and Swin~SMT~\cite{plotka2024swin} integrates Soft~MoE
into Swin~UNETR for whole-body CT segmentation.
PAMoE~\cite{wu2025learning} routes tissue patches to specialized experts for gigapixel whole-slide image analysis, adapting capacity to heterogeneous histological regions, while
ERMoE~\cite{cheng2026ermoe} reparameterizes experts in a learned
eigenbasis and shows that the resulting routing yields anatomically
interpretable specializations in 3D brain MRI. In the multimodal domain, MoHAVE~\cite{kim2025mohave} and
MoME~\cite{cappellazzo2025mome} apply hierarchical and Matryoshka-style MoE to audio-visual speech recognition, while
M3-JEPA~\cite{lei2025mjepa} uses a multi-gate MoE predictor for
general multimodal alignment. While current MoE models perform routing based on low-level feature heuristics, they remain agnostic to the global anatomical landscape. Our work addresses this fundamental gap: the Histogram Representation Router is the first to condition expert selection on a high-level, structured representation of anatomical distribution, ensuring that routing is biologically informed rather than purely data-driven.
\noindent
\textbf{Anatomical conditioning and organ-level alignment.} Anatomical priors have been incorporated into both expert routing
and medical vision--language representation learning.
OAMoE~\cite{mi2026organ} organizes segmentation experts into
anatomically guided pools with adaptive expert selection. HAR-MoE~\cite{mu2026har} combines
task-conditioned expert screening with organ-conditioned refinement
and learnable affinity priors for unified ultrasound segmentation
and classification, sharing image-level routing decisions across
spatial tokens. For cross-modal learning, ASG~\cite{li2024anatomical} aligns
anatomical regions with corresponding report sentences, while
Anatomy-VLM~\cite{gu2026anatomy} combines anatomical localization,
structured clinical knowledge, and multi-scale alignment for
chest radiograph interpretation.
In volumetric CT, fVLM~\cite{shuilarge} performs anatomy-level
contrastive pre-training with disease-aware pairing;
ViSD-Boost~\cite{cao2025boosting} strengthens anatomical
representations through normal--abnormal discrimination and
anatomy normality modeling; and RADAR~\cite{zhang2026expert}
scales anatomy-wise image--text learning for generalist abdominal
CT diagnosis.
Building on these directions, A$^{2}$MoE couples
crop-composition-conditioned encoder routing with multi-scale
organ-level alignment: an anatomy histogram and learned
class-to-expert affinity prior condition expert mixtures, while
shared organ queries aggregate features across encoder stages
without masks at inference.
\noindent
\textbf{Medical Vision--Language Pre-training.}
Early methods such as GLoRIA~\cite{huang2021gloria} and
LoVT~\cite{muller2022joint} combine global image--report alignment
with local cross-modal objectives to learn transferable
representations from chest radiographs.
Subsequent work extends language-supervised representation
learning to volumetric imaging through complementary strategies.
Merlin~\cite{blankemeier2026merlin} combines CT volumes,
radiology reports, and structured electronic health records
to support diverse downstream tasks.
HLIP~\cite{zhao2026towards} enables pre-training on uncurated
clinical studies through hierarchical attention across slices,
scans, and studies, while SPECTRE~\cite{claessens2025scaling}
combines self-supervised and cross-modal pre-training for
volumetric CT transformers.
MG-3D~\cite{ni2026mg} incorporates multi-grained report knowledge
through intra-patient cross-modal learning and inter-patient
semantic alignment, while ExGra-Med~\cite{mh2026exgra}
explores graph-based cross-modal alignment for semantic grounding.
Beyond representation alignment,
R-Super~\cite{bassi2025learning,bassi2025scaling} converts
radiology reports into voxel-wise supervision for tumor
segmentation, extending report-derived knowledge to dense prediction.
Addressing volumetric representation efficiency,
BTB3D~\cite{hamamci2025better} learns compact, frequency-aware
3D tokens for report generation and text-conditioned CT synthesis.
Complementing these methodological advances,
CARES~\cite{xia2024cares} benchmarks the trustworthiness of
medical vision--language models.
A$^{2}$MoE builds on this progress by coupling
anatomy-conditioned expert mixtures within the visual encoder
with shared organ queries for multi-scale image--text alignment.
\section{Preliminaries}
\paragraph{Selective State-Space Models (Mamba).} Our architecture builds on the Mamba layer~\cite{mamba}, a structured state-space model designed for long-sequence modeling with linear computational complexity. In contrast to Transformer architectures, whose attention mechanism incurs a quadratic cost in sequence length, Mamba achieves linear-time processing via a discrete-time recurrence derived from continuous-time state-space dynamics. Given a feature sequence \(z = \{z_t\}_{t=1}^N \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times N \times d}\), the hidden state \(h_t\) and output \(y_t\) evolve according to:
\begin{equation}
h_t = A(z_t)\, h_{t-1} + B(z_t)\, z_t, 
\qquad 
y_t = C(z_t)\, h_t,
\end{equation}
where \(A(\cdot), B(\cdot), C(\cdot)\) denote learnable, input-dependent linear maps. This parameterization enables the model to capture both short- and long-range dependencies with computational cost that scales
linearly with sequence length for fixed feature and state dimensions \(\mathcal{O}(Nd)\). Within our network, the Mamba layer acts as the backbone for volumetric sequence modeling, providing an efficient mechanism for capturing spatial dependencies in 3D imaging data.
\paragraph{Dense Mixtures-of-Experts with Soft Gating.} A Mixture-of-Experts (MoE)~\cite{jacobs1991adaptive} processes inputs
using a bank of $E$ independent experts. Unlike sparse MoE
\cite{riquelme2021scaling,mustafa2022multimodal}, which uses
top-$k$ gating~\cite{zhou2022mixture,lapsum} to activate a subset
of experts, dense soft gating computes a differentiable weighted
combination of all $E$ expert outputs:
\begin{equation}
y_{b,n} = \sum_{e=1}^{E} p_{b,n,e}\,
\mathrm{FFN}_e(x_{b,n}),
\end{equation}
where $\mathrm{FFN}_e$ denotes the $e$-th expert and
$p_{b,n,e}\geq 0$, with $\sum_e p_{b,n,e}=1$, denotes its
mixture weight. This dense output mixture differs from the
slot-based token dispatch used in Soft MoE~\cite{puigcerversparse}.
Token-level gating requires routing-logit evaluations proportional
to the number of tokens. Our anatomy-activated MoE instead predicts
crop-specific mixture weights that are shared across spatial
positions within each encoder stage and conditioned on anatomical
composition. All experts still execute; spatially shared gating
reduces routing-logit evaluations rather than sparsifying expert
computation. Here, anatomy-activated refers to anatomical
conditioning of the mixture weights, without requiring equal
expert utilization or a one-to-one correspondence between experts
and organs.
\section{Methodology}
\subsection{Anatomy-aware image--text pairs}
\label{sec:data}
We denote a multimodal dataset as $\{(\mathbf{X}_n^{I},\,\mathbf{X}_n^{T})\}_{n=1}^{N}$, where $\mathbf{X}_n^{I}$ is the $n$-th CT volume, $\mathbf{X}_n^{T}$ its radiology report, and $N$ the number of pairs. Following \cite{shuilarge}, we decompose each pair into anatomy-level components:
\begin{equation}
(\mathbf{X}_n^{I},\,\mathbf{X}_n^{T}) \;\longmapsto\;
\bigl\{(\mathbf{X}_{n,c}^{I},\,\mathbf{X}_{n,c}^{T})\bigr\}_{c\in\mathcal{C}_n},
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{X}_{n,c}^{I}$ and $\mathbf{X}_{n,c}^{T}$ are the volumetric region and free-text findings of organ class $c$, and $\mathcal{C}_n\subseteq\{1,\dots,C-1\}$ is the set of anatomical classes present and described for the $n$-th study. Anatomical structures are extracted via TotalSegmentator~\cite{wasserthal2023totalsegmentator}. Diagnostic reports are decomposed into organ-level findings with Qwen3-30B-A3B-Instruct~\cite{yang2025qwen3}. Subsequently, the masks and text snippets are reviewed by experienced radiologists to improve anatomical precision (see Appendix~\ref{sec:appendixdatapreprocessing}). These masks and organ-level snippets are used to construct pre-training supervision only; at inference, A$^2$MoE requires only the raw CT volume as input.
\subsection{Anatomy-Activated Mixture-of-Experts (A$^2$MoE) layer}
\label{sec:a2moe}
Dense 3D encoders route every spatial position through identical feed-forward parameters, implicitly assuming homogeneous feature statistics across the volume \cite{lu2026segmote,plotka2026mamba}. This is suboptimal for volumetric medical images, where anatomical structures vary in scale, shape, and appearance within a single crop \cite{szczepanski2024let,zhang2025foundation}. Standard MoE layers activate different experts per input but condition gating solely on hidden visual features, which does not explicitly represent the anatomical composition of the crop. We propose \textbf{A$^2$MoE}, which conditions expert selection on a compact \emph{anatomy representation} of the crop together with global visual context. A$^2$MoE executes four steps at each encoder stage $s$: (i)~build a batch of routing vectors $\mathbf{v}_s$ from the feature map $\mathbf{X}_s$; (ii)~form a normalized histogram representation $\widetilde{\mathbf{h}}$; (iii)~map $(\mathbf{v}_s,\widetilde{\mathbf{h}})$ to expert logits and softmax weights $\mathbf{W}_s\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times K_s}$; (iv)~apply the same mixture $\mathbf{W}_s$ to every spatial location of $\mathbf{X}_s$ and add a residual. 
\noindent
\textbf{Routing context.} Let $\mathbf{X}_s\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C_s\times D_s\times H_s\times W_s}$ denote the feature volume at hierarchical encoder stage $s\in\{1,2,3,4\}$. We aggregate complementary global spatial statistics using global average and max pooling (GAP, GMP) to capture both tissue composition and salient structural boundaries, then concatenate a learnable stage embedding $\mathbf{e}_s\in\mathbb{R}^{d_s}$:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{v}_s \;=\;
\bigl[\operatorname{GAP}(\mathbf{X}_s)\,\|\,\operatorname{GMP}(\mathbf{X}_s)\,\|\,\mathbf{e}_s\bigr]
\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{B\times (2C_s+d_s)}.
\end{equation}
The stage embedding $\mathbf e_s$ is broadcast across the $B$ samples. GAP captures overall tissue composition, while GMP preserves small or boundary structures that averaging would suppress.
\noindent
\textbf{Anatomical composition.} Let $\Omega=\{1,\ldots,DHW\}$ denote the
voxel domain of the input crop, and let class $0$ denote background. From the
crop's pseudo-label mask $L$, we compute:
\begin{equation}
 h_{b,c}=\frac{1}{|\Omega|}\sum_{j\in\Omega}\mathbf 1[L_{b,j}=c],
 \qquad c\in\{0,\ldots,C-1\}.
\end{equation}
Each stage predicts raw composition using a lightweight MLP,
$\hat{\mathbf h}_{s,b}=\operatorname{softmax}(\Psi_{\rm pres}^{(s)}(\mathbf v_{s,b}))$.
The head predicts crop-level voxel fractions; foreground normalization is applied only when constructing the routing representation.
Let $\mathcal T$ denote the foreground normalization defined below, and write $\widetilde{\mathbf h}=\mathcal T(\mathbf h)$. We supervise the raw and foreground-normalized composition using:
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal L_{\rm comp}^{(s)}=\frac1B\sum_b
 \left[\operatorname{KL}(\mathbf h_b\Vert\hat{\mathbf h}_{s,b})+
 \operatorname{KL}(\mathcal T(\mathbf h_b)\Vert \mathcal T(\hat{\mathbf h}_{s,b}))\right].
\end{equation}
\noindent
\textbf{Anatomical routing representation.} For a composition vector
$\mathbf h$ over background and foreground classes, let
$m_{\rm fg}(\mathbf h)=\sum_{j=1}^{C-1}h_j$. The routing representation is defined as follows:
\begin{equation}
[\mathcal T(\mathbf h)]_c =
\begin{cases}
h_c/m_{\rm fg}(\mathbf h),
 & c\geq1,\quad m_{\rm fg}(\mathbf h)>0,
0,
 & c=0,\quad m_{\rm fg}(\mathbf h)>0,
h_c,
 & m_{\rm fg}(\mathbf h)=0.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
The transformation removes background and normalizes the remaining organ fractions. When the crop contains no foreground, the raw distribution is retained; the implementation uses a small positive tolerance for this fallback. The same normalization is used for label-derived and predicted composition. During pre-training, routing uses label-derived or detached predicted composition according to the schedule in Appendix~\ref{sec:objective_details}; inference uses predicted composition only. The resulting representation preserves the relative extent of visible organs and supplies anatomical context to the router. Expert weights also depend on visual features and learned affinities; normalization alone does not impose equal expert usage or a one-to-one organ--expert correspondence.
\noindent
\noindent
\textbf{Representation-aware gating.} Each stage $s$ has $K_s$ experts
and a learned class-to-expert affinity matrix
$\mathbf A^{(s)}\in\mathbb R^{C\times K_s}$.
Its row-wise softmax, $\widetilde{\mathbf A}^{(s)}$, assigns a
distribution over experts to each anatomical class. Weighting these
distributions by the normalized anatomical histogram
$\widetilde{\mathbf h}$ yields the structural expert prior:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf p_s
=
\widetilde{\mathbf h}\,\widetilde{\mathbf A}^{(s)}
\;\in\;(\Delta^{K_s-1})^B.
\end{equation}
The gating logits combine a joint visual--anatomical route,
a direct anatomical route, and the structural prior:
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol\ell_s =
\underbrace{
\Psi_{\rm gate}^{(s)}
\!\left([\mathbf v_s\Vert\psi^{(s)}(\widetilde{\mathbf h})]\right)
}_{\text{visual--anatomical route}}
+
\underbrace{
\Psi_{\rm anat}^{(s)}(\widetilde{\mathbf h})
}_{\text{direct anatomical route}}
+
\underbrace{
\beta_s\log(\mathbf p_s+\epsilon)
}_{\text{structural affinity prior}}.
\end{equation}
Here $\psi^{(s)}$ embeds the anatomical routing representation,
$\Psi_{\rm gate}^{(s)}$ maps the concatenated context to $K_s$ logits,
and $\Psi_{\rm anat}^{(s)}$ provides a direct anatomy-to-logit path.
The learned positive coefficient $\beta_s$ controls the strength of the anatomical prior, while $\epsilon$ is a small constant
ensuring numerical stability.
The normalized expert mixture weights are:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf W_s
=
\operatorname{softmax}
\!\left(\boldsymbol\ell_s/T_{\rm route}\right),
\qquad T_{\rm route}=0.7.
\end{equation}
These affinities provide anatomical conditioning without imposing
a one-to-one correspondence between organs and experts.
