\noindent
\textbf{Expert aggregation.} Each stage holds $K_s$ bottleneck MLP experts $\{E_k^{(s)}\}_{k=1}^{K_s}$ implemented as $1\!{\times}\!1\!{\times}\!1$ convolutions over channels. With $\hat{\mathbf{X}}_s = \operatorname{InstanceNorm}(\mathbf{X}_s)$,
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{Y}_{s,b} \;=\; \mathbf{X}_{s,b} \;+\;
\sum_{k=1}^{K_s} W_{s,b,k}\,E_k^{(s)}(\hat{\mathbf{X}}_{s,b}).
\end{equation}
\noindent
\textbf{Computational cost.} Let $M_s=D_sH_sW_s$ be the number of spatial positions at stage $s$, and let $d_s^{\rm gate}$ be the width entering a linear expert-logit projection. Per sample, evaluating this projection at every token costs $\mathcal O(M_s d_s^{\rm gate}K_s)$, whereas one crop-level evaluation costs $\mathcal O(d_s^{\rm gate}K_s)$. These bounds concern logit evaluation after context aggregation: pooling still reads the spatial feature map, and anatomy embedding and affinity-prior computations contribute additional per-crop work. All experts are applied to the feature map, so the savings concern repeated routing computations rather than a sparse reduction in expert FLOPs.
\subsection{The A$^2$MoE VLM architecture}
\label{sec:architecture}
A$^2$MoE-VLM is based on a 3D CNN-Mamba vision encoder, a frozen biomedical text encoder, and OQMSP that aligns per-organ visual features with the corresponding organ-specific report findings at every encoder stage (see Figure~\ref{fig:overview}).
\noindent
\textbf{Vision encoder.} The A$^{2}$MoE Block (A$^{2}$MoEB) extends the SegMamba~\cite{xing2024segmamba} layer with a Gated Spatial Convolution (GSC) \cite{xing2024segmamba} module and the A$^{2}$MoE layer (Sec.~\ref{sec:a2moe}).  Given a volume $\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times 1\times D\times H\times W}$, a stem produces the initial feature map in $\mathbb{R}^{B\times 48\times D/2\times H/2\times W/2}$, which is then processed by four A$^{2}$MoEBs with strided downsampling. For the $\ell$-th layer of stage $s$,
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{X}^{\prime\,\ell}_s = f_{\mathrm{GSC}}(\mathbf{X}^{\,\ell}_s),
\quad
\bar{\mathbf{X}}^{\,\ell}_s = f_{\mathrm{Mamba}}\!\bigl(f_{\mathrm{Norm}}(\mathbf{X}^{\prime\,\ell}_s)\bigr)+\mathbf{X}^{\prime\,\ell}_s,
\quad
\mathbf{X}^{\,\ell+1}_s = {f_{\mathrm{A^2MoE}}}\!\bigl(f_{\mathrm{Norm}}(\bar{\mathbf{X}}^{\,\ell}_s)\bigr)+\bar{\mathbf{X}}^{\,\ell}_s,
\end{equation}
where $f_{\text{Norm}}$ denotes instance normalization. Features undergo spatial linearization to form a 1D sequence prior to $f_{\text{Mamba}}$ and are subsequently restored to their volumetric dimensions before $f_{\text{A$^{2}$MoE}}$. We write $\mathbf{X}_s$ for the MoE-refined output of stage $s$.
\noindent
\textbf{Text encoder.} Each per-organ finding $t$ is encoded by a frozen PubMedBERT~\cite{gu2021domain} backbone. Token hidden states $\{\mathbf{u}_j\}$ are averaged with the attention mask $m_j\in\{0,1\}$ and passed through a trainable linear projection $\phi_{\text{txt}}$:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{z}_{\text{txt}} \;=\;
\operatorname{L2}\!\left(
\phi_{\text{txt}}\!\Bigl(
\tfrac{\sum_j m_j\mathbf{u}_j}{\sum_j m_j}\Bigr)
\right)
\;\in\;\mathbb{S}^{d_e-1},
\end{equation}
where $\operatorname{L2}$ denotes $\ell_2$-normalization. Mean pooling aggregates information across content tokens, providing a pooled representation of each finding~\cite{reimers2019sentence}.
