\noindent
\textbf{Organ-Query Multi-Scale Projector (OQMSP).} Aligning volumetric features with per-organ text requires a per-organ embedding at every scale. Prior multi-scale CLIP-style projectors either pool globally, losing anatomical specificity~\cite{park2026moeclip}, or rely on a ground-truth mask for organ-wise pooling, which can be unavailable at inference or computationally expensive. We instead use a lightweight DETR-style slot projector~\cite{carion2020end} whose forward pass does not use segmentation masks. Masks are used only to define organ-level supervision during pre-training.
We learn $C-1$ organ queries $\mathbf{Q}\in\mathbb{R}^{(C-1)\times d_e}$ (one per non-background class), shared across all four stages, together with per-stage embeddings $\mathbf r_s\in\mathbb R^{d_e}$\footnote{At stage $s$ the key/value $3{\times}3{\times}3$ convolution can use a stride $>1$ to keep attention tractable on the finest-resolution volume.}. At stage $s$, a stage-specific 3D convolution produces keys and values from $\mathbf{X}_s$; $\operatorname{LN}$ denotes layer normalization:
\begin{equation}
[\mathbf{K}_s,\mathbf{V}_s] \;=\;
\operatorname{LN}\!\bigl(\operatorname{Flatten}(\operatorname{Conv3D}_s(\mathbf{X}_s))\bigr)
\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{B\times N_s\times 2d_e},
\end{equation}
where $N_s$ is the number of key/value tokens after the stage-specific convolution, including any spatial downsampling. A multi-head cross-attention (MHA) block with a residual feed-forward network (FFN) then yields per-stage organ embeddings:
\begin{align}
 &\tilde{\mathbf{Q}}_s = \operatorname{LN}\!\bigl(\mathbf{Q}+\mathbf r_s\bigr), \quad \mathbf{E}_s = \mathbf{Q}+\operatorname{MHA}\!\bigl(\tilde{\mathbf{Q}}_s,\mathbf{K}_s,\mathbf{V}_s\bigr), 
 &\hat{\mathbf{E}}_s = \operatorname{L2}\!\bigl(\mathbf{E}_s+\operatorname{FFN}_s(\mathbf{E}_s)\bigr) \in \mathbb{R}^{B\times(C-1)\times d_e}.
\end{align}
Sharing $\mathbf Q$ across stages encourages consistent organ representations across resolutions, so the consistency regularizer below becomes an explicit prior on a scale-invariant organ latent. The cross-attention weights additionally provide
an organ-indexed, mask-free soft localization map. Scales are combined with learnable weights normalized by softmax $\boldsymbol{\alpha}=\operatorname{softmax}(a_1,\dots,a_4)$:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{Z}_{\text{vis}} \;=\;
\operatorname{L2}\!\Bigl(\operatorname{LN}\!\Bigl(\textstyle\sum_{s=1}^{4}\alpha_s\hat{\mathbf{E}}_s\Bigr)\Bigr)
\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{B\times(C-1)\times d_e}.
\end{equation}
The row $\mathbf{Z}_{\text{vis}}[b,c]$ is the visual embedding of organ $c$ in sample $b$ and is produced by OQMSP without an explicit segmentation-mask or histogram input. At inference, the encoder features remain conditioned on predicted anatomical composition.
\noindent
\textbf{Pre-training objective.} We jointly train with a weighted objective that combines spatial segmentation, anatomy-conditioned routing, organ--text alignment, cross-stage consistency, and query grounding:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal L =
\lambda_{\rm seg}\,\mathcal L_{\rm seg}
+\lambda_{\rm MoE}\,\mathcal L_{\rm MoE}
+\lambda_{\rm align}\,\mathcal L_{\rm align}
+\lambda_{\rm stage}\,\mathcal L_{\rm stage}
+\lambda_{\rm ground}\,\mathcal L_{\rm ground}.
\end{equation}
Here, $\mathcal L_{\rm seg}$ is Dice--cross-entropy supervision for the parallel decoder, and $\mathcal L_{\rm MoE}$ combines crop-composition supervision with regularization of the anatomy-conditioned router. $\mathcal L_{\rm align}$ is symmetric organ-level InfoNCE between OQMSP visual embeddings and their corresponding report findings. $\mathcal L_{\rm stage}$ penalizes cosine disagreement between same-organ embeddings from different encoder stages, while $\mathcal L_{\rm ground}$ uses a KL loss to align normalized query attention with downsampled organ pseudo-masks. Pseudo-masks supervise the decoder and query attention during pre-training; the router and OQMSP do not require masks at inference.
Full objective definition details appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:objective_details}.
\section{Experiments}
\subsection{Experimental setup}
\noindent
\textbf{Datasets.} We pre-train A$^2$MoE on a curated dataset of 67,331 multi-center, multi-phase CT scans from 37,989 patients. These data are sourced from the public Merlin \cite{blankemeier2026merlin,bassi2026merlin} and CancerVerse \cite{li2026cancerverse} datasets and one proprietary dataset. All scans are paired with corresponding radiology reports. We provide comprehensive dataset specifications in Appendix \ref{sec:appendixdatasets}.
\noindent
\textbf{Evaluation metrics.} We compare the performance of state-of-the-art pre-training methods for various downstream tasks, including multi-disease classification, detection, and segmentation. For classification and detection, following \cite{hamamci_generalist_2026,shuilarge}, we evaluate using specificity, sensitivity, and ROC-AUC. For segmentation, we use the Dice similarity coefficient (DSC) and the 95th-percentile Hausdorff distance (HD95).
\noindent
\textbf{Implementation details.} Following \cite{blankemeier2026merlin}, we resample each CT scan to $1.5\! \times\! 1.5\! \times\! 3.0$ $\text{mm}^{3}$ anisotropic resolution. For intensity normalization, all CT scans are clipped to the Hounsfield unit (HU) window of $[-1000, 1000]$ and scaled linearly to $[0, 1]$. During pre-training, $192\!\times\! 192 \!\times\! 96$ foreground-biased crops are used as model inputs. Anatomy histograms are computed from the visible label voxels inside each crop; organ-text contrastive pairs are formed only for organs with sufficient crop support, while the presence head is trained on the crop-level histogram. Detailed pre-training and fine-tuning settings are provided in Appendix~\ref{sec:experimentalsetup}.
