\subsection{Comparison with state-of-the-art methods}
We compare our method with several state-of-the-art segmentation models, including VoCo \cite{wu2024voco,wu2025large}, SegMamba \cite{xing2024segmamba}, Mamba-HoME \cite{plotka2026mamba}, SuPreM \cite{liwell}, and uC 3DU-Net \cite{huang2024upping}. We also evaluate against MedVLM-based methods, specifically Merlin \cite{blankemeier2026merlin}, fVLM \cite{shuilarge}, BTB3D \cite{hamamci2025better}, RadGPT \cite{bassi2025radgpt}, and CT-CLIP \cite{hamamci_generalist_2026}. For classification and detection tasks, we compare against CT-CHAT \cite{hamamci_generalist_2026}, CT2Rep \cite{hamamci2024ct2rep}, M3D \cite{bai2024m3d}, and RadFM \cite{wu2025towards}. To ensure a fair comparison, we trained each model using consistent hardware settings and data augmentations \cite{eisenmann2023winner}. All hyperparameters were set according to their original papers.
\subsection{Downstream tasks} 
To demonstrate the robustness and capability of our pre-trained encoder, we conduct additional experiments on several downstream tasks, including segmentation, classification, and detection.
\noindent
\textbf{Segmentation.} We integrate the pre-trained encoder with a UNETR-based decoder \cite{hatamizadeh2022unetr}. These experiments utilize both in-distribution and out-of-distribution datasets across multiple modalities, including AMOS-CT/MRI \cite{ji2022amos}, PANORAMA \cite{alves2026artificial}, GEPAR3D \cite{szczepanski2025gepar3d}, MRISegmenter \cite{zhuang2025mrisegmenter}, and MVSeg \cite{carnahan2021deepmitral}. Table \ref{tab:segmentationbenchmark} shows quantitative segmentation performance results. A$^{2}$MoE demonstrates superior performance across diverse imaging modalities, consistently outperforming established state-of-the-art models. Quantitative evaluation indicates that the architecture is uniquely capable of segmenting both fine-grained anatomical details and large-scale structures with high precision.
To assess the robustness of the observed performance gains,  we use critical-difference diagrams (see Figure \ref{fig:cd_combined_statistical_tests}). We consider differences statistically significant at $p < 0.05$. Qualitatively, A$^{2}$MoE produces anatomically consistent results that are largely free from the artifacts typically found in competing methods (see Figure~\ref{fig:qualitative_main}). Furthermore, the architecture maintains high reliability across both in-distribution and out-of-distribution datasets, demonstrating a robust ability to generalize across varying object scales and data acquisition protocols.
\noindent
\textbf{Classification.} Figure~\ref{fig:classification_mean} and Table~\ref{tab:ratic_per_grade} evaluate abdominal trauma classification on RATIC~\cite{rudie2024rsna}. A$^2$MoE achieves the best mean ROC-AUC among the evaluated encoders and improves most strongly on bowel and liver injury, where organ-localized context is clinically important. These results support the use of anatomy-conditioned representations beyond segmentation, while kidney and spleen results show that the gains are not uniform across every organ and metric.
\noindent
\textbf{Detection.} Figure~\ref{fig:detection_plot} and Table~\ref{tab:detection_results} evaluate tumor detection on AbdomenAtlas~3.0 \cite{bassi2025radgpt}. A$^{2}$MoE is most competitive for small lesions ($\le$2 cm), improving sensitivity for kidney and liver tumors and matching the best pancreatic sensitivity among the evaluated models. The specificity--sensitivity trade-off differs by organ, especially for kidney tumors, so we interpret these results as evidence that anatomy-conditioned features improve small-lesion sensitivity rather than as a uniformly dominant detector.
