\subsection{Ablation studies}
We investigate the impact of several factors on the performance of A$^2$MoE, focusing on: (1) key architectural components; (2) histogram-aware routing; and (3) the number of experts per encoder stage. For a fair comparison, we maintain the same training dataset and hyperparameters across all experimental configurations to isolate the impact of each component. Detailed ablation studies are provided in Appendix \ref{sec:appendixablationstudies}.
\noindent
\textbf{Effect of the key components.} We evaluate the contribution of the two main components of our segmentation model: A$^{2}$MoE routing and contrastive alignment. We compare three configurations: (i) the SegMamba baseline~\cite{xing2024segmamba} (\#1);
(ii) SegMamba with A$^{2}$MoE (\#2); and (iii) the full model combining A$^{2}$MoE and contrastive alignment (OQMSP).
As shown in Table~\ref{tab:segmentationbenchmark}, A$^{2}$MoE improves over the baseline across datasets, while the full model gives the strongest overall
performance. These results indicate that anatomy-activated routing and contrastive alignment provide complementary benefits for segmentation tasks.
