\section{Conclusions}
We introduced A$^{2}$MoE, a 3D MedVLM that incorporates explicit anatomical priors into both conditional computation and cross-modal alignment. The proposed HRR grounds expert selection in crop-level class histograms combined with a learnable class-to-expert affinity matrix, producing a gating distribution per crop and stage. Furthermore, the OQMSP aligns MoE-refined visual features with organ-specific textual representations through scale-shared queries. At inference, anatomical composition is predicted from image features, allowing the visual branch to operate without segmentation masks or report text. Pre-trained on a large-scale cohort of over $60{,}000$ CT scans, A$^{2}$MoE achieves state-of-the-art performance on external benchmarks for segmentation, classification, and detection. Moreover, it demonstrates strong transfer to MRI and ultrasound, suggesting that anatomical grounding serves as a modality-agnostic prior.
\noindent
\textbf{Limitations and future directions.} Despite its performance, A$^{2}$MoE has certain limitations. First, the current framework relies on a fixed set of anatomical classes for the composition histogram; while this covers major organ systems, it may require adaptation for highly granular or rare pathological structures not represented in the pre-training prior. Second, while the model exhibits strong cross-modality transfer, its performance is inherently tied to the quality and diversity of the initial CT-report pairs. Finally, A$^{2}$MoE focuses on static 3D volumes; extending this anatomical conditioning to longitudinal data remains an open challenge for future research.
