We leverage the pre-trained TotalSegmentator~\cite{wasserthal2023totalsegmentator} to generate voxel-wise pseudo-labels (see Figure~\ref{fig:ts_segmentation_results}). Note that the pseudo-labels and radiology organ snippets were verified by experienced radiologists; see Sec.~\ref{sec:verificationsnippets} for details. Although the original model encompasses 117 anatomical structures, we directly extract 14 key abdominal organs, including \textit{spleen, left kidney, right kidney, gallbladder, liver, stomach, pancreas, left adrenal gland, right adrenal gland, duodenum, small bowel, colon, urinary bladder, and prostate} (see Table~\ref{tab:selectedclasses}). This pruning process serves two primary purposes: first, it filters out structures irrelevant to our domain-specific radiology reports (e.g., thoracic organs); and second, it removes low-prevalence classes (e.g., skeletal and vascular structures) that introduce unnecessary noise. This semantic alignment bridges the gap between high-resolution volumetric segmentations and the coarser, descriptive terminology utilized in clinical practice. By consolidating these structures, we reduce computational complexity and ensure the segmentation output is clinically actionable, facilitating better integration with standardized reporting frameworks (see Figure~\ref{fig:3dvisualizations}).
The adoption of these masks also addresses a fundamental challenge in medical vision-language modeling: the inherent scarcity and spatial ambiguity of lesion localization in clinical text. Pathological findings in specific anatomical regions often follow a long-tailed distribution, leading to an acute class imbalance during training. Without such anatomical grounding, many training iterations would consist of "normal" samples, potentially leading to representation collapse or a bias toward the majority class. By incorporating these 14 structures as an auxiliary spatial prior, we provide the model with a robust framework to learn meaningful diagnostic features even in the presence of sparse or imbalanced clinical labels.
