\subsubsection{Report decomposition}
\label{appendix:report_decomposition}
Raw radiology reports are cleaned and decomposed at the organ level based on the selected anatomical structures. We clean these reports by removing unnecessary information, such as dates, details regarding modalities other than CT, and findings related to previous examinations. Following \cite{shuilarge}, the decomposition is performed as follows:
\begin{enumerate} 
 \item We develop domain-knowledge prompts with guidance from experienced radiologists to ensure comprehensive terminology coverage, preventing missed organ structures arising from synonym variation in clinical reports (see Section \ref{appendix:prompts}); 
 \item We employ Qwen3-30B-A3B-Instruct \cite{yang2025qwen3} for image-text alignment at the organ level using the prompts presented in Section \ref{appendix:prompts}. To accelerate inference, we employ vLLM \cite{kwon2023efficient}; 
 \item We then align the descriptions of each anatomical structure from the \textit{findings} and \textit{impression} sections,
 \item Lastly, for an organ described as \textit{normal}, we use a template describing the absence of pathology (see Section  \ref{appendix:normaltemplates}).
\end{enumerate}
\subsubsection{Diagnosis extraction}
To automate the labeling process, we utilized an LLM (Qwen3-30B-A3B-Instruct), prompted with a domain-specific heuristic to distinguish between clinical pathology and benign anatomical descriptions for each organ (see Table \ref{tab:selectedclasses}). The core challenge in radiological labeling is preventing "false positives" triggered by descriptive text that does not indicate active disease.
We established a strict hierarchy for the \textit{Pathological (1)} label: it is reserved for intrinsic tissue abnormalities, including mass effects, inflammatory processes (e.g., dilatation, stones), or persistent lesions (including stable cysts). Conversely, the \textit{Normal/Unremarkable (0)} label accounts for three specific clinical scenarios:
\begin{enumerate}
 \item Anatomical and Vascular Variants: Non-pathological deviations such as "replaced" arterial supplies,
 \item Stable Post-Surgical States: The absence of an organ or expected post-operative changes without secondary complications,
 \item External Proximity: Displacement or compression of an organ by an adjacent mass (e.g., "modeling") is excluded from the pathological count unless the report explicitly notes tissue infiltration or parenchymal damage.
\end{enumerate}
\subsubsection{Normal templates}
\label{appendix:normaltemplates}
To mitigate the representation collapse often observed in medical domain embeddings, we move beyond the use of monolithic labels like "Normal." Such simplistic categorizations fail to capture the multimodal distribution of healthy anatomical states, leading to an underspecified latent space that lacks the granularity required for robust contrastive learning.
Instead, we synthesize structured normal templates to serve as high-fidelity prototypical anchors. By embedding rich, organ-specific descriptive text rather than a binary state, we force the model to align its representations with detailed physiological features (e.g., organ axis, attenuation, and morphology). This approach ensures that during the contrastive optimization, specifically when minimizing the distance between positive pairs, the model learns a structured embedding space where "normality" is defined by a dense cluster of anatomical constraints. Consequently, pathological deviations are not just "not normal," but are explicitly represented as semantic residuals relative to a highly defined physiological baseline, enhancing the model’s ability to generalize across heterogeneous clinical manifestations.
