\subsubsection{Radiology report translation}
We translate our proprietary radiology reports from Polish to English using Bielik-11B-v3.0-Instruct~\cite{ociepa2025bielik}.
\subsubsection{Verification of anatomy-aware snippets}
\label{sec:verificationsnippets}
\textbf{Pseudo-label correction.} To maximize segmentation reliability, we implement a multi-layered verification framework that integrates clinical ground truth with automated quality-control heuristics. The first layer involves cross-referencing pseudo-masks with the original radiology report to ensure anatomical plausibility. For instance, when a report explicitly documents a "status post-nephrectomy" or "surgically absent" organ, any voxels erroneously detected by TotalSegmentator \cite{wasserthal2023totalsegmentator} for that structure are programmatically reclassified as background. This report-guided correction directly addresses the most common systematic failure mode of deep-learning segmenters: the tendency to misidentify scar tissue, surgical clips, or adjacent structures as an organ that is no longer present.
Building upon this clinical validation, we adopt the methodology of "Label Critic" \cite{bassi2025label} as a formal quality-control mechanism to manage label uncertainty. This framework identifies likely incorrect labels in single-annotation settings by flagging outliers that deviate from learned anatomical priors. Furthermore, in scenarios where multiple segmenters or iterations produce competing outputs, the mechanism acts as an arbiter to select the superior annotation based on spatial consistency and boundary fidelity. By combining NLP-driven anatomical pruning with these formal QC metrics, the pipeline ensures that downstream quantitative analysis, such as radiomics or volumetry, is performed on masks that are verified against the patient’s specific surgical history and radiological reality.
\textbf{Radiology report snippets.} To ensure the clinical accuracy of the dataset, each anatomy-aware snippet underwent manual review by a senior radiologist with over 30 years of clinical experience. The verification process focused on confirming that the extracted segments maintained anatomical integrity and clinical relevance relative to the original imaging reports.
