\subsubsection{Merlin} 
Merlin \cite{blankemeier2026merlin} consists of 25,494 publicly available\footnote{Merlin is publicly available at \url{https://stanfordaimi.azurewebsites.net/datasets/60b9c7ff-877b-48ce-96c3-0194c8205c40}} contrast-enhanced computed tomography (CECT) scans from 18,317 patients. Each CT exam is paired with the corresponding radiology report. The dataset includes abdominal and pelvic CT exams conducted between 2012 and 2018 at the Stanford Hospital Emergency Departments. 
\subsubsection{CancerVerse} 
CancerVerse \cite{li2026cancerverse} is a publicly available longitudinal and multimodal CT dataset for multicancer screening\footnote{CancerVerse is publicly available at \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/BodyMaps/CancerVerse}}. The current release contains 24,422 CT scans from 14,599 patients, acquired at hospitals in Switzerland and Turkey between 2010 and 2025. It covers the chest, abdomen, and pelvis and pairs CT imaging with radiology reports, complemented by pathology results and clinical variables. The dataset provides expert voxel-wise tumor annotations for 13 cancer types and includes a verified healthy cohort for evaluating false-positive detections. Patient histories span up to 14.4 years, with up to 26 examination dates per patient, supporting longitudinal analysis of imaging findings and their associated reports.
\subsubsection{Proprietary set}
We utilized a nationwide cohort from Poland comprising 17,415 multi-phase CT scans across 5,805 examinations from 4,004 patients. The dataset was collected from over 300 hospitals across the country, with each scan paired with its corresponding radiology report. Ethical approval for this study was granted by the Bioethics Committee at the Faculty of Medicine, Collegium Medicum, University of Warmia and Mazury in Olsztyn (Approval No. 9/2023, March 30, 2023).
\subsection{Fine-tuning}
\subsubsection{AMOS-CT}
The AMOS-CT dataset\footnote{AMOS-CT dataset is publicly available at \url{https://zenodo.org/records/7262581}} \cite{ji2022amos} consists of a total of 500 CT scans collected from diverse clinical centers, ensuring significant variability in scanner types and patient demographics. However, the ground-truth labels are publicly accessible only for the training and validation cohorts, comprising 200 and 100 scans, respectively. Each volume provides high-quality, voxel-level manual annotations for 15 distinct abdominal organs: spleen, right kidney, left kidney, gallbladder, esophagus, liver, stomach, aorta, inferior vena cava, pancreas, right adrenal gland, left adrenal gland, duodenum, bladder, and prostate/uterus. For our experimental setup, we utilize the 200 training scans for model optimization and the 100 validation scans as our test set. The dataset is particularly challenging due to its large intra-class variation; for instance, the number of axial slices per scan ranges from 68 to 353, and the voxel spacing varies significantly from $[0.45,1.07]\times[0.45,1.07]\times[1.25,5.0]$ $\text{mm}^3$. The mean spacing is $0.70 \times 0.70 \times 4.20$ $\text{mm}^3$. Beyond its scale, AMOS-CT is recognized for including both healthy and pathological cases, requiring models to be robust against anatomical distortions and varied fields of view typical in real-world clinical environments.
\subsubsection{AMOS-MRI}
AMOS-MRI is the MRI subset of the AMOS dataset \cite{ji2022amos}, comprising 100 3D abdominal MRI scans with voxel-wise annotations of 15 anatomical structures\footnote{AMOS-MRI is publicly available at \url{https://zenodo.org/records/7262581}}. The annotations cover major abdominal organs and vessels, including the liver, spleen, kidneys, pancreas, stomach, aorta, and inferior vena cava. Although the training annotations include 15 foreground anatomical classes, the AMOS-MRI test-set annotations do not include the urinary bladder or the prostate/uterus class. We therefore evaluate segmentation performance on the remaining 13 foreground classes. The benchmark includes 40 scans for training, 20 for validation, and 40 for testing, with the test set further divided into 11 in-distribution and 29 out-of-distribution scans. Its heterogeneous acquisition settings support the evaluation of segmentation accuracy and generalization across scanner domains.
\subsubsection{PANORAMA} The PANORAMA dataset \cite{alves2024panorama} comprises 2,238 multi-center contrast-enhanced computed tomography (CECT) scans acquired in a single portal-venous phase. It includes 1,964 newly acquired scans from five European centers, as well as publicly available data from two medical datasets in the United States, namely NIH \cite{roth2015deeporgan} and MSD Pancreas \cite{antonelli2022medical}. The dataset consists of 676 PDAC (pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma) cases and 1,562 non-PDAC cases. While the dataset contains six voxel-wise labels (PDAC lesion, veins, arteries, pancreatic parenchyma, pancreatic duct, and common bile duct), we use only the PDAC lesion and merge the pancreatic parenchyma and pancreatic duct classes into a single \textit{pancreas} label. Each CT image consists of 37--1,572 slices, with a resolution ranging from 512$\times$512 to 1024$\times$1024 pixels. The voxel spacing ranges over $[0.31,1.03]\times[0.31,1.03]\times[0.45,5.0]$ mm$^3$ with a mean of 0.75 $\times$ 0.75 $\times$ 2.0 mm$^3$.
\subsubsection{GEPAR3D}
We utilize the GEPAR3D dataset\footnote{GEPAR3D dataset is publicly available at \url{https://zenodo.org/records/15739014}} \cite{szczepanski2025gepar3d}, which is derived from 98 publicly available cone-beam computed tomography (CBCT) scans originally released by \citep{cui2022fully}. While the initial iteration of these data provided only binary masks (distinguishing tooth structure from background), it presented significant limitations for tasks requiring individual tooth identification or pathological analysis. To address this, the GEPAR3D authors performed an extensive manual reannotation process~\citep{szczepanski2025gepar3d}. Experienced clinicians labeled the dataset into 32 distinct anatomical classes following the Universal Numbering System, effectively transforming it into a benchmark for fine-grained multi-class dental segmentation. The dataset is characterized by its high anatomical complexity, featuring diverse dental conditions including restorations and implants that introduce metallic artifacts, varying stages of tooth decay and bone loss, and impacted teeth and irregular dental alignments. The GEPAR3D test set consists of 46 publicly available CBCT scans from four external centers as part of the GEPAR3D dataset \citep{szczepanski2025gepar3d}. This cohort includes two subsets sourced from public repositories: 15 scans from \citep{cui2022fully}, which also constitute a subset of the ToothFairy2 dataset \citep{bolelli2024segmenting, bolelli2025segmenting, lumetti2024enhancing}, and two newly released centers provided to the research community. The remaining scans originate from two independent clinical centers, ensuring a diverse evaluation of model generalization across different scanning protocols and anatomical variations.
\subsubsection{MRISegmenter} We utilize the MRISegmenter abdominal MRI cohort\footnote{The MRISegmenter segmentation tool, model weights, and accompanying dataset are publicly available at \url{https://github.com/rsummers11/MRISegmenter} under the data-use terms described in the original publication.}~\cite{zhuang2025mrisegmenter}, introduced as part of a retrospective study of patients acquired at the U.S.\ National Institutes of Health (NIH) Clinical Center. The resource comprises 195 patients with 780 3D T1-weighted abdominal MRI series in total, corresponding to four acquisitions per patient: axial pre-contrast T1 and contrast-enhanced T1 in arterial, portal venous, and delayed phases. Each series is accompanied by dense, voxel-level reference segmentations of abdominal organs and anatomic structures, providing a fine-grained multi-class benchmark for volumetric abdominal MRI segmentation beyond coarse organ-only labels. The cohort is clinically heterogeneous across adult patients (training split: 135 subjects; internal test split: 60 subjects) and spans the full multi-phase dynamic-liver protocol, which introduces realistic variability in parenchymal enhancement, vascular conspicuity, and partial-volume effects across phases. 
\subsubsection{MVSeg}
We utilize the MVSeg2023 benchmark\footnote{The MVSeg2023 challenge data are catalogued on Hugging Face at \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/pcarnahan/MVSeg2023}; the official challenge portal is \url{https://www.synapse.org/MVSeg2023}. Access may require accepting the host platform's data-use conditions.}~\cite{carnahan2021deepmitral}, released in conjunction with the MICCAI~2023 workshop on \emph{Mitral Valve Segmentation from 3D Transesophageal Echocardiography}. The goal is automatic voxel-wise delineation of the mitral valve leaflets from \emph{single-frame} three-dimensional transesophageal echocardiography (3D TEE) volumes acquired at end-diastole in a mid-esophageal, en-face mitral view. Volumes were collected on a Philips Epiq cardiac ultrasound system according to clinical standard of care at King's College Hospital (London, UK), converted from vendor DICOM to Cartesian coordinates with Philips QLab, and distributed as NIfTI files with spatial metadata limited to image size and spacing. Each case consists of one patient-specific 3D TEE volume with dense annotations of the anterior and posterior mitral leaflets (three labels: background, posterior leaflet, anterior leaflet), yielding a fine-grained yet low-cardinality segmentation task that is representative of intra-operative imaging noise, acoustic shadowing, and thin-structure ambiguity. The public release is partitioned into 105 training, 30 validation, and 40 test volumes for standardized comparison.
\subsubsection{AbdomenAtlas 3.0}
We utilize AbdomenAtlas~3.0\footnote{AbdomenAtlas~3.0 and the RadGPT
release are publicly available at \url{https://github.com/MrGiovanni/RadGPT}.}~\cite{bassi2025radgpt},
a large-scale abdominal CT resource as part of the RadGPT pipeline. The cohort aggregates
9{,}262 isotropic three-dimensional CT volumes in NIfTI format
(2{,}789{,}975 axial slices) drawn from 17 previously
released public abdominal CT datasets that originally shipped without
paired radiology reports. Each study is distributed as a \emph{triplet}
of (i)~the CT volume, (ii)~dense voxel-wise annotations of liver, kidney,
and pancreatic tumors together with ancillary structures used for staging
and localization---including Couinaud liver sub-segments (1--8),
pancreatic sub-regions (head, body, tail), and peripancreatic vasculature
relevant to pancreatic ductal
adenocarcinom (PDAC) \mbox{T1--T4}
staging, and (iii)~expert-reviewed radiology text. In total
3{,}955 scans contain at least one annotated tumor; the
curators report a $4.2\times$ expansion of publicly available
tumor masks relative to the union of the source releases, with
3{,}011 additional tumors identified after revision of automatic segmentations by board-certified radiologists. The textual modality
comprises structured, narrative, and (where applicable) enhanced reports
generated and verified under radiologist supervision with RadGPT,
documenting per-lesion size, attenuation (HU), volume, anatomic location
within sub-segments, and resectability-related attributes, for a total of
1{,}843{,}262 report tokens. AbdomenAtlas~3.0 is the first public
distribution in this line that couples every scan with both high-resolution tumor (and staging-relevant) segmentations and
paired, tumor-focused radiology text, making it a natural benchmark for 3D abdominal vision--language pre-training and
segmentation-assisted report generation.
\subsubsection{RATIC}
We utilize the RSNA Abdominal Traumatic Injury CT (RATIC)
cohort\footnote{RATIC is distributed for non-commercial research through
the RSNA AI Challenge platform at \url{https://imaging.rsna.org/dataset/5}
and the companion Kaggle release at
\url{https://www.kaggle.com/c/rsna-2023-abdominal-trauma-detection}.}~\cite{rudie2024rsna},
curated by the Radiological Society of North America (RSNA) in collaboration
with the American Society of Emergency Radiology and the Society of
Abdominal Radiology. The resource comprises 4{,}274 consecutive
adult ($\geq$18~years) abdominal--pelvic CT studies, totaling
6{,}481 image series contributed from 23 institutions
across 14 countries on six continents---at the time of its
release, the largest publicly available multi-center collection
explicitly labeled for blunt abdominal traumatic injury on CT. Each
study ships in DICOM format together with comma-separated tabular
metadata: patient demographics, series-level acquisition descriptors
(imaging phase and anatomic coverage), and study-level injury labels for
the liver, kidneys, spleen, bowel/mesentery, and active contrast
extravasation. Solid-organ injuries are graded on the American
Association for the Surgery of Trauma (AAST) organ-injury scale,
with low-grade (grades~I--III) versus high-grade (grades~IV--V)
injuries dichotomized to reduce inter-rater noise; bowel/mesenteric
injury and extravasation receive complementary image-level annotations
because of their heterogeneous appearance across phases. In
addition, voxel-wise segmentations of the liver, spleen, left and right
kidneys, and a composite gastrointestinal label (esophagus through
colon) are publicly released in NIfTI format for a 206-series
pilot subset of the training portion, enabling joint segmentation and
injury-classification benchmarks. The dataset was used for the RSNA
2023 Abdominal Trauma Detection challenge and is intended to stress-test
algorithms under realistic protocol heterogeneity (e.g.\ mono- versus
multi-phase acquisition, variable slice thickness), reflecting the
clinical variability encountered in emergency CT workflows.
