\section{Experimental setup}
\label{sec:experimentalsetup}
We provide a detailed description of our experimental setup, covering data preprocessing, model implementation specifics, and evaluation metrics.
\subsection{Hardware and software setup}
\label{appendix:hardware_and_software_setup}
All experiments are conducted on a high-performance computing cluster utilizing 4 $\times$ NVIDIA GH200 GPUs, each with 96 GB of memory. All networks are implemented using Python 3.11, PyTorch 2.7 \cite{paszke2019pytorch}, and the medical imaging framework MONAI 1.3.0. Training stability and efficiency are ensured by employing the PyTorch Distributed Data-Parallel (DDP) framework. For DDP training, we use PyTorch Lightning Fabric.
\subsection{Pre-training}
We utilize TotalSegmentator \cite{wasserthal2023totalsegmentator} to segment anatomical structures from the 3D CT scans. For downstream analysis, we focus on the primary anatomical structures within the abdomen and pelvis, resulting in $C-1=14$ foreground classes and $C=15$ total classes (see Table \ref{tab:selectedclasses} for the full list).
We resample all CT scans to 1.5 $\times$ 1.5 $\times$ 3.0 mm$^{3}$ anisotropic resolution.  All CT scans are normalized to a range of $[0, 1]$. We first apply a clipping operation to the raw Hounsfield Units (HU) to focus on clinically relevant intensities and remove artifacts. The normalized intensity $I_{\text{norm}}$ is calculated as:
\begin{equation}
I_{\text{norm}} = \frac{\text{clamp}(I, HU_{\min}, HU_{\max}) - HU_{\min}}{HU_{\max} - HU_{\min}}
\label{eq:normalization}
\end{equation}
where the clipping bounds are set to $[-1000, 1000]$.
\subsection{Pre-training objective and routing details}
\label{sec:objective_details}
The implementation described here uses the anatomical routing representation defined in the main text. Each stage predicts raw crop composition. During training, label-derived or detached predicted composition is selected independently for each sample; for zero-based epoch $e$, the probability of predicted routing is $p_e=0.5+0.5\min(e/3,1)$. At inference, routing uses predicted composition only. No segmentation mask or report is required.
\noindent
\textbf{Routing supervision.} The routing objective sums the composition loss and router penalties over stages:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_{\rm MoE}=\sum_{s=1}^{4}\left[
\mathcal L_{\rm comp}^{(s)}+
0.5\,\mathbb E_b\operatorname{KL}\!\left(
\mathbf W_{s,b}\,\Vert\,
\operatorname{sg}\!\left[\mathcal T(\mathbf h_b)\widetilde{\mathbf A}^{(s)}\right]\right)
+\mathcal R_s\right].
\end{equation}
Here $\operatorname{sg}$ stops gradients through the alignment target. The usage regularizer $\mathcal R_s$ combines rolling anatomical-prior matching, a normalized per-sample entropy-band penalty with bounds $[0.25,0.8]$, and foreground affinity coverage. Its outer weight is $0.1$ and the relative coverage weight is $0.1$. These terms encourage controlled mixture usage, without guaranteeing equal weights or organ-exclusive experts. The positive prior strength is parameterized as $\beta_s=\operatorname{softplus}(b_s)$, and the log-prior uses $\epsilon=10^{-6}$.
\noindent
\textbf{Organ--text supervision.} The symmetric within-organ InfoNCE objective uses structured report findings. Exact duplicates after case folding and whitespace normalization form multiple positives. Negatives are different-case, different-text findings with opposite known normal/abnormal report labels; other pairs are masked. Each direction uses current-batch anchors and current plus queued candidates. Detached per-organ FIFO queues of length 64 are cleared at the start of each training epoch and are not used for validation. An anchor contributes only when it has both a positive and an eligible negative. Missing findings are excluded. Organ--text pairs require nonempty findings and visible organ support of at least $0.001$ of the crop; no additional whole-organ coverage filter is applied in this recipe. Report labels are weak supervision and do not establish clinical incompatibility between every negative pair.
\noindent
\textbf{Cross-stage consistency.} Organ-indexed embeddings are encouraged to agree across the four scales:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_{\rm stage}=\frac16\sum_{1\leq s<s'\leq4}
\mathbb E\!\left[1-\cos(\hat{\mathbf E}_s,\hat{\mathbf E}_{s'})\right].
\end{equation}
\noindent
\textbf{Query grounding.} For each valid organ--stage target, its binary pseudo-mask is average-pooled to the attention grid and normalized to a distribution $\mathbf m_{s,b,c}$. With normalized query attention $\mathbf a_{s,b,c}$, we use
\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_{\rm ground}=\frac1{|\mathcal G|}
\sum_{(s,b,c)\in\mathcal G}
\operatorname{KL}(\mathbf m_{s,b,c}\Vert\mathbf a_{s,b,c}),
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal G$ contains eligible targets with nonzero mask mass. The term is zero when no target is valid. Masks supervise attention during training but are not inputs to OQMSP's forward computation. This encourages organ-level attention rather than establishing lesion localization.
\subsection{Downstream tasks}
In this section, we provide a detailed description of our experimental setup for downstream tasks, including segmentation, classification, and detection.
\subsubsection{Segmentation}
Each segmentation model architecture is trained with an input patch size of $P \times P \times P$, where $P = 128$, to accommodate GPU memory constraints while preserving sufficient spatial context. We employ the sum of Dice and cross-entropy losses as our final loss function.
We utilize the AdamW optimizer with an initial learning rate $\eta_0$ set to 1e-4. The learning rate schedule follows cosine annealing with a minimum learning rate of $\eta_{\text{min}}$, which is set to 1e-6 and a warm-up period of $W$ epochs. We train the model for a total of $T$ epochs with a batch size of $B=2$ per GPU, totaling an effective batch size of $B_{\text{eff}} = 8$. Weight decay is applied with a factor of $\lambda=1e-5$.
\noindent
\textbf{AMOS-CT.} All CT scans are resampled to 1.5 $\times$ 1.5 $\times$ 1.5 mm$^{3}$ isotropic resolution. Then, we set the clipping window to $[-1000, 1000]$ and linearly scale the intensities to $[0,1]$. We train each model for 800 epochs. 
\noindent
\textbf{PANORAMA.} All CT scans are resampled to 0.8 $\times$ 0.8 $\times$ 3.0 mm$^{3}$ anisotropic resolution. Then, we set the clipping window to $[-175,250]$ and linearly scale the intensities to $[0,1]$. We train each model for 800 epochs.
\noindent
\textbf{GEPAR3D.} All CT scans are resampled to 0.4 $\times$ 0.4 $\times$ 0.4 mm$^{3}$ isotropic resolution. Then, we set the clipping window to $[0, 5000]$ and linearly scale the intensities to $[0,1]$. We train each model for 1,000 epochs.
\noindent
\textbf{AMOS-MRI.} All MRI scans are resampled to 1.5 $\times$ 1.5 $\times$ 1.5 mm$^{3}$ isotropic resolution. Then, we set the clipping window to $[0, 1000]$ and linearly scale the intensities to $[0,1]$. We train each model for 1,000 epochs.
\noindent
\textbf{MRISegmenter.} All MRI scans are resampled to 1.5 $\times$ 1.5 $\times$ 3.0 mm$^{3}$ anisotropic resolution. Then, we set the clipping window to $[0, 1000]$ and linearly scale the intensities to $[0,1]$. We train each model for 1,000 epochs.
\noindent
\textbf{MVSeg.} All US scans are resampled to 0.5 $\times$ 0.5 $\times$ 0.5 mm$^{3}$ isotropic resolution. Then, we set the clipping window to $[0, 255]$ and linearly scale the intensities to $[0,1]$. We train each model for 1,000 epochs.
\subsubsection{Classification}
We evaluate abdominal trauma \emph{classification} on RATIC~\cite{rudie2024rsna}. Each CT scan is resampled to $1.5 \times 1.5 \times 3.0$~mm$^{3}$ anisotropic spacing. For every compared model we keep only the pre-trained 3D \emph{encoder} (the segmentation decoder is discarded), attach a case-level head, and fine-tune the encoder and head on the RATIC training split. Volumes are processed with a sliding window of $P \times P \times P$ patches ($P{=}128$): we encode all valid patches, apply global average pooling over patch embeddings to obtain a fixed-length volume descriptor, and pass it through a lightweight MLP that outputs one logit per evaluated binary or graded task (bowel, liver, kidney, and spleen; see Figure~\ref{fig:classification_mean} and Table~\ref{tab:ratic_binary_compact} and Table~\ref{tab:ratic_per_grade}). Training minimizes the sum of sigmoid cross-entropy losses over the active heads on each sample.
\subsubsection{Detection}
We report pancreas, kidney, and liver tumor \emph{detection} on AbdomenAtlas~3.0~\cite{bassi2025radgpt} (dataset details in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendixdatasets}) using \emph{only} the corresponding segmentation-trained models: we do not train a separate detector. Study-level predictions are obtained from the \emph{segmentation} decoder by aggregating voxel-wise tumor-class \emph{logits} (e.g., thresholding the maximum tumor-class probability or the volume of connected high-confidence voxels). Sensitivity is stratified by lesion long-axis diameter ($\le$2~cm vs.\ $>$2~cm); specificity is computed at the study level. Results are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:detection_plot} and Table~\ref{tab:detection_results}; formal definitions appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendixevaluationmetrics_classification}.
\subsection{Evaluation metrics}
\label{sec:appendixevaluationmetrics}
\subsubsection{Segmentation}
\label{sec:appendixevaluationmetrics_segmentation}
For segmentation tasks, we use the Dice similarity coefficient (DSC) and the 95th-percentile Hausdorff distance (HD95) to quantitatively assess model performance. The DSC measures the spatial overlap between the predicted segmentation \(P\) and the ground truth \(G\), and is defined as:
\begin{equation}
 \operatorname{DSC}(P,G) = \frac{2|P \cap G|}{|P| + |G|},
\end{equation}
where \(|P|\) and \(|G|\) denote the number of voxels in the predicted and ground-truth masks, respectively. 
To evaluate boundary accuracy, we report HD95, a robust variant of the Hausdorff distance that reduces sensitivity to outliers. Let $\partial P$ and $\partial G$ denote the contours of the predicted and ground-truth masks. The following definition takes the maximum of the two directed 95th-percentile contour distances:
\begin{equation}
 \operatorname{HD95}(P, G) = \max \left\{ 
\operatorname{Percentile}_{95}\big(d(p, G)\big),\ 
\operatorname{Percentile}_{95}\!\left(\{d(g,\partial P):g\in\partial G\}\right)
\right\},
\end{equation}
where $d(p,\partial G)=\min_{g\in\partial G}\|p-g\|_2$ and $d(g,\partial P)=\min_{p\in\partial P}\|g-p\|_2$, using physical coordinates in millimeters. Higher DSC and lower HD95 indicate better segmentation performance.
\subsubsection{Classification and detection}
\label{sec:appendixevaluationmetrics_classification}
For classification and detection tasks, we evaluate model performance using sensitivity, specificity, F1-score, and the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (ROC-AUC). Let \(TP\), \(TN\), \(FP\), and \(FN\) denote the number of true-positive, true-negative, false-positive, and false-negative predictions, respectively. Sensitivity (also called recall or the true-positive rate) and specificity (the true-negative rate) are defined as:
\begin{equation}
 \text{Sensitivity} = \frac{TP}{TP + FN},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
 \text{Specificity} = \frac{TN}{TN + FP}.
\end{equation}
To account for class imbalance, we report the F1-score, which is the harmonic mean of precision and sensitivity. Given precision \( = \frac{TP}{TP + FP} \), the F1-score is defined as:
\begin{equation}
 \text{F1-score} = \frac{2TP}{2TP + FP + FN}.
\end{equation}
The ROC-AUC value ranges from 0 to 1, where an AUC of 0.5 indicates performance no better than random chance, and an AUC of 1.0 represents a perfect classifier. Higher sensitivity, specificity, F1-score, and ROC-AUC correspond to superior classification results.
