\section{Generalizability}
\label{sec:appendixgeneralizability}
The strength of a medical foundation model lies not in its performance on a single dataset, but in its ability to transcend the specific conditions of its training \cite{bassi2024improving}. A central design goal of A$^{2}$MoE is that the inductive biases it
encodes -- conditional computation routed by anatomical composition and
mask-free organ--text alignment via shared queries -- should not be
specific to any single modality, organ system, or label set.  We provide
empirical evidence for this claim along four orthogonal axes:
(i)~\emph{cross-modality transfer} from CT pre-training to MRI and 3D
ultrasound; (ii)~\emph{cross-anatomy transfer} from
abdominal and pelvic supervision to dental cone-beam CT;
(iii)~\emph{out-of-distribution robustness} on multi-center,
multi-vendor, and multi-protocol cohorts that were never seen during
pre-training; and (iv)~\emph{multi-task transfer} to downstream tasks
that the pre-training objective does not directly optimize (organ injury classification on RATIC and tumor detection on
AbdomenAtlas~3.0).
\begin{enumerate}
 \item \textbf{Cross-modality transfer (CT~$\rightarrow$~MRI/US).}
Although A$^{2}$MoE is pre-trained exclusively on multi-phase
CT, the encoder transfers without architectural modification to volumetric MRI
(AMOS-MRI~\cite{ji2022amos}, MRISegmenter~\cite{zhuang2025mrisegmenter})
and 3D transesophageal ultrasound
(MVSeg2023~\cite{carnahan2021deepmitral}). On AMOS-MRI e observe a gain of 5.6 DSC percentage points over the SegMamba backbone and $+2.2$ over
VoCo (Table~\ref{tab:segmentationbenchmark}), and on MRISegmenter the
full A$^{2}$MoE attains the best mean DSC across all 62 anatomical
classes (Table~\ref{tab:mri_segmentation_full}).  These improvements are
notable because (a)~the histogram router was supervised only on
TotalSegmentator CT labels and never observed MRI tissue contrast, and
(b)~OQMSP queries were aligned to English CT-report snippets rather
than MRI findings.  The fact that the same queries still localize the
correct organs on MRI/US supports the interpretation that A$^{2}$MoE
learns a \emph{modality-agnostic} anatomical prior rather than a
CT-specific texture model.
 \item \textbf{Cross-anatomy transfer.} GEPAR3D~\cite{szczepanski2025gepar3d} evaluates fine-grained
multi-class dental segmentation in cone-beam CT, an anatomy that is
\emph{absent} from the pre-training cohort and the histogram class
list (which targets abdominal and pelvic structures).
A$^{2}$MoE nevertheless improves over the SegMamba baseline by
$+0.8$ DSC and matches or exceeds modality-matched 3D pre-training
baselines, indicating that the encoder retains capacity for transfer
to anatomies outside the routing prior.
 \item \textbf{Out-of-distribution robustness.} The downstream cohorts used in this study cover heterogeneous
acquisition conditions: AMOS spans multiple scanners and slice
thicknesses; PANORAMA aggregates seven contributing centers from two
continents; MRISegmenter spans four imaging phases per patient;
RATIC~\cite{rudie2024rsna} consists of 4{,}274 trauma studies from 23
institutions across 14 countries; and AbdomenAtlas~3.0 aggregates 17
previously released public CT datasets.  None of these datasets
contributes to A$^{2}$MoE pre-training. The consistent improvements
reported in Tables~\ref{tab:segmentationbenchmark},
\ref{tab:ratic_binary_compact}, \ref{tab:ratic_per_grade}, and
\ref{tab:detection_results} therefore reflect generalization across
sites, scanners, and protocols, not in-distribution memorization.
 \item \textbf{Multi-task transfer.}
Although the pre-training loss couples segmentation, presence
prediction, and contrastive alignment, A$^{2}$MoE also generalizes to
downstream \emph{classification} (binary trauma $0$ versus $\{1,2\}$ and per-grade
$0$ versus $1$, $0$ versus $2$ on RATIC; Tables~\ref{tab:ratic_binary_compact}--\ref{tab:ratic_per_grade}) and
\emph{detection} (per-organ tumor detection split by lesion size on
AbdomenAtlas~3.0; Table~\ref{tab:detection_results}). On RATIC, the same
backbone improves binary mean ROC-AUC from 86.5 (Merlin) to 88.2
without organ-specific fine-tuning of the encoder, and on
AbdomenAtlas~3.0 it dominates sensitivity for clinically critical
$\leq2$~cm lesions across  pancreatic, kidney, and liver tumors. Together,
these results support the claim that the anatomical prior introduced
by A$^{2}$MoE is broadly useful, rather than a segmentation-specific
inductive bias.
\end{enumerate}
