\section{Limitations and future work}
\label{sec:appendixlimitations}
\begin{enumerate}
 \item \textbf{Anatomical scope.}
The histogram and the per-stage class-to-expert affinity matrix
$\mathbf{A}^{(s)}$ are defined over $C-1=14$ abdominal and pelvic
classes, with $C=15$ including background. The pre-training labels themselves are
\emph{not} raw automatic segmentations: TotalSegmentator outputs and
LLM-decomposed report snippets are subsequently revised by
experienced radiologists, as detailed in
Appendix~\ref{sec:appendixdatapreprocessing}. The remaining limitation
is therefore one of \emph{coverage} rather than label quality: anatomies
outside the 14-class taxonomy are routed using the residual visual
context only and do not benefit from the structural prior.
Whole-body, head--neck, musculoskeletal, and pediatric imaging are
likely to require either a richer class taxonomy or hierarchical
routing priors.
 \item \textbf{Modality and language scope.}
Pre-training is performed on adult multi-phase CT paired with
English-language reports. Transfer to MRI, ultrasound, and dental CBCT
is supported by our results, but A$^{2}$MoE has not been pre-trained
with non-English reports, T2/diffusion-weighted
MRI, X-ray, PET, or echocardiographic videos.
The mask-free OQMSP architecture is modality-agnostic by construction,
so extending pre-training to additional modalities is a natural
direction but is not demonstrated here.
 \item \textbf{Backbone and decoder choice.}
We instantiate A$^{2}$MoE on top of a SegMamba-style 3D
encoder~\cite{xing2024segmamba} with a UNETR-style
decoder~\cite{hatamizadeh2022unetr}. The histogram router and OQMSP
are designed to be backbone-agnostic and the gating equation makes no
assumption about the operator inside each expert (we use bottleneck
$1{\times}1{\times}1$ convolutions). Verifying that the same gains
hold for purely convolutional, transformer-only, or state-space-only
backbones is left to future work.
 \item \textbf{Compute and reproducibility.}
Pre-training A$^{2}$MoE requires non-trivial compute and access to one
proprietary CT cohort in addition to the public Merlin~\cite{blankemeier2026merlin}, and CancerVerse~\cite{li2026cancerverse} releases. We mitigate the latter by
(i) reporting the public-only ablations needed for reproducibility,
(ii) providing all hyperparameters and pre-processing details in
Appendix~\ref{sec:experimentalsetup}, and (iii) committing to release
checkpoints, pre-processed metadata, and training code on acceptance.
Reproducing the full proprietary-data results is, however, contingent
on data-use agreements that are outside the authors' control.
 \item \textbf{Clinical readiness.}
A$^{2}$MoE is a representation-learning method evaluated on
retrospective benchmarks. It has not been validated prospectively, has
not undergone reader studies, and is not approved as a medical device.
Its results should not be used to guide clinical decisions.
\end{enumerate}
\textbf{Future directions.} Our findings open several concrete directions:
(i)~scaling the histogram taxonomy beyond 14 classes via hierarchical
or learned anatomical ontologies, with the goal of native whole-body
support;
(ii)~hybrid token/patch routing that recovers fine-grained
specialization when crops contain many organs in similar proportions;
(iii)~longitudinal modeling that pairs the per-organ embeddings of
OQMSP across time points to support follow-up and treatment-response
reasoning;
(iv)~extension to non-CT pre-training corpora (MRI, ultrasound,
PET/CT) to test whether the same anatomy-activated prior emerges natively
in those modalities;
(v)~replacement of the SegMamba backbone with alternative 3D encoders
to confirm backbone independence;
and (vi)~prospective and human-in-the-loop validation on triage and
reporting workflows, paired with calibration and uncertainty
estimation, to bridge the gap between retrospective benchmark
performance and clinical utility.
