\section{Impact statement}
\label{sec:appendiximpactstatement}
\label{sec:appendiximpactstatement}
\begin{enumerate}
 \item \textbf{Contribution.} A$^{2}$MoE contributes a 3D medical vision--language pre-training
framework that combines anatomy-activated mixture-of-experts routing with
mask-free organ-query alignment and multi-task supervision. The
intended scientific advance is a more structured, scalable use of
imaging--report pairs for learning representations that support
segmentation, classification, and detection, with explicit modeling
of organ-specific evidence rather than purely global image--text
matching. The approach is mask-free at inference, transfers across
modalities (CT, MRI, ultrasound) and anatomies, and reaches
state-of-the-art performance on external benchmarks, providing a
reusable building block for future medical foundation models.
 \item \textbf{Clinical impact.}
If validated and integrated thoughtfully, the representations learned
by A$^{2}$MoE could support \emph{assistive} radiology workflows:
faster contouring and quality control for segmentation-heavy planning,
triage and worklist prioritization when paired with calibrated
uncertainty, retrieval of similar prior studies, teaching tools that
link images to report language, and research pipelines that reduce
manual annotation burden. Because the model exposes an organ-indexed
soft-localization map per organ via OQMSP cross-attention, downstream
clinical tools can show \emph{which} regions support a given finding,
although the
interpretability and clinical usefulness of these maps require dedicated validation \cite{chrabaszcz2025aggregated}. These benefits
are contingent on rigorous local evaluation, regulatory pathways where
applicable, and continued human responsibility for diagnosis and
treatment decisions.
 \item \textbf{Negative impact.} A$^{2}$MoE inherits limitations common to medical machine-learning systems and can produce confident errors, particularly under distribution shift, in patients with atypical anatomy, or in pediatric or pregnant populations. It may encode and amplify biases present
in who is scanned, how findings are documented, and which sites and
scanners dominate the training data, and it has not been audited for
fairness across demographic, scanner, or protocol strata. Without
careful governance, misuse could include over-reliance on automation,
privacy risks from mishandling of patient data, deployment to
populations underrepresented in training, or use as a stand-alone
diagnostic tool. We therefore emphasize that this work is a
methodological contribution, not a clinical product, and that any
real-world deployment must be preceded by prospective validation,
robustness and fairness auditing, clinician oversight, transparent
limitation reporting, and full compliance with ethics review,
de-identification standards, and data-use agreements.
\end{enumerate}
