\section*{Abstract}
\noindent Access to echocardiography is often limited, delaying recognition of progressive aortic stenosis (AS). A brief, low-cost physiological measurement obtained during routine care could help identify patients who may warrant earlier imaging. Here, we present PulseAS, integrating deep photoplethysmography (PPG) representations, waveform biomarkers and clinical information for high-grade AS screening. Three complementary analyses assessed (1) validation and transfer across cohorts differing in geography and ethnic composition, (2) score-based enrichment of AS and pre-transcatheter aortic valve implantation (TAVI) cases, and (3) preoperative and postoperative pulse changes. Using 15-s recordings, separate patient-level cross-validation in EDU (n = 1,209) and MIMIC-III (n = 2,970) yielded AUROCs of 0.818 (PulseAS--XGB) and 0.838 (PulseAS--L2), respectively. In external validation from MIMIC-III to EDU, PulseAS--XGB achieved an AUROC of 0.716, improving to 0.813 after local adaptation. The highest-scoring 10\
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:1}
\noindent Aortic stenosis (AS) can remain clinically silent and asymptomatic while pressure overload already drives progressive myocardial hypertrophy, fibrosis and scar, which may persist after valve replacement and worsen long-term outcomes\cite{ESC2025,Everett2018Fibrosis,Thornton2024Scar}. The EARLY TAVR trial supports early transcatheter intervention in asymptomatic severe AS, showing a reduction in the composite of death, stroke or unplanned cardiovascular hospitalization\cite{EARLYTAVR}. RECOVERY and AVATAR, including their long-term follow-up, provide complementary evidence for early surgery in selected patients with asymptomatic severe AS\cite{RECOVERY2020,RECOVERY10Year2026,AVATAR2022,AVATARLongTerm2024}. Early detection of severe AS is therefore increasingly important for timely surveillance and intervention.
However, translating earlier recognition into practice requires more accessible assessment. Echocardiography remains the reference standard for determining valve severity and cardiac consequences, but its dependence on specialized equipment and trained personnel limits routine use, screening, and repeated evaluation\cite{ESC2025}. In Hong Kong, echocardiography waiting times exceeding 68 weeks have been reported as a barrier to diagnosis and monitoring\cite{HAHeartFailure2024}. Similar delays have been reported in Waikato, New Zealand, where urgent echocardiography patients waited 3--4 months and semi-urgent patients about a year\cite{RNZ2025EchoWait}. In low- and middle-income settings, limited equipment and specialist availability restrict access to echocardiography, with advanced assessments often confined to referral centres\cite{GlobalHeart2025Access,CURLS2019}. These constraints highlight the need for more scalable tools that can support risk stratification for high-grade aortic stenosis and prioritise patients for earlier confirmatory echocardiographic assessment.
Accessible physiological signals can extend cardiac assessment beyond imaging. Recent studies have shown that electrocardiograms (ECGs) can detect structural heart disease and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, highlighting diagnostic information in routinely acquired signals\cite{EchoNext,HCMECGImages}. Photoplethysmography (PPG), recorded by pulse oximeters and wearable devices, captures pulsatile changes in peripheral blood volume\cite{PPGDLReview}. Pulse timing, width and amplitude reflect the interaction of ventricular ejection with the arterial circulation\cite{pyPPG,SVA2024}. The delayed arterial upstroke of AS therefore suggests a quantifiable peripheral pulse expression of valvular obstruction\cite{ASWave2023}. The broader applications in blood-pressure estimation, vascular ageing and cardiovascular screening further demonstrate the physiological information available from optical pulse recordings\cite{SIFPBPNet,SmartwatchHeartAge,PpgAge,MobilePPGOLED}. Whether this information can identify clinically important stenosis and reveal treatment-related changes remains to be determined.
PPG-based studies have begun to establish this potential. PASS evaluated fingertip PPG for AS screening\cite{PASS}, PiLA introduced physiology-guided self-supervised learning for AS\cite{PiLA}, and CardioPPG used ECG-guided representation learning to identify cardiovascular conditions including mitral and aortic valve disease\cite{CardioPPG}. Advances in PPG and multimodal foundation models offer further opportunities to combine learned representations with explicitly defined waveform biomarkers\cite{PaPaGei,PulsePPG,AnyPPG,CSFM}. Yet the clinical value of a pulse-derived score depends on more than classification performance. Because PPG also reflects age, blood pressure, vascular properties and recording conditions\cite{SVA2024,WristSQI}, it is essential to determine what information it adds to routine clinical assessment, whether that information transfers across populations and institutions, and how it relates to observable physiological change. These questions link the development of screening algorithms to the identification of clinically informative peripheral pulse phenotypes.
Valve intervention provides a particularly informative setting in which to examine this physiological relevance. By relieving valvular obstruction and altering ventricular loading, intervention allows pulse morphology to be studied alongside a defined change in cardiovascular physiology\cite{ESC2025,ASWave2023}. A pilot study linking wrist PPG features to invasively measured filling-pressure categories in patients undergoing transcatheter aortic valve implantation (TAVI) has provided initial evidence of a relationship between optical pulse measurements and intracardiac haemodynamics\cite{Scholte}. Comparing recordings before and after intervention can extend this evidence from between-group differences to changes over the treatment course. Together with cross-sectional morphology and external validation, such observations can determine whether PPG captures both disease-related predictive information and a measurable response to intervention—complementary properties of a candidate digital biomarker for screening and longitudinal assessment.
Here, we introduce PulseAS, an integrative PPG framework for AS assessment. It combines deep PPG representations, waveform-derived biomarkers and routine clinical information to investigate the peripheral pulse phenotype of high-grade AS (Fig. \ref{fig:1}). The clinical--PPG implementations use XGBoost gradient-boosted trees (PulseAS--XGB), L2-regularised logistic regression (PulseAS--L2) or a multilayer perceptron (PulseAS--MLP). We analyzed 15-s recordings from the Electro-Diagnostic Unit (EDU) cohort and Medical Information Mart for Intensive Care III (MIMIC-III). AS classifiers were developed and internally validated separately within each cohort using patient-level nested cross-validation. In a separate analysis, models developed in MIMIC-III underwent direct external validation in EDU, followed by local adaptation and evaluation on the same held-out test set. High-grade AS, defined as disease more severe than moderate, is the primary screening target.
We show that short-duration PPG captures multidimensional AS-associated pulse patterns and adds predictive information beyond routine clinical variables. Higher-scoring strata concentrate both high-grade AS and pre-TAVI cases, linking the predictions to clinically relevant disease and intervention histories. Crucially, perioperative recordings further reveal earlier systolic peaks, narrower systolic contours and higher diastolic-to-systolic width ratios after valve intervention, providing physiological corroboration alongside disease classification. Discrimination in the external cohort and further improvement after local adaptation extend the predictive evidence beyond the development setting. Together, these findings provide a physiologically supported foundation for scalable PPG-based AS pre-screening and identify peripheral pulse morphology as a candidate measure for repeated assessment after diagnosis. This approach opens a path towards pulse-informed follow-up that could help clinicians recognize patients who warrant earlier echocardiographic reassessment.
