\subsection{PPG reveals delayed systolic pulse development in AS}\label{sec:2.2}
\noindent Population waveforms and quantitative morphology features provided complementary descriptions of the AS-associated pulse phenotype. As shown in Fig. \ref{fig:3}a,b, AS-positive groups had later systolic peaks, with more pronounced waveform separation in EDU than in MIMIC-III. The waveform landmarks underlying the representative morphology features are illustrated in Supplementary Fig. \ref{fig:S1}. Waveform summaries are shown with pointwise 95\
To test whether the AS-associated pulse phenotype changed after valve intervention, we compared preoperative and postoperative recordings. In paired recordings, postoperative median pulses peaked earlier and had narrower systolic contours (Fig. \ref{fig:3}c,f). The feature-level analysis likewise showed decreases in Tsp and Tsw33 and increases in Au/Asp and Tdw10/Tsw10, consistent with attenuation of AS-associated pulse abnormalities. Across available observations, preoperative and postoperative medians were 0.267 and 0.210 s for Tsp (25th percentile, P25), 0.167 and 0.133 s for Tsw33 (75th percentile, P75), and 2.151 and 2.707 for Tdw10/Tsw10 (median), respectively. Tsp/Tpi also decreased, indicating earlier peaking relative to the pulse interval (Fig. \ref{fig:3}h).
Nine of the ten representative longitudinal features differed between preoperative and postoperative stages in the full-data analysis (all q \textless{} 0.01). At follow-up, Au/Asp, Tsp and Tdw10/Tsw10 remained different from preoperative values (all q \textless{} 0.05), while no postoperative-to-follow-up differences were detected among the ten features. The attenuation of AS-associated pulse features after TAVI provides intervention-based physiological support for the disease-associated phenotype.
