\subsection{Clinical and PPG data discriminate AS across patient groups}\label{sec:2.3}
\noindent To assess the predictive relevance of these pulse patterns, we compared morphology-based models, pretrained PPG representations and their combinations with clinical variables with classifiers developed and internally evaluated separately in each cohort using patient-level nested cross-validation (Table \ref{tab:2}). Both forms of PPG information supported discrimination: morphology-based models achieved areas under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROCs) of 0.801 in EDU and 0.767 in MIMIC-III, while models using frozen AnyPPG representations achieved 0.803 and 0.778, respectively (Fig. \ref{fig:4}a,c).
Combining clinical and PPG information yielded the highest AUROC and average precision (AP) point estimates among the eight strategies. In EDU, PulseAS--XGB achieved an AUROC of 0.818 (95\
Across age, sex and recorded blood-pressure strata, the selected models retained discrimination, with AUROC point estimates ranging from 0.750 to 0.880 in EDU (Fig. \ref{fig:4}e,f,i,j) and from 0.786 to 0.863 in MIMIC-III (Fig. \ref{fig:4}g,h,k,l). Finer age stratification revealed no monotonic pattern in EDU and decreasing AUROC point estimates in MIMIC-III. The youngest MIMIC-III group contained only two AS cases (Supplementary Fig. \ref{fig:S4}).
AS discrimination was higher when comparison patients had no more than mild disease in every assessed valve lesion. In this sensitivity analysis, AUROC increased from 0.818 to 0.834 for PulseAS--XGB in EDU and from 0.838 to 0.897 for PulseAS--L2 in MIMIC-III (Supplementary Table \ref{tab:S1}).
