\subsection{Incremental value of PPG beyond clinical predictors}\label{sec:2.4}
\noindent Matched L2 comparisons quantified the contribution of PPG beyond age, sex and systolic and diastolic blood pressure while holding the classifier, patients and validation folds constant. Adding PPG increased AUROC from 0.744 to 0.801 in EDU and from 0.760 to 0.837 in MIMIC-III, corresponding to increments of 0.057 and 0.077, respectively (Fig. \ref{fig:4}b,d; Supplementary Table \ref{tab:S2}).
This direction of change extended across all five clinical baselines incorporating blood pressure with or without age and sex. For pulse pressure alone, adding PPG increased AUROC from 0.578 to 0.767 in EDU and from 0.640 to 0.811 in MIMIC-III. The direction of change was preserved after complete-case refitting and allowance for nonlinear clinical effects, with AUROC point estimates favouring the inclusion of PPG in both settings. In MIMIC-III, the increments beyond the four-variable baseline were 0.092 after complete-case refitting and 0.089 with nonlinear clinical terms. The corresponding EDU estimates were 0.068 and 0.076. Paired estimates and confidence intervals are reported in Supplementary Table \ref{tab:S2}. These comparisons support PPG as a complementary source of predictive information alongside routinely measured clinical variables.
