\section{Discussion}\label{sec:3}
\noindent PulseAS integrates deep PPG representations, waveform-derived biomarkers and routine clinical information to identify high-grade AS and characterise pulse changes around valve intervention. Disease-associated morphology, matched clinical comparisons and external validation provide complementary evidence for the information contained in short-duration PPG. Perioperative recordings extend this evidence to changes after treatment. Building on previous PPG-based valve-disease classification and representation learning\cite{PASS,PiLA,CardioPPG}, these findings link AS identification to an observable peripheral pulse phenotype and support accessible tools for pre-screening patients for confirmatory echocardiography and for repeated physiological assessment.
\noindent\needspace{3\baselineskip}
\noindent\textbf{Incremental value of PPG beyond routine clinical variables}\par
\noindent The matched clinical comparisons assessed the information contributed by PPG beyond routinely measured patient characteristics. Holding the classifier, patients and validation folds constant separated the addition of PPG from changes in modelling strategy. Higher AUROC point estimates after adding PPG were retained across alternative blood-pressure-based predictors, complete-case refitting and nonlinear clinical models, with more precise evidence for an increment in MIMIC-III. These findings support complementary predictive information beyond the measured demographic and blood-pressure variables. Importantly, these clinical parameters are routinely and easily obtained by paramedical staff during follow-up visits and can potentially be acquired by patients themselves using widely available devices. Unlike auscultation or ECG, which typically require dedicated trained personnel and specialised equipment, this combination of simple clinical variables and short-duration PPG offers a scalable approach that could facilitate risk stratification and earlier disease detection outside conventional cardiology settings. PPG could therefore augment routine clinical assessment with waveform information to support earlier AS detection and closer monitoring.
\noindent\needspace{3\baselineskip}
\noindent\textbf{AS-associated peripheral pulse morphology and perioperative changes}\par
\noindent Population waveforms and quantitative morphology features identified an AS-associated pattern involving pulse timing, systolic width and amplitude relationships. The later systolic peaking observed in both cohorts is consistent with the delayed arterial upstroke of AS\cite{ASWave2023}. Because PPG records the peripheral consequences of ventricular ejection and arterial transmission, this pattern reflects the interaction of cardiac and vascular physiology rather than a direct measurement of valve anatomy\cite{pyPPG,SVA2024}. The involvement of multiple waveform domains supports a multidimensional description of the peripheral expression of AS. Paired perioperative recordings extended this assessment to within-patient changes. Postoperative median pulses peaked earlier and showed narrower systolic contours, while longitudinal feature analysis revealed a higher diastolic-to-systolic width ratio. These changes were directionally consistent with the differences observed between comparison patients and those with high-grade AS in both cohorts. Although the incremental AUROC from adding PPG to routine clinical parameters was modest—likely reflecting partial overlap between waveform and clinical features—the clear, directionally consistent perioperative pulse changes provide strong physiological support for PPG-based AS identification and a basis for evaluating morphology during perioperative assessment and subsequent follow-up after TAVI.
\noindent\textbf{Potential for score-guided screening}\par
\noindent A PPG-assisted screening pathway could use PulseAS scores to prioritise patients for echocardiography. Supporting this approach, the highest-scoring tenth of each cohort contained more than two-fifths of its high-grade AS cases. In EDU, this stratum also captured more than half of the pre-TAVI cases. This concentration indicates that model scores relate both to disease severity and to recorded proximity to the need for intervention. Although the primary analysis included moderate AS and concomitant valve disease---placing the assessment in a clinically heterogeneous population---the intended screening target remains AS more severe than moderate. The present results therefore support evaluation of score-guided referral for confirmatory imaging, rather than using the score itself to determine an indication for valve intervention. Future prospective studies should establish the balance between case detection, missed disease, and the resulting echocardiographic workload.
\noindent\textbf{Cross-institutional transfer and adaptation}\par
\noindent Evaluation in Hong Kong and Boston assessed model transfer across institutions with different patient populations and clinical practices. The predominantly Asian EDU cohort and predominantly White MIMIC-III population span echocardiography attendance and critical care, providing geographically, demographically and clinically distinct settings. Source-trained PulseAS--XGB retained AS discrimination in EDU without local adaptation, and adaptation using 20\
\noindent\needspace{3\baselineskip}
\noindent\textbf{Limitations}\par
\noindent First, incomplete quantitative echocardiographic characterisation limits interpretation of the scores as measures of haemodynamic severity. Although the current evidence supports high-grade AS identification, a continuous relationship with valve obstruction and its cardiac consequences has not been established. Time-matched measurements of aortic valve area, peak velocity, mean gradient, stroke-volume index and ventricular structure and function, together with model-level attribution, would help link pulse features and learned representations to specific cardiac phenotypes. Previous work generating tissue Doppler waveforms from surface ECGs illustrates the value of paired physiological signals and imaging for such investigations\cite{ECGTDI}. The morphology findings provide physiological corroboration without resolving the mechanisms represented by the learned features.
Second, the longitudinal analysis was limited by the small follow-up sample and the absence of synchronised haemodynamic measurements. Changes in blood pressure, rhythm and perioperative treatment may accompany valve intervention, limiting attribution of the observed pulse changes to relief of obstruction alone. Continuous wearable PPG synchronised with invasive arterial pressure waveforms and procedural events could characterise the temporal correspondence between peripheral pulse changes and haemodynamic responses. Pilot work combining wrist PPG with invasive filling-pressure measurements supports the feasibility of this approach\cite{Scholte}. Repeated postoperative imaging and PPG, and longer follow-up in patients with established AS, would help distinguish immediate circulatory responses from longer-term adaptation and evaluate the value of individual pulse trajectories for prompting earlier reassessment.
Third, although the two-cohort design and external validation broaden the evidence base, both populations were hospital-based, and transfer was assessed in one source-to-target direction using retrospective partitions. Prospective multicentre studies should include more high-grade AS cases, expand demographic representation and evaluate both direct transfer and local adaptation in new patients. Differences in ethnic composition do not establish equivalent performance across all groups, and community screening requires evaluation in its intended population. Translation from fingertip recordings to ambulatory wearable monitoring will also require device- and site-specific assessment of waveform fidelity, signal quality and repeatability\cite{WristSQI} to establish the conditions and thresholds under which PPG can contribute to screening and follow-up.
\noindent\textbf{Conclusion}\par
\noindent Short-duration PPG combined with routine clinical data improved discrimination of high-grade AS and concentrated high-grade AS in higher AS-score strata. External discrimination and further gains with local adaptation support the potential of clinical–PPG models for AS-score stratification in new clinical populations, warranting prospective evaluation of their utility in prioritizing echocardiography.
\section{Methods}\label{sec:4}
\subsection{Study cohorts and reference standards}\label{sec:4.1}
\noindent Fingertip PPG and clinical data were obtained from Prince of Wales Hospital, Hong Kong (EDU), and MIMIC-III, linking its matched waveforms to ECHO-NOTE2NUM annotations\cite{MIMIC,MIMICWave,EchoNotes}. EDU recruitment included patients attending echocardiography and was not restricted to suspected AS. PPG and blood pressure were acquired during the same visit. Ethical approval was obtained from the Joint Chinese University of Hong Kong--New Territories East Cluster Clinical Research Ethics Committee (2026.578). Analyses used de-identified data.
AS grades were obtained from clinical echocardiography reports and classified according to the American Society of Echocardiography guidelines\cite{ASEAS2017}. High-grade AS comprised moderate-to-severe or severe disease in EDU. MIMIC-III used each patient’s highest valid historical AS grade, with severe disease classified as positive. Comparison patients had AS no greater than moderate, with concomitant valve disease retained. Patients with unknown AS grades were excluded. Unknown non-AS grades remained missing, and pulmonary valve grades were unavailable in MIMIC-III.
\subsection{PPG acquisition and representations}\label{sec:4.2}
\noindent EDU recordings were acquired at 60 Hz using a CONTEC CMS50E. Eligible 15-s segments required finite, nonconstant signals, stable amplitude and no contact-loss or abrupt-discontinuity artefacts, usable morphology features and a mean NeuroKit2 template-matching quality index \ensuremath{\geq}0.90\cite{NeuroKit}. MIMIC-III PPG recordings (PLETH channel) at 125 Hz were screened using MIMIC-III-Ext-PPG: all three 10-s sub-windows of each 30-s candidate had to pass\cite{MIMICWave,ExtPPG}. Accepted recordings were divided into 15-s segments, retaining at most 20 per patient by label-blind selection.
The 102 pyPPG variables were summarised within segments by mean, median, 25th and 75th percentiles, and interquartile range, yielding 510 morphology features\cite{pyPPG}. Adding standard deviation, skewness, kurtosis and median absolute deviation yielded 918 predictive candidates. Unreliable values were treated as missing. Inputs to the pretrained, frozen AnyPPG encoder were resampled to 125 Hz and standardised to zero mean and unit variance\cite{AnyPPG}. Training-fold selection retained ten features, at most one per morphology domain, ranked by \(r_j=\lvert\operatorname{AUROC}(f_j,y)-0.5\rvert\) for feature \(f_j\) and outcome \(y\).
Within PulseAS, frozen AnyPPG representations, selected morphology features and clinical variables were concatenated:
\begingroup
\setlength{\abovedisplayskip}{4pt plus 1pt minus 1pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{4pt plus 1pt minus 1pt}
\setlength{\abovedisplayshortskip}{2pt plus 1pt minus 1pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayshortskip}{2pt plus 1pt minus 1pt}
\postdisplaypenalty=10000
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fusion}
\mathbf z_{\mathrm{PPG}}=\left[f_{\theta_0}(x_{10});\,\mathbf m\right],
\qquad
\mathbf z_{\mathrm{clinical+PPG}}=\left[\mathbf z_{\mathrm{PPG}};\,\mathbf c\right].
\end{equation}
\endgroup
Here, \(f_{\theta_0}(x_{10})\in\mathbb R^{512}\) encodes the central 10-s window, \(\mathbf m\in\mathbb R^{10}\) contains training selected morphology features, and \(\mathbf c\) contains clinical variables and missingness indicators. Brackets denote concatenation.
\subsection{Model development and internal validation}\label{sec:4.3}
\noindent Model development and nested internal validation were conducted separately within EDU and MIMIC-III, with classifier fitting and data-dependent preprocessing confined to the training patients of the corresponding cohort. Eight strategies used morphology, AnyPPG representations, clinical inputs or their combinations, with ExtraTrees, XGBoost, L2-regularised logistic regression or a multilayer-perceptron head (Table~\ref{tab:2})\cite{ExtraTrees,XGBoost}. Clinical models used demographic and haemodynamic variables with missingness indicators. Patients with documented AF were retained in the analyses, but AF status and its missingness indicator were not used as predictors. MIMIC-III segments received equal total weight per patient. Segment scores were averaged within each patient before Platt calibration:
\begingroup
\setlength{\abovedisplayskip}{4pt plus 1pt minus 1pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{4pt plus 1pt minus 1pt}
\setlength{\abovedisplayshortskip}{2pt plus 1pt minus 1pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayshortskip}{2pt plus 1pt minus 1pt}
\postdisplaypenalty=10000
\begin{equation}\label{eq:patient-score}
\bar s_i=\frac{1}{m_i}\sum_{k=1}^{m_i}s_{ik},
\qquad
\hat p_i=\operatorname{logit}^{-1}\!\left(a+b\bar s_i\right).
\end{equation}
\endgroup
Here, \(m_i\) counts eligible segments and \(s_{ik}\) is segment \(k\)'s pre-calibration score. EDU scores came directly from patient-level features.
Nested validation used five patient-grouped outer folds and three inner folds shared across strategies, keeping all records from each patient together\cite{NestedCV}. Calibration parameters \(a\) and \(b\) were fitted using inner-training OOF patient scores\cite{Platt}. Feature selection, imputation, scaling and tuning used only training patients. Model selection prioritised AUROC, with AP resolving ties. Thresholds maximised sensitivity subject to training specificity \ensuremath{\geq}60\
\subsection{Clinical comparisons and score stratification}\label{sec:4.4}
\noindent Matched L2 models compared five clinical baselines with and without PPG using identical patients and folds: DBP; pulse pressure (SBP\ensuremath{-}DBP); age plus either pressure measure; and age, sex, SBP and DBP. Sensitivity analyses refitted patients with observed age, sex, SBP and DBP and used restricted cubic splines with training-set knots at the 10th, 50th and 90th percentiles.
Selected-model OOF analyses stratified age, sex, observed SBP (\textless{}130/\ensuremath{\geq}130 mmHg), DBP (\textless{}80/\ensuremath{\geq}80 mmHg) and missing measurements. The SBP and DBP cutoffs correspond to the respective thresholds for hypertension in the 2017 American College of Cardiology/American Heart Association (ACC/AHA) guideline\cite{Whelton2018ACC2017}. Exploratory age bands were \textless{}50, 50--64, 65--74 and \ensuremath{\geq}75 years. For the restricted-control sensitivity analysis, comparison patients were required to have complete grading and no more than mild disease in all eight assessed valve lesions in EDU or all six in MIMIC-III. MIMIC-III used the highest valid historical grade for each lesion. All AS cases were retained, and performance was evaluated using the original OOF predictions and screening thresholds.
Patients were ranked retrospectively by PulseAS score, the calibrated OOF probability from each cohort’s highest-AUROC implementation (PulseAS--XGB in EDU and PulseAS--L2 in MIMIC-III), with higher scores indicating greater likelihood of high-grade AS. For \(N\) patients with \(E\) cases, the highest-scoring fraction \(f\) selected \(n_f\) patients and \(E_f\) cases:
\begingroup
\setlength{\abovedisplayskip}{4pt plus 1pt minus 1pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{4pt plus 1pt minus 1pt}
\setlength{\abovedisplayshortskip}{2pt plus 1pt minus 1pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayshortskip}{2pt plus 1pt minus 1pt}
\postdisplaypenalty=10000
\begin{equation}\label{eq:enrichment}
\operatorname{Capture}(f)=\frac{E_f}{E},
\qquad
\operatorname{Enrichment}(f)=\frac{E_f/n_f}{E/N}.
\end{equation}
\endgroup
Counts were rounded up with boundary ties included in \(n_f\). The case proportion in the selected group was \(E_f/n_f\). AS and pre-TAVI analyses used their respective case totals. EDU pre-TAVI cases required registry-confirmed procedures and pre-procedure index PPG. Pre-TAVI status was neither an input nor a prediction target. Capture for cases linked to later postoperative records used that subset’s denominator.
\subsection{Waveform and longitudinal analyses}\label{sec:4.5}
\noindent Complete pulses were onset-aligned, baseline-shifted and amplitude-normalised without time warping. Second derivatives used seconds-based units and the same amplitude scaling. Pointwise medians were aggregated across pulses, segments and patients on a 16.7-ms grid. Template points required \ensuremath{\geq}50\
For MIMIC-III, features were first median-aggregated within patients. Cross-sectional comparisons used asymptotic, continuity-corrected two-sided Mann--Whitney U tests and Cliff's delta, without imputation or outlier removal\cite{Cliff}.
\noindent\begin{minipage}[t]{\linewidth}
For AS feature \(y_{it}\) in patient \(i\) at stage \(t\), preTAVI--postTAVI differences used a patient random-intercept model:
\begingroup
\setlength{\abovedisplayskip}{4pt plus 1pt minus 1pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{4pt plus 1pt minus 1pt}
\setlength{\abovedisplayshortskip}{2pt plus 1pt minus 1pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayshortskip}{2pt plus 1pt minus 1pt}
\postdisplaypenalty=10000
\begin{equation}\label{eq:longitudinal}
y_{it}=\beta_0+\beta_{\mathrm{post}}\mathbf{1}(t=\mathrm{post})+u_i+\varepsilon_{it}.
\end{equation}
\endgroup
Here, \(u_i\) is a patient random intercept, \(\varepsilon_{it}\) is residual error, and \(\beta_{\mathrm{post}}\) estimates the postoperative--preoperative difference. Models used paired and unpaired observations and were fitted by restricted maximum likelihood, with two-sided Wald tests. Patient-cluster-robust models provided sensitivity analyses. Follow-up comparisons used paired Wilcoxon tests with automatic exact/asymptotic selection.
\end{minipage}\par
Benjamini--Hochberg correction covered all 510 features within each cohort--endpoint comparison or longitudinal contrast, retaining unestimable tests in the family. Statistical significance was defined as q\ensuremath{<}0.05\cite{BH}. Descriptive reference distributions comprised EDU patients with no more than mild disease in all eight assessed lesions.
\subsection{External validation and local adaptation}\label{sec:4.6}
\noindent To assess cross-cohort generalisability, AS classifiers developed and calibrated in MIMIC-III were evaluated in EDU, using consistent definitions of clinical variables and missingness indicators. Eligible EDU patients were stratified by AS status and divided into an adaptation and validation subset (20\
We subsequently adapted the models using EDU data while retaining the features selected in MIMIC-III and the original preprocessing procedures where applicable. XGBoost models were updated by adding trees, with model selection based on EDU validation data, whereas logistic regression coefficients were updated with regularisation towards their original values. For neural fusion models, only the prediction heads were updated, with the AnyPPG encoder remaining frozen. The end-to-end waveform model underwent full-network fine-tuning, while ExtraTrees received recalibration alone. Calibration and threshold selection for the other adapted models were performed using EDU validation data under the original specificity criterion. The original and locally adapted models were evaluated on the same held-out EDU test set, which was excluded from all model development and adaptation procedures in this experiment. Pre-calibration discrimination was reported separately.
\subsection{Statistical analysis}\label{sec:4.7}
\noindent Patients were the independent analysis units. Clinical variables were summarised as medians [interquartile ranges] or available-case counts and percentages, with unadjusted two-sided Mann--Whitney U or Fisher exact tests. Performance measures were AUROC, AP, sensitivity, specificity, F1, balanced accuracy and Brier score. Reliability plots used ten approximately equal-sized groups.
