 egin{abstract}
Closed-form fractional programming (FP), the weighted minimum mean-squared error (WMMSE) algorithm, and the two surrogate-enhanced methods proposed in 2026---the surrogate-enhanced FP (SEFP) of Jiao \emph{et al.} and the XMMSE algorithm of Yang and Zhao---all maximize the weighted sum rate (WSR) by iteratively maximizing a surrogate function that is \emph{linear in the interference power} of each link. In this paper, we show that this family admits a unique best member. We propose the interference-conjugate transform (ICT), which replaces each rate term $\log(1+a/b)$ by $\max_{\kappa\ge0}[\Omega(\kappa a)-\kappa b]$, where $\Omega$ is the closed-form partial convex conjugate of the rate in the interference variable and the optimal multiplier is the classical interference price $a/(b(a+b))$. We prove that, for any fixed price, $\Omega(\kappa\,\cdot)$ is the pointwise largest own-signal function compatible with a valid interference-linear lower bound, and that tightness at an anchor point forces the price. Consequently, all four benchmark surrogates, which we show share this price and differ only in their own-signal term, are dominated pointwise by the ICT surrogate; as a by-product, we prove that the single-stream XMMSE surrogate coincides exactly with that of SEFP. At a signal-to-interference-plus-noise ratio $\gamma$, the own-signal curvature of the benchmark surrogates exceeds the true curvature by factors that behave as $\gamma/2$ (FP/WMMSE) and $(\log\gamma)/2$ (SEFP/XMMSE), against $1+1/(\gamma(\gamma+2))$ for ICT, and this ordering carries over to the certified ascent per iteration and to the asymptotic linear convergence rate. The ICT yields closed-form updates of the same per-iteration cost as the benchmarks for single-antenna power control, uplink joint scheduling and power control, and multi-cell MISO beamforming, and it yields the first solver-free algorithm for sum-least-$q$th-percentile rate maximization, whose subproblem is solved through a $K$-dimensional convex dual with closed-form gradient and Hessian and a certified duality gap. In the uplink scheduling setting of Jiao \emph{et al.}, ICT improves the proportional-fairness utility over FP by \pfICTminusFP{} and over SEFP by \pfICTminusSEFP{} (\pfICTminusSEFPwins{}/\pfNdrops{} drops) with \pfItersICT{} iterations per slot instead of \pfItersFP{} and \pfItersSEFP{}; in MISO beamforming with $M=8$ antennas, it reaches $99.9\
\end{abstract}
 egin{IEEEkeywords}
Fractional programming, minorization--maximization, weighted sum rate, interference pricing, WMMSE, scheduling, beamforming, percentile optimization, convex conjugate.
\end{IEEEkeywords}
\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{W}{eighted} sum-rate (WSR) maximization under the treatment of interference as noise is the mainstay formulation of radio resource management in interference-limited wireless networks. Power control, user scheduling and beamforming are all instances of
 egin{equation}
\max_{\mathbf x\in\mathcal X}\; \sum_{i} w_i \log\Big(1+ rac{a_i(\mathbf x)}{b_i(\mathbf x)}\Big),
\label{eq:wsr}
\end{equation}
in which $a_i$ denotes the useful signal power of link $i$ and $b_i$ its interference-plus-noise power. Problem \eqref{eq:wsr} is non-convex and NP-hard in general \cite{LuoZhang08}, and the practical state-of-the-art consists of iterative algorithms that are guaranteed to converge to stationary points. Three ingredients have dominated this class of problems for more than a decade: the WMMSE algorithm \cite{Christensen08,Shi11}, interference pricing \cite{Huang06,ShiBerryHonig09}, and the fractional programming (FP) framework of Shen and Yu \cite{ShenYuI,ShenYuII}, whose closed-form variant composes a Lagrangian dual transform (LDT) with a quadratic transform (QT). It was later recognized \cite{ShenMFP19,Zhang23,Shen24} that all of these are instances of minorization--maximization (MM) \cite{Sun17}: each iteration replaces \eqref{eq:wsr} by a surrogate function that lies below the objective, touches it at the current point, and is easy to maximize; furthermore, closed-form FP and WMMSE generate identical iterates.
Two recent papers improve the quality of the surrogate rather than the algorithmic wrapper around it. Jiao \emph{et al.} \cite{Jiao26} observe that the LDT is a tangent bound in the reciprocal coordinate $z=1/(1+r)$ of the SINR $r$ which makes it conservative, and propose the reciprocal-inversion transform (RIT), a tangent bound of the concave function $1/\log(1+1/	au)$ in the coordinate $	au=1/r$; combined with the QT, it yields the surrogate-enhanced FP (SEFP) algorithm, which retains the per-cell separability and closed-form updates of FP and consistently outperforms it in uplink joint scheduling and power control. Independently, Yang and Zhao \cite{YangZhao26} start from the exact integral relation $\log\det(\mathbf I+\mathbf X)=\int_0^1	r[(\mathbf I+\varepsilon\mathbf X)^{-1}\mathbf X]\,d\varepsilon$ between the rate and a family of regularized mean-squared errors, and derive the XMMSE algorithm, whose quadratic surrogate is provably tighter than that of WMMSE and converges faster, in particular at high SNR.
Both papers improve one specific bound and compare it with one specific benchmark. In this paper, we address the obvious question that these results invite: \emph{among all surrogates with the structure that makes the updates separable and closed form, which one is the best, and how far from it are FP, WMMSE, SEFP and XMMSE?} The structure in question is \emph{linearity in the interference power}. Interference is the only quantity that couples the links; hence, if a surrogate charges a fixed price per unit of interference, the maximization decouples into per-link subproblems, which is precisely why closed-form FP, SEFP, XMMSE and the pricing algorithms are computationally cheap. We show that this family contains a unique tightest surrogate, that it admits a closed form, that the four benchmarks are strictly dominated members of the family, and that the algorithms it yields have the same per-iteration cost as the benchmarks.
\subsection{Contributions}
The contributions of this paper can be summarized as follows.
 egin{itemize}
\item 	extbf{The interference-conjugate transform (ICT).} For $a\ge0$ and $b>0$, we establish the identity
 egin{equation*}
\log\Big(1+ rac ab\Big)=\max_{\kappa\ge0} ig[\Om(\kappa a)-\kappa b ig],\qquad \kappa^\star= rac{a}{b(a+b)},
\end{equation*}
with $\Om(u)=\log(1+hof(u))+hof(u)/(1+hof(u))$ and $hof(u)=	frac12(u+\sqrt{u^2+4u})$. $\Om$ is the partial convex conjugate of the rate in the interference variable alone, and $\kappa^\star$ is the classical interference price \cite{Huang06}. For fixed $\kappa$ the transformed objective is concave and separable in the powers, and the transformed problem is block-concave, so that alternating maximization is a block-coordinate ascent whose fixed points are the KKT points of \eqref{eq:wsr}.
\item 	extbf{Tightness (Theorem~ef{thm:tightest}).} For any fixed price $\kappa$, every function $\phi$ such that $\phi(a)-\kappa b\le\log(1+a/b)$ for all $(a,b)$ satisfies $\phi\le\Om(\kappa\,\cdot)$, and a surrogate of this form that is tight at an anchor $(a_0,b_0)$ must use $\kappa=\kappa^\star(a_0,b_0)$. Hence, ICT is the pointwise largest interference-linear minorizer, and the interference price is a consequence of tightness rather than a design choice.
\item 	extbf{One family, one price, four dominated members (Propositions~ef{prop:family} and ef{prop:xmmse}).} We show that LDT+QT (closed-form FP), WMMSE, RIT+QT (SEFP) and single-stream XMMSE are interference-linear surrogates with the common price $w_i\kappa_i^\star$, whose own-signal functions in the normalized variable $
u=a/a_0$ are
 egin{align*}
\phi_{\LQ}(
u)&=\log(1+\gamma)-\gamma+2\gamma\sqrt
u-	frac{\gamma^2}{1+\gamma}
u,
\phi_{\RQ}(
u)&=2\log(1+\gamma)\sqrt
u-	frac{b(\gamma)}{1+\gamma}
u,
\phi_{\ICT}(
u)&=\Om ig(	frac{\gamma^2}{1+\gamma}
u ig),
\end{align*}
with $b(\gamma)=(1+\gamma)\log(1+\gamma)-\gamma$, and that $\phi_{\LQ}\le\phi_{\RQ}\le\phi_{\ICT}\le\log(1+\gamma
u)+\gamma/(1+\gamma)$ with equality only at $
u=1$. Crucially, we prove that the single-stream XMMSE surrogate equals $\phi_{\RQ}$ exactly, including its complex phase structure for beamforming; hence XMMSE and SEFP are the same method for single-stream users.
\item 	extbf{Quantitative consequences (Propositions~ef{prop:curv}--ef{prop:rate}).} The own-signal curvature at the anchor exceeds the true curvature by the factors $(1+\gamma)^2/(2\gamma)$, $(1+\gamma)^2\log(1+\gamma)/(2\gamma^2)$ and $1+1/(\gamma(\gamma+2))$, respectively, and the certified ascent of one MM step and the asymptotic linear rate at a non-degenerate stationary point are ordered accordingly.
\item 	extbf{Algorithms.} We derive closed-form ICT updates for single-antenna power control, for uplink joint scheduling and power control (where the candidate score becomes $w_k\log(1+ho_k)$, the exact best response to the current prices, in place of the bounded LDT score of \cite{ShenYuII}), and for multi-cell MISO beamforming, where a rank-one price and a Cauchy--Schwarz argument reduce the per-base-station problem to an eigendecomposition and a scalar bisection. All of these have the per-iteration complexity of their FP counterparts.
\item 	extbf{A solver-free algorithm for percentile optimization (Section~ef{sec:slqp}).} For the sum-least-$q$th-percentile (SLqP) rate problem of Khan and Adve \cite{Khan24}, whose QFT and LFT subproblems require a conic solver and for which closed-form algorithms were stated to be unknown, we show that the ICT subproblem admits a $K$-dimensional convex dual with closed-form gradient and Hessian and a certified duality gap. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first solver-free algorithm for this class of problems; it reaches the same values as QFT in fewer outer iterations, and its certified subproblem solve is an order of magnitude faster than an interior-point conic solver from about 140 links upward.
\item 	extbf{Simulations.} In the settings of \cite{ShenYuI,ShenYuII,Jiao26,Khan24}, ICT reaches equal or better stationary points in one to two orders of magnitude fewer iterations than FP and in fewer iterations than SEFP/XMMSE, and under proportional-fairness scheduling its utility gain over SEFP is as large as the gain of SEFP over FP.
\end{itemize}
\subsection{Related Work, Organization and Notation}
The MM interpretation of FP and WMMSE is due to \cite{ShenMFP19,Zhang23}, and \cite{Shen24} accelerates QT/WMMSE by extrapolation, which is compatible with any choice of surrogate. Interference pricing \cite{Huang06,ShiBerryHonig09} uses the price $\kappa^\star$ with a best-response own-signal term, which is not a valid global minorizer, so that monotonicity requires additional conditions; ICT is the tightest \emph{valid} choice. SCALE \cite{Papandriopoulos09} uses the bound $\log(1+z)\ge\alpha\log z+ eta$, which is linear neither in the interference nor in the signal. The QFT and LFT of Khan and Adve \cite{Khan24} are logarithms of affine functions of the interference and require a conic solver per iteration; they lie outside the family studied here and are not pointwise comparable with ICT, but Section~ef{sec:slqp} compares with them on their own SLqP problem. We confine ourselves to single-stream users; the multi-stream $\log\det$ case is discussed in Section~ef{sec:concl}.
Section~ef{sec:prelim} formulates the problem and places the four benchmark surrogates in a common notation; Section~ef{sec:ict} introduces the ICT, proves its tightness and derives its consequences; Sections~ef{sec:siso}, ef{sec:miso} and ef{sec:slqp} apply it to SISO power control and scheduling, MISO beamforming and percentile optimization, each with a proposed approach followed by simulation results; Section~ef{sec:concl} concludes. Throughout, $\log$ denotes the natural logarithm; rates in figures are in Mbps over the system bandwidth; $\gamma=a_0/b_0$ denotes the SINR at the anchor point; and for a Hermitian matrix $\mathbf A$, $\mathbf A\succeq0$ means positive semidefinite.
\section{Preliminaries}
\label{sec:prelim}
\subsection{Problem Formulation}
We consider \eqref{eq:wsr} with $a_i,b_i$ determined by the decision variables, in three instances. \emph{SISO power control:} $K$ links, $a_i=g_{ii}p_i$, $b_i=\sum_{j
e i}g_{ij}p_j+\sigma^2$, $p_{\min}\le p_i\le P_{\max}$. \emph{Uplink joint scheduling and power control} \cite{ShenYuII,Jiao26}: cells $i\in\mathcal B$, each scheduling one user $s_i\in\mathcal K_i\cup\{\varnothing\}$ at power $p_{s_i}$; $a_i=g_{i,s_i}p_{s_i}$, $b_i=\sum_{j
e i}g_{i,s_j}p_{s_j}+\sigma^2$, with $g_{i,k}$ the uplink gain of user $k$ at base station (BS) $i$ and weight $w_{s_i}$. \emph{Multi-cell MISO beamforming} \cite{ShenYuI}: BS $j$ with $M$ antennas serves user $j$ with $\mathbf v_j\in\C^M$, $\|\mathbf v_j\|^2\le P$; $a_i=|\mathbf h_{ii}^H\mathbf v_i|^2$, $b_i=\sum_{j
e i}|\mathbf h_{ij}^H\mathbf v_j|^2+\sigma^2$, with $\mathbf h_{ij}$ the channel from BS $j$ to user $i$.
An MM algorithm \cite{Sun17} for \eqref{eq:wsr} chooses, at the current point $ ar{\mathbf x}$, a surrogate $S(\mathbf x; ar{\mathbf x})\le f(\mathbf x)$ with $S( ar{\mathbf x}; ar{\mathbf x})=f( ar{\mathbf x})$ and moves to a maximizer of $S$. The objective is then non-decreasing, and the limit points are stationary under mild conditions. For WSR problems every surrogate of interest is built term by term from a scalar minorizer of $\log(1+a/b)$ at the anchor $(a_0,b_0)$, which we refer to as the \emph{single-ratio surrogate}.
\subsection{The Four Benchmark Surrogates}
\label{sec:benchmarks}
\emph{LDT+QT (closed-form FP).} The LDT \cite{ShenYuI} is the tangent of $-\log z$ at $z_0=1/(1+\gamma)$ in the coordinate $z=1/(1+r)$, $r=a/b$:
 egin{equation}
\log(1+r)\ \ge\ \ell_{\mathrm{LDT}}(r;\gamma)	riangleq\log(1+\gamma)-\gamma+ rac{(1+\gamma)r}{1+r}.
\label{eq:ldt}
\end{equation}
The ratio $r/(1+r)=a/(a+b)$ is then decoupled by the QT \cite{ShenYuI}, $A/B=\max_y[2y\sqrt A-y^2B]$ with $y^\star=\sqrt A/B$. With $y$ fixed at its anchor value, the resulting single-ratio surrogate is
 egin{equation}
\ell^{\LQ}(a,b)=\log(1+\gamma)-\gamma+ rac{2(1+\gamma)\sqrt{a_0a}}{a_0+b_0}- rac{(1+\gamma)a_0}{(a_0+b_0)^2}(a+b).
\label{eq:lq}
\end{equation}
\emph{WMMSE.} With the MMSE receiver $u_0=\sqrt{a_0}/(a_0+b_0)$, the MSE $e(a,b)=1-2u_0\sqrt a+u_0^2(a+b)$ and the weight $	ilde w=1/e(a_0,b_0)=1+\gamma$, the WMMSE surrogate \cite{Shi11,Zhang23} is $\log	ilde w-	ilde w\,e(a,b)+1$, which equals \eqref{eq:lq} identically (this is the scalar form of the equivalence of closed-form FP and WMMSE established in \cite{Zhang23}).
\emph{RIT+QT (SEFP).} Jiao \emph{et al.} \cite{Jiao26} define $b(\gamma)=(1+\gamma)\log(1+\gamma)-\gamma$ and $c(\gamma)=(1+\gamma)\log^2(1+\gamma)$ and prove that
 egin{equation}
\log(1+r)\ \ge\ \ell_{\mathrm{RIT}}(r;\gamma)	riangleq rac{c(\gamma)\,r}{\gamma^2+b(\gamma)\,r},
\label{eq:rit}
\end{equation}
a tangent bound of the concave function $	au\mapsto1/\log(1+1/	au)$ at $	au=1/\gamma$. Applying the QT to the ratio in \eqref{eq:rit} with $y$ fixed at its anchor value gives
 egin{equation}
\ell^{\RQ}(a,b)= rac{2\log(1+\gamma)}{\sqrt{a_0}}\sqrt a- rac{b(\gamma)}{(1+\gamma)a_0}a- rac{\gamma}{(1+\gamma)b_0}\,b,
\label{eq:rq}
\end{equation}
where we have used $y^\star=\sqrt{c(\gamma)a_0}/(\gamma^2b_0+b(\gamma)a_0)=1/\sqrt{(1+\gamma)a_0}$.
\emph{XMMSE.} For a single stream, the sum-regularized-MSE surrogate of \cite{YangZhao26} is a quadratic in the complex scalar $z=\mathbf h^H\mathbf v$ (Section~ef{sec:miso}); its scalar form at the anchor $x_0=\sqrt{a_0}$, $b=b_0$, is the quadratic in the amplitude $x=\sqrt a$ given in \cite[Sec.~IV]{YangZhao26},
 egin{multline}
g_X(x)=\Big[ rac1{1+\gamma}- rac{\log(1+\gamma)}{\gamma}\Big](x-x_0)^2
+ rac{2x_0}{1+\gamma}(x-x_0)+\log(1+\gamma),
\label{eq:xmmse-scalar}
\end{multline}
to be compared with the WMMSE quadratic $g_W$, which has $1/(1+\gamma)-1$ in place of the bracket.
It is critical to recognize that all four surrogates have the form $\phi(a)-\lambda b$: they are \emph{affine in the interference}, with a price $\lambda$ that does not depend on $a$. This is the structural property that makes the per-link updates separable and closed form, and it is the family over which we optimize in the sequel.
\section{The Interference-Conjugate Transform}
\label{sec:ict}
\subsection{The Transform}
 egin{theorem}[Interference-conjugate transform]
\label{thm:ict}
Let $hof(u)=	frac12 ig(u+\sqrt{u^2+4u} ig)$ for $u\ge0$, the unique non-negative root of $hof^2/(1+hof)=u$, and define
 egin{equation}
\Om(u)=\log ig(1+hof(u) ig)+ rac{hof(u)}{1+hof(u)}.
\label{eq:omega}
\end{equation}
For every $a\ge0$ and $b>0$,
 egin{equation}
\log\Big(1+ rac ab\Big)=\max_{\kappa\ge0} ig[\Om(\kappa a)-\kappa b ig],\qquad \kappa^\star= rac{a}{b(a+b)},
\label{eq:ict}
\end{equation}
and the maximizer is unique when $a>0$. Consequently, $\Om(\kappa a)-\kappa b\le\log(1+a/b)$ for every $\kappa\ge0$.
\end{theorem}
 egin{IEEEproof}
Fix $a>0$ and $\kappa>0$, and consider $g(b)=\log(1+a/b)+\kappa b$ on $b>0$. Since $\log(1+a/b)=\log(a+b)-\log b$ has second derivative $1/b^2-1/(a+b)^2>0$, $g$ is strictly convex; it tends to $+\infty$ as $b	o0^+$ and as $b	o\infty$, so it has a unique minimizer $b^\star$, characterized by $g'(b^\star)=0$, i.e., $\kappa=a/(b^\star(a+b^\star))$. Writing $ho	riangleq a/b^\star$, this condition reads $\kappa a=ho^2/(1+ho)$, hence $ho=hof(\kappa a)$, and the minimum value is $\log(1+ho)+\kappa b^\star=\log(1+ho)+ho/(1+ho)=\Om(\kappa a)$. Hence $\Om(\kappa a)\le\log(1+a/b)+\kappa b$ for all $b>0$, with equality if and only if $b=b^\star$, i.e., if and only if $\kappa=a/(b(a+b))=\kappa^\star$. This proves the minorization and the identity \eqref{eq:ict} for $a>0$ (for $\kappa=0$ the inequality is trivial, and for $a=0$ both sides of \eqref{eq:ict} vanish at $\kappa=0$). Uniqueness of the maximizer follows from the strict concavity of $\kappa\mapsto\Om(\kappa a)$ established in Proposition~ef{prop:omega}(a) below.
\end{IEEEproof}
The identity \eqref{eq:ict} is the Fenchel--Legendre duality of the rate in the interference variable alone: $b\mapsto\log(1+a/b)$ is convex, and $\Om(\kappa a)=\inf_b[\log(1+a/b)+\kappa b]$ is the intercept of its tightest affine minorant of slope $-\kappa$. We emphasize that the own-signal variable $a$ is never approximated.
 egin{proposition}[Properties of $\Om$]
\label{prop:omega}
(a) $\Om$ is strictly increasing and strictly concave on $[0,\infty)$, $\Om(0)=0$, $\Om'(u)=1/hof(u)$ and $\Om''(u)=-(1+hof)^2/(hof^3(hof+2))$.
(b) $\Om(u)=2\sqrt u-	frac u2+	frac{u^{3/2}}{12}+O(u^2)$ as $u	o0$, and $\Om(u)=\log u+1+	frac1u+O(u^{-2})$ as $u	o\infty$.
(c) $(a,\kappa)\mapsto\Om(\kappa a)$ is jointly concave on $\R_+^2$.
(d) At the maximizer of \eqref{eq:ict}, $hof(\kappa^\star a)=a/b$, i.e., $hof$ returns the SINR.
\end{proposition}
 egin{IEEEproof}
(a) Taking $a=1$ in the proof of Theorem~ef{thm:ict}, $\Om(u)=\inf_{b>0}[\log(1+1/b)+ub]$ with minimizer $b^\star(u)=1/hof(u)$. By the envelope theorem, $\Om'(u)=b^\star(u)=1/hof(u)>0$; since $hof$ is strictly increasing, $\Om$ is strictly concave, and $\Om''=-hof'/hof^2$ with $hof'=(1+hof)^2/(hof(hof+2))$ obtained by implicit differentiation of $hof^2/(1+hof)=u$. (b) Substituting $hof(u)=\sqrt u+u/2+u^{3/2}/8+O(u^2)$ and $hof(u)=u+1-1/u+O(u^{-2})$ into \eqref{eq:omega} yields the expansions. (c) For each fixed $b>0$, $(a,\kappa)\mapsto\log(1+a/b)+\kappa b$ is concave (concave in $a$ and affine in $\kappa$), and an infimum of concave functions is concave. (d) was shown in the proof of Theorem~ef{thm:ict}, where $ho=a/b^\star$ and $b^\star=b$ at $\kappa=\kappa^\star$.
\end{IEEEproof}
Part (b) exhibits the two regimes of the ICT: at low SINR, $\Om(\kappa a)\approx2\sqrt{\kappa a}$ is the QT-type square-root bound, so that ICT and FP agree to first order; at high SINR, $\Om(\kappa a)\approx\log(\kappa a)+1$ has the logarithmic growth of the rate itself. Part (c) makes the transformed objective $\sum_iw_i[\Om(\kappa_ia_i)-\kappa_ib_i]$ jointly concave in $(\mathbf a, oldsymbol\kappa)$ and, for fixed $ oldsymbol\kappa$, concave and separable in the powers.
\subsection{Derivation and Relation to Existing Transforms}
\label{sec:derivation}
The construction has three steps, each of which is forced once the goal is stated. (i) \emph{What must be approximated.} The links of \eqref{eq:wsr} are coupled only through $b_i$; for fixed $b_i$ the rate is already concave in the own signal. Hence, the interference is the only variable that needs replacing, and approximating the own signal as well---as the QT does when it conjugates the whole denominator $a+b$ of the LDT ratio $a/(a+b)$---gives away accuracy that was never needed. (ii) \emph{The simpler form.} A function of the sum $\sum_{j
e i}g_{ij}p_j$ splits into per-link terms only if it is affine, with a slope independent of $a_i$ (otherwise bilinear terms $p_ip_j$ appear); this leads to a fixed price $\kappa_i$ per unit of interference. (iii) \emph{The tightest such form.} Since $b\mapsto\log(1+a/b)$ is convex, the tightest affine minorant of slope $-\kappa$ that is valid for \emph{every} $b$ has intercept $\inf_b[\log(1+a/b)+\kappa b]$, the partial Fenchel conjugate, and \eqref{eq:omega} is the result; the dual variable is an interference price by construction.
Three remarks place the ICT among the known transforms. First, it is the same operation as the QT, which is the partial conjugate of the ratio $A/B$ in $B$; the QT conjugates a ratio after the logarithm has been removed by a separate bound (LDT or RIT), whereas the ICT conjugates the logarithm of the ratio in one step, and one bound loses less than two composed bounds (Proposition~ef{prop:family}). Second, the closed form rests on the fact that the stationarity condition in $b$ is a quadratic equation (the origin of $\sqrt{u^2+4u}$), because the rate is the logarithm of a ratio of affine functions; for multi-stream $\log\det$ rates the analogous equation is of Riccati type and does not collapse (Remark~ef{rem:mimo}). Third, the ICT is distinct from Dinkelbach's and the Charnes--Cooper transforms, from the LDT and the RIT (tangents in $1/(1+r)$ and $1/r$), from SCALE's bound, and from the QFT/LFT of \cite{Khan24}, which lie outside the family; by Proposition~ef{prop:omega}(b), its own-signal term is the QT square root at low SINR and the logarithm at high SINR.
\subsection{Tightness}
 egin{theorem}[Tightest interference-linear minorizer]
\label{thm:tightest}
Fix $\kappa\ge0$.
(i) If $\phi:\R_+	o\R$ satisfies $\phi(a)-\kappa b\le\log(1+a/b)$ for all $a\ge0$, $b>0$, then $\phi(a)\le\Om(\kappa a)$ for all $a\ge0$.
(ii) If in addition $\phi(a_0)-\kappa b_0=\log(1+a_0/b_0)$ for some $a_0>0$, $b_0>0$, then $\kappa=\kappa^\star(a_0,b_0)$ and $\phi(a_0)=\Om(\kappa a_0)$.
\end{theorem}
 egin{IEEEproof}
(i) For fixed $a$, $\phi(a)\le\inf_{b>0}[\log(1+a/b)+\kappa b]$, and this infimum equals $\Om(\kappa a)$ by the proof of Theorem~ef{thm:ict}. (ii) From (i) and the hypothesis, $\Om(\kappa a_0)-\kappa b_0\ge\phi(a_0)-\kappa b_0=\log(1+a_0/b_0)=\max_{\kappa'}[\Om(\kappa'a_0)-\kappa'b_0]$, so that $\kappa$ attains the maximum in \eqref{eq:ict}, which is unique for $a_0>0$ by the strict concavity of $\kappa\mapsto\Om(\kappa a_0)$ (Proposition~ef{prop:omega}(a)). Hence $\kappa=\kappa^\star$, and the two inequalities are equalities.
\end{IEEEproof}
Theorem~ef{thm:tightest} says two things. First, the interference price of every tight interference-linear surrogate is forced: it must be the marginal rate loss per unit of interference, $-\partial\log(1+a/b)/\partial b=a/(b(a+b))$, the price of \cite{Huang06,ShiBerryHonig09}; that WMMSE, FP, SEFP and XMMSE, derived by unrelated routes, all charge this price is therefore explained rather than coincidental. Second, once the price is fixed, the own-signal function is bounded above by $\Om(\kappa\,\cdot)$: everything a surrogate loses relative to ICT is lost on the own-signal side. The proof is short because the construction \emph{is} the optimality argument; the content lies in the closed form \eqref{eq:omega} and in the next proposition, which places the benchmarks in the family.
