\subsection{Joint Uplink Scheduling and Power Control: Proposed Approach}
\label{sec:sched}
Shen and Yu \cite{ShenYuII} showed that the LDT+QT surrogate, after fixing the QT variables $y_i$, separates across cells into a sum over scheduled users of a reward minus the interference prices paid to the other cells, so that each BS can score its candidate users independently; Jiao \emph{et al.} \cite{Jiao26} retain this structure with RIT-induced coefficients. The ICT surrogate separates in the same way. Let $\lambda_i=w_{s_i}\kappa_i$ be the current price of cell $i$ (zero if the cell is off), and for a candidate $k\in\mathcal K_i$ let $\mu_k=\sum_{j
e i}\lambda_jg_{j,k}$ be the price it would pay. Replacing the own-signal term of cell $i$ by $w_k\Om(\lambda_ig_{i,k}p_k/w_k)$ yields a valid, separable minorizer that is tight at the current schedule (Appendix~ef{app:sched}); maximizing it over $p_k$ gives the candidate power and score
 egin{equation}
 egin{aligned}
ho_k&= rac{\lambda_ig_{i,k}}{\mu_k},\qquad
p_k=\min\Big\{P_{\max}, rac{w_kho_k^2}{(1+ho_k)\lambda_ig_{i,k}}\Big\},
\Xi_i(k)&=w_k\log(1+ho_k)
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:score}
\end{equation}
when the power is not clipped, and $\Xi_i(k)=w_k\Om(\lambda_ig_{i,k}P_{\max}/w_k)-\mu_kP_{\max}$ otherwise. Each BS then schedules $s_i=\argmax_{k\in\mathcal K_i}\Xi_i(k)$, or $\varnothing$ if all scores are non-positive.
 egin{proposition}[Structure of the three scheduling metrics]
\label{prop:sched}
With $ho_k$ as in \eqref{eq:score}, the unclipped candidate scores of closed-form FP \cite[eqs.~(31)--(33)]{ShenYuII}, SEFP \cite[eqs.~(55)--(59)]{Jiao26} and ICT are
$\Xi_i(k)=w_k\,\ell(ho_k;\gamma_i)$ with $\ell=\ell_{\mathrm{LDT}}$, $\ell_{\mathrm{RIT}}$ and $\log(1+\cdot)$, respectively, where $\gamma_i$ is the SINR of the user currently scheduled in cell $i$. Hence, the three methods charge the same prices $\lambda_i$ and differ only in $\ell$, and since $\ell_{\mathrm{LDT}}\le\ell_{\mathrm{RIT}}\le\log(1+\cdot)$, Proposition~ef{prop:ascent} applies to each scheduling step.
\end{proposition}
The proposition is proved in Appendix~ef{app:sched} by writing $y_i^2=\lambda_i$ for FP and $\alpha_i^2y_i^2=\lambda_i$ for SEFP. The ICT score is the exact best response of candidate $k$ to the current prices. In contrast, the LDT score $w_k[\log(1+\gamma_i)-\gamma_i+(1+\gamma_i)ho_k/(1+ho_k)]$ is bounded above by $w_k[\log(1+\gamma_i)+1]$ and is \emph{negative} whenever $ho_k<(\gamma_i-\log(1+\gamma_i))/(1+\log(1+\gamma_i))$ (about $17$ at $\gamma_i=20$\,dB): a candidate with a large weight but a modest $ho_k$ cannot be scheduled by FP, and if the condition holds for every candidate of a cell, FP switches the cell off for the rest of the slot. ICT and SEFP never switch a cell off, since their scores are positive for every $ho_k>0$. We show in Section~ef{sec:num-sched} that this is the mechanism behind the utility deficit of FP under proportional fairness. The ICT algorithm is Algorithm~1 of \cite{ShenYuII} with \eqref{eq:score} in place of its eqs.~(30)--(33); its complexity of $O(|\mathcal K||\mathcal B|)$ per iteration and its monotone convergence (Proposition~1 of \cite{ShenYuII}) are unchanged.
