\section{Multi-Cell MISO Beamforming}
\label{sec:miso}
\subsection{Proposed Approach}
For MISO beamforming, $a_i=|\mathbf h_{ii}^H\mathbf v_i|^2$ is quadratic and $b_i$ is a sum of quadratics in the other beamformers, so that the ICT surrogate at the anchor $\{ ar{\mathbf v}_j\}$ is
 egin{equation}
\sum_jw_j\Om ig(\kappa_j|\mathbf h_{jj}^H\mathbf v_j|^2 ig)-\sum_j\mathbf v_j^H\mathbf C_j\mathbf v_j+\mathrm{const},
\label{eq:bfsurr}
\end{equation}
with $\mathbf C_j=\sum_{i
e j}w_i\kappa_i\mathbf h_{ij}\mathbf h_{ij}^H$, which separates across BSs. The per-BS problem $\max_{\|\mathbf v\|^2\le P}w\Om(\kappa|\mathbf h^H\mathbf v|^2)-\mathbf v^H\mathbf C\mathbf v$ is not concave in $\mathbf v$; nevertheless, it admits a closed-form global maximizer.
 egin{proposition}[Closed-form ICT beamformer]
\label{prop:bf}
Let $\mathbf C=\mathbf Q\diag(\mathbf c)\mathbf Q^H\succeq0$, $	ilde{\mathbf h}=\mathbf Q^H\mathbf h$ and, for $\eta\ge0$, $q(\eta)=\sum_m|	ilde h_m|^2/(c_m+\eta)$, $ho(\eta)=w\kappa q(\eta)$, $s(\eta)=ho^2/(\kappa(1+ho))$ and
 egin{equation}
\mathbf v(\eta)= rac{\sqrt{s(\eta)}}{q(\eta)}\,(\mathbf C+\eta\mathbf I)^{-1}\mathbf h.
\label{eq:vbf}
\end{equation}
Then $\|\mathbf v(\eta)\|^2$ is non-increasing in $\eta$, and the global maximizer of the per-BS problem is $\mathbf v(0)$ if $\|\mathbf v(0)\|^2\le P$ and otherwise $\mathbf v(\eta^\star)$, with $\eta^\star\in(0,w/P]$ the root of $\|\mathbf v(\eta)\|^2=P$. The unclipped value is $w\log(1+ho(0))$.
\end{proposition}
 egin{IEEEproof}
For fixed $\eta$, write $s=|\mathbf h^H\mathbf v|^2$. Among all $\mathbf v$ with this $s$, $\mathbf v^H(\mathbf C+\eta\mathbf I)\mathbf v$ is minimized by $\mathbf v\propto(\mathbf C+\eta\mathbf I)^{-1}\mathbf h$ with minimum $s/q(\eta)$ (Cauchy--Schwarz), so that the Lagrangian $L(\mathbf v,\eta)=w\Om(\kappa|\mathbf h^H\mathbf v|^2)-\mathbf v^H(\mathbf C+\eta\mathbf I)\mathbf v$ is maximized by solving the concave scalar problem $\max_{s\ge0}w\Om(\kappa s)-s/q$, whose stationarity condition $w\kappa/hof(\kappa s)=1/q$ gives $hof=ho(\eta)$ and $s=s(\eta)$; this yields \eqref{eq:vbf}. The function $\eta\mapsto\max_{\mathbf v}L(\mathbf v,\eta)+\eta P$ is convex with derivative $P-\|\mathbf v(\eta)\|^2$, so that $\|\mathbf v(\eta)\|^2$ is non-increasing; the bound $\|\mathbf v(\eta)\|^2\le s/(q\eta)\le w/\eta$ gives the bracket. If $\eta^\star\ge0$ satisfies complementary slackness, then for every feasible $\mathbf v$, $f(\mathbf v)\le L(\mathbf v,\eta^\star)\le L(\mathbf v(\eta^\star),\eta^\star)=f(\mathbf v(\eta^\star))$, which proves global optimality despite the non-concavity.
\end{IEEEproof}
The ICT-BF iteration (Algorithm~ef{alg:bf}) therefore costs one $M	imes M$ eigendecomposition and one scalar bisection per BS, which is the cost of the closed-form FP beamformer $\mathbf v_j=\sqrt{w_j(1+\gamma_j)}\,y_j(\sum_i|y_i|^2\mathbf h_{ij}\mathbf h_{ij}^H+\eta\mathbf I)^{-1}\mathbf h_{jj}$ of \cite{ShenYuI}, of WMMSE, and of XMMSE. Specifically, the per-BS updates of the four methods have the common form $\mathbf v_j= eta_j(\mathbf C_j+	heta_j\mathbf h_{jj}\mathbf h_{jj}^H+\eta\mathbf I)^{-1}\mathbf h_{jj}$ with $\mathbf C_j=\sum_{i
e j}w_i\kappa_i^\star\mathbf h_{ij}\mathbf h_{ij}^H$, a scalar $ eta_j$, and an own-channel coefficient $	heta_j$ equal to the curvature of the own-signal term: $	heta_j=w_j\kappa_j^\star$ for FP/WMMSE (the QT flattens the own signal with the same weight as the interference), $	heta_j=w_jb(\gamma_j)/(\gamma_j(1+\gamma_j)b_j)$ for XMMSE/RIT+QT, which is smaller by the factor $b(\gamma)/\gamma^2\approx\log\gamma/\gamma$, and $	heta_j=0$ for ICT, whose $ eta_j=\sqrt{s(\eta)}/q(\eta)$ depends on $\eta$ through $\Om$ instead. This is the algorithmic face of Proposition~ef{prop:curv}.
 egin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{ICT beamforming (ICT-BF)}
\label{alg:bf}
 egin{algorithmic}[1]
\STATE Initialize $\mathbf v_j$ with $\|\mathbf v_j\|^2\le P$.
\REPEAT
\STATE $a_i\leftarrow|\mathbf h_{ii}^H\mathbf v_i|^2$, $b_i\leftarrow\sum_{j
e i}|\mathbf h_{ij}^H\mathbf v_j|^2+\sigma^2$, $\kappa_i\leftarrow a_i/(b_i(a_i+b_i))$.
\STATE For each BS $j$: $\mathbf C_j\leftarrow\sum_{i
e j}w_i\kappa_i\mathbf h_{ij}\mathbf h_{ij}^H$; $\mathbf v_j\leftarrow\mathbf v(\eta^\star)$ from Proposition~ef{prop:bf} with $(\mathbf C,\mathbf h,w,\kappa)=(\mathbf C_j,\mathbf h_{jj},w_j,\kappa_j)$; rescale to the power floor if $\|\mathbf v_j\|^2<p_{\min}$.
\UNTIL{the WSR has converged}
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
The beamforming surrogates of closed-form FP and WMMSE are quadratic in the $\mathbf v_j$: with $z_j=\mathbf h_{jj}^H\mathbf v_j$, the price $|y_i|^2=w_i\kappa_i^\star$ and the own-signal term $2\Real(y_j^*\sqrt{w_j(1+\gamma_j)}\,z_j)-|y_j|^2|z_j|^2$, they are $w_j\ell^{\LQ}$ evaluated at $a=|z_j|^2$, except that the linear term $2\gamma\sqrt
u$ of \eqref{eq:philq} is replaced by $2\gamma\,\Real(z_{j,0}^*z_j)/|z_{j,0}|^2\le2\gamma\sqrt
u$. By Proposition~ef{prop:xmmse}, the single-stream XMMSE surrogate is \eqref{eq:xmmse-vec}, with the same price and the same modification of $\phi_{\RQ}$. Both own-signal terms are therefore bounded above by $w_j\phi_{\LQ}(
u_j)$ and $w_j\phi_{\RQ}(
u_j)$ with $
u_j=|z_j|^2/|z_{j,0}|^2$, which Theorem~ef{thm:tightest} bounds by $w_j\phi_{\ICT}(
u_j)$, the ICT-BF own-signal term. Hence, we have the following corollary.
 egin{corollary}
\label{cor:bf}
For every anchor and every set of beamformers, the WSR surrogates satisfy $S_{\mathrm{FP}}=S_{\mathrm{WMMSE}}\le S_{\mathrm{XMMSE}}=S_{\RQ}\le S_{\ICT}\le\mathrm{WSR}$, and the ICT-BF surrogate is the tightest surrogate of the form $\sum_j[\phi_j(\mathbf h_{jj}^H\mathbf v_j)-\lambda_jb_j]$.
\end{corollary}
We have verified numerically that closed-form FP and WMMSE produce identical beamformers (maximum relative deviation  fMfourEqFPW{} over 300 iterations, consistent with \cite{Zhang23}) and, which is new, that XMMSE and RIT+QT beamforming produce identical beamformers ( fMfourEqXR{}), so that the MISO comparison below has two baselines.
 egin{remark}[Single-stream MIMO users]
\label{rem:mimo}
If user $i$ has $N$ receive antennas and one stream, its rate is $\log(1+ oldsymbol\alpha^H\mathbf B^{-1} oldsymbol\alpha)$ with $ oldsymbol\alpha=\mathbf H_{ii}\mathbf v_i\in\C^N$ and $\mathbf B\succ0$ the interference-plus-noise covariance. Among surrogates $\phi( oldsymbol\alpha)-	r(\mathbf K\mathbf B)$ affine in $\mathbf B$, tightness at $( oldsymbol\alpha_0,\mathbf B_0)$ forces the rank-one price $\mathbf K_0=\mathbf B_0^{-1} oldsymbol\alpha_0 oldsymbol\alpha_0^H\mathbf B_0^{-1}/(1+\gamma_0)$, and by Cauchy--Schwarz, $\inf_{\mathbf B\succ0}[\log(1+ oldsymbol\alpha^H\mathbf B^{-1} oldsymbol\alpha)+	r(\mathbf K_0\mathbf B)]=\Om(|\mathbf u_0^H oldsymbol\alpha|^2/(1+\gamma_0))$ with $\mathbf u_0=\mathbf B_0^{-1} oldsymbol\alpha_0$ (Appendix~ef{app:mimo}). The matrix case thus collapses to the scalar $\Om$ with no Riccati equation, the ICT beamformer is \eqref{eq:vbf} with $\mathbf h$ replaced by $\mathbf H_{ii}^H\mathbf u_0$ up to scaling, and the single-stream MIMO version of XMMSE, whose price is $w_i\mathbf K_0$ by inspection of its coefficients, is dominated as well. Multi-stream users with $\log\det$ rates are not covered: there the infimum over $\mathbf B$ does not collapse to a scalar function.
\end{remark}
