\section{Solver-Free Percentile Optimization}
\label{sec:slqp}
\subsection{Problem Formulation}
The sum-least-$q$th-percentile (SLqP) rate problem of Khan and Adve \cite{Khan24} maximizes the sum of the $K_q$ smallest user rates,
 egin{equation}
\max_{\mathbf p\in[p_{\min},P_{\max}]^K}\ f_{K_q}(\mathbf r(\mathbf p)),\qquad f_{K_q}(\mathbf r)=\min_{\mathbf x\in\mathcal X}\mathbf x^T\mathbf r,
\label{eq:slqp}
\end{equation}
with $\mathcal X=\{\mathbf x\in[0,1]^K:\mathbf 1^T\mathbf x=K_q\}$ the capped simplex (its vertices are the $0/1$ selections of $K_q$ users, so that the minimum is the sum of the $K_q$ smallest rates). It interpolates between max-min ($K_q=1$) and sum-rate ($K_q=K$) and is strongly NP-hard for $K_q>1$ \cite{Khan24}. The QFT and LFT of \cite{Khan24} are MM methods whose subproblems are convex but admit no closed form and are solved by a conic solver at every iteration; the authors state that closed-form algorithms for SLqP are unknown \cite{Khan24}.
\subsection{Proposed Approach}
The ICT surrogate supplies such an algorithm. At the anchor $ ar{\mathbf p}$, replace $r_k$ by $S_k(\mathbf p)=\Om(\kappa_kg_{kk}p_k)-\kappa_kb_k(\mathbf p)$. The MM subproblem $\max_{\mathbf p}\min_{\mathbf x\in\mathcal X}\mathbf x^T\mathbf S(\mathbf p)$ is concave in $\mathbf p$ and linear in $\mathbf x$ on compact convex sets, so that by Sion's minimax theorem it equals $\min_{\mathbf x\in\mathcal X}d(\mathbf x)$ with the dual function
 egin{equation}
d(\mathbf x)=\max_{\mathbf p}\sum_k ig[x_k\Om(\kappa_kg_{kk}p_k)-p_k\mu_k(\mathbf x) ig]-\sigma^2 oldsymbol\kappa^T\!\mathbf x
\label{eq:dual}
\end{equation}
with $\mu_k(\mathbf x)=\sum_{j
e k}x_j\kappa_jg_{jk}$ and the maximization over $[p_{\min},P_{\max}]^K$. Because the surrogate is affine in the interference and separable in the own powers, the maximization in \eqref{eq:dual} is \eqref{eq:pc} with the weights $w_k$ replaced by $x_k$: $\mathbf p(\mathbf x)$ is closed form, and so are the derivatives of $d$.
 egin{proposition}[Dual of the ICT-SLqP subproblem]
\label{prop:dual}
$d$ is convex on $\mathcal X$, with $
abla d(\mathbf x)=\mathbf S(\mathbf p(\mathbf x))$ and $
abla^2d(\mathbf x)=\mathbf J_S\,\partial\mathbf p/\partial\mathbf x$, where $(\mathbf J_S)_{kj}=\delta_{kj}\phi_k'(p_k)-(1-\delta_{kj})\kappa_kg_{kj}$, $\phi_k(p)=\Om(\kappa_kg_{kk}p)$, and, for the free coordinates, $\partial p_k/\partial x_j=[(1-\delta_{kj})\kappa_jg_{jk}-\delta_{kj}\phi_k'(p_k)]/(x_k\phi_k''(p_k))$. For every $\mathbf x\in\mathcal X$, $f_{K_q}(\mathbf S(\mathbf p(\mathbf x)))\le\max_{\mathbf p}\min_{\mathbf x'}\mathbf x'^T\mathbf S(\mathbf p)\le d(\mathbf x)$, so that the difference $d(\mathbf x)-f_{K_q}(\mathbf S(\mathbf p(\mathbf x)))$ certifies the suboptimality of $\mathbf p(\mathbf x)$ for the subproblem.
\end{proposition}
 egin{IEEEproof}
$d$ is a maximum of functions affine in $\mathbf x$, hence convex; the gradient follows from the envelope theorem (the maximizer is unique, and clipped coordinates have zero derivative); the Hessian follows by differentiating the stationarity condition $x_k\phi_k'(p_k)=\mu_k(\mathbf x)$ of the free coordinates. The bounds are weak duality and the definition of $f_{K_q}$.
\end{IEEEproof}
Consequently, the subproblem is a $K$-dimensional smooth convex problem over a capped simplex, which Algorithm~ef{alg:slqp} solves by a barrier Newton method: each step is one $K	imes K$ linear solve, the gradient and Hessian are closed form, and the certified gap of Proposition~ef{prop:dual} serves as the stopping rule. No conic solver and no modeling layer are involved. We emphasize that the construction uses exactly the two properties of Section~ef{sec:ict}: affineness in the interference (so that the inner maximization separates and the dual has closed-form derivatives) and tightness (so that the outer MM loop needs as few iterations as the solver-based transforms). The first property is shared by the LDT+QT and RIT+QT surrogates, which can be used in the same way; Section~ef{sec:num-slqp} compares them. Convergence to directional stationary points of \eqref{eq:slqp} follows as for QFT in \cite[Thm.~4]{Khan24}, since the ICT surrogate is a tangent minorant with matching directional derivatives. The same dual applies to SLqP beamforming \cite{KhanAdveII}: for fixed $\mathbf x$ the maximization separates across BSs, each beamformer is given by Proposition~ef{prop:bf} with $w_j=x_j$, and $
abla d=\mathbf S(\mathbf v(\mathbf x))$ as before.
 egin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Solver-free ICT-SLqP}
\label{alg:slqp}
 egin{algorithmic}[1]
\STATE Initialize $\mathbf p$; $\mathbf x\leftarrow(K_q/K)\mathbf 1$.
\REPEAT
\STATE $\kappa_k\leftarrow a_k/(b_k(a_k+b_k))$ at the current $\mathbf p$ (ICT surrogate).
\STATE Minimize $d$ over $\mathcal X$ by barrier Newton steps (warm start $\mathbf x$): at each step evaluate $\mathbf p(\mathbf x)$ by \eqref{eq:pc} with $w_k=x_k$, $
abla d$ and $
abla^2d$ by Proposition~ef{prop:dual}; stop when $d(\mathbf x)-f_{K_q}(\mathbf S(\mathbf p(\mathbf x)))\le10^{-9}(1+|d|)$.
\STATE $\mathbf p\leftarrow\mathbf p(\mathbf x)$.
\UNTIL{$f_{K_q}(\mathbf r(\mathbf p))$ has converged}
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
