\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:concl}
In this paper, we have shown that every closed-form algorithm for weighted sum-rate maximization that we are aware of---closed-form FP, WMMSE, interference pricing, SEFP and XMMSE---maximizes a surrogate that is linear in the interference power, because that is the structure that decouples the links, and that this family has a unique tightest member, the interference-conjugate transform. Tightness forces the interference price, which explains why all of these methods charge the same one; the single-stream XMMSE surrogate coincides with that of SEFP; and the benchmarks lose everything they lose on the own-signal side, by curvature factors of $\gamma/2$ (FP/WMMSE) and $(\log\gamma)/2$ (SEFP/XMMSE) against $1$ for ICT. The resulting power-control, scheduling and MISO beamforming algorithms have the per-iteration cost of their FP counterparts, and the same structure yields the first solver-free algorithm for sum-least-$q$th-percentile rate maximization, with a certified duality gap in place of a conic solver. In our simulations, ICT needed the fewest iterations in every setting, reached equal or better stationary points, and under proportional-fairness scheduling improved the utility over SEFP by as much as SEFP improves over FP.
The ICT updates use the same information as interference pricing and closed-form FP, so that the algorithms are as amenable to distributed implementation as the benchmarks. Three limitations should be stated. (i) The guarantees require $p_{\min}>0$ (or a power floor for beamformers) to avoid the degenerate anchor $a_i=0$; this is shared with FP, WMMSE, SEFP and XMMSE. (ii) Dominance orders certified ascent and asymptotic rates rather than realized iterates; the simulations show that ICT reaches better points on average but not on every drop. (iii) Multi-stream users with $\log\det$ rates are not covered: the partial conjugate in the interference covariance does not collapse to a scalar function when the signal covariance has rank larger than one, and the quadratic XMMSE surrogate remains the best closed-form choice there; the tightest interference-affine surrogate for the $\log\det$ rate is the natural next step.
