\section{Simulation Results}
This section presents numerical results to evaluate the performance of the proposed MA-assisted covert communication scheme. Unless otherwise specified, $M=6$, $K_o=K_c=Q=2$, $P=0$~dBW, and the noise power at all users and Willies is $-80$~dBW. Alice is located at $(0,0)\rm m$. Overt users 1 and 2 are randomly placed in disks of radius $r_o=15$~m centered $60$~m from Alice at $70^\circ$ and $110^\circ$, respectively.
One covert user is located at the center of each disk.
The Willies are located $60$~m from Alice, and the azimuth angles from Alice to the two Willies are $80^\circ$ and $100^\circ$, respectively. 
The linear transmit region has length $D=12\lambda$, with $\lambda=0.1$~m and minimum MA spacing $d=\lambda/2$. Each user channel contains one LoS path and $L-1$ non-line-of-sight (NLoS) paths, with $L=3$. The independent path gains satisfy $\beta_k^1\sim\mathcal{CN}\bigl(0,{\kappa c_k^2}/(\kappa+1)\bigr)$ for the LoS path and $\beta_k^\ell\sim\mathcal{CN}\bigl(0,{c_k^2}/{((\kappa+1)(L-1))}\bigr)$ for the NLoS paths, where $\ell=2,\ldots,L$ and $\kappa=2$. The large-scale path loss is $c_k^2=C_0d_k^{-\alpha_{\mathrm{PL}}}$, where $d_k$ is the link distance in meters, $C_0=10^{-3}$, and $\alpha_{\mathrm{PL}}=2.6$. Similarly, the Willie channel gain is $\beta_q=c_qe^{\jmath\phi_q}$, where $\phi_q\sim\mathcal{U}[0,2\pi)$, $c_q^2=C_0d_q^{-\alpha_{\mathrm{PL}}}$, and $d_q$ is the link distance in meters. The LoS azimuth angles are determined by the user and Willie locations, while the NLoS azimuth angles are independently and uniformly distributed over $[0,\pi]$. The transmission blocklength is $N=32$, the covertness level is $\epsilon=0.1$, and the covert user priority parameter is $\nu=0.5$. The CSI uncertainty coefficient is set to $\kappa_{\mathrm{CSI}}=0.01$. The bounded-error radius is chosen as $e_q^2=\kappa_{\mathrm{CSI}}\norm{\bar{\bm h}_q(\bm t)}_2^2$. 
Fig.~\ref{fig:results}\subref{fig:iteration} depicts the convergence behavior of the proposed MA algorithms for $M=6$ and $7$. It can be observed that the weighted max-min SINR achieved by the proposed MA algorithms increases monotonically and converges rapidly after 30 iterations.
In addition, from Fig.~\ref{fig:results}\subref{fig:iteration}, we can observe that the weighted max-min SINR improves with increasing $M$.
The reason lies in the additional DoFs provided by more antenna elements.
Fig.~\ref{fig:results}\subref{fig:power} illustrates the weighted max-min SINR versus the maximum transmit power $P$ for different transmission blocklengths.
It can be observed that the weighted max-min SINRs of all schemes increase as $P$ increases.
This is because a larger transmit-power budget provides more power for the transmit signal while satisfying the covertness constraints. Moreover, the proposed MA schemes outperform the conventional FPA schemes by properly optimizing the antenna positions to enhance the desired channels and mitigate interference. It can also be observed that the weighted max-min SINR achieved with $N=128$ is lower than that with $N=32$.
This is because a larger $N$ provides each Willie with more observations, improving the accuracy of received power estimation and the reliability of covert transmission detection.
For a fixed received power ratio, the KL divergence in \eqref{eq:KL_div} increases linearly with $N$.
For a fixed $\epsilon$, the threshold $\eta$ therefore decreases, imposing more stringent constraints on covert signal leakage.
These constraints restrict the feasible region and limit the achievable weighted max-min SINR.
In Fig.~\ref{fig:results}\subref{fig:csi_error}, we present the weighted max-min SINR versus the relative CSI uncertainty $\kappa_{\mathrm{CSI}}$. It can be observed that the weighted max-min SINR of all schemes decreases with increasing $\kappa_{\mathrm{CSI}}$. This is because increased uncertainty in the Willie channels makes it more difficult to satisfy the covertness constraints. For a fixed $\epsilon$, the proposed MA schemes outperform the conventional FPA schemes by properly optimizing the antenna positions to enhance the desired user channels and suppress information leakage.
\section{Conclusion}
In this paper, we investigated MA-assisted multiuser covert communications with multiple Willies under norm-bounded CSI errors.
We aimed to maximize the weighted minimum SINR by jointly optimizing the transmit beamforming vectors, AN covariance matrix, and MA positions subject to worst-case covertness constraints.
To solve this problem, we developed a BCD-based algorithm incorporating the S-procedure, SDR, bisection search, and SCA methods.
Simulation results demonstrated that the proposed MA scheme outperforms the FPA schemes in terms of weighted max-min SINR.
