	\label{fig4}
\end{figure}
As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig5}, the worst-case secrecy rate increases with the BS transmit power for all antenna configurations, since higher transmit power improves the received signal quality at Bob. Moreover, increasing the BS antenna size leads to higher secrecy rates. For a small-scale array, e.g., a $2\times2$ UPA, the performance gain is limited by the insufficient spatial degrees of freedom for simultaneously enhancing Bob's signal and suppressing information leakage toward Eve under location uncertainty. With more antennas, the increased array gain and spatial degrees of freedom enable more effective beamforming optimization, resulting in higher worst-case secrecy rates. These results highlight the importance of the BS spatial degrees of freedom in robust RIS-assisted ISAC secure transmission.
\begin{figure}[htbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{fig5.pdf}
	\caption{Impact of BS Transmit Power and Antenna Configuration on the Worst-Case Secrecy Rate.}
	\label{fig5}
\end{figure}
As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig6}, the worst-case secrecy rate increases with the BS transmit power for all RIS configurations. Moreover, increasing the number of RIS elements further improves the secrecy performance by providing more reflection and phase-control degrees of freedom for enhancing Bob's signal and suppressing information leakage toward Eve. Compared with random RIS phase shifts, the proposed optimized RIS phase design achieves higher secrecy rates across different RIS sizes and transmit power levels, demonstrating the effectiveness of jointly optimizing the RIS phase shifts and transmit beamforming for robust secure transmission.
\begin{figure}[htbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{fig6.pdf}
	\caption{Worst-case secrecy rate versus RIS size under different transmit powers.}
	\label{fig6}
\end{figure}
Fig.~\ref{fig7} shows the secrecy rate versus transmit power for different schemes. For all schemes, the secrecy rate increases with transmit power, and the perfect-CSI benchmark achieves the highest secrecy rate since it is not affected by Eve's location uncertainty. In the low-power regime, the advantage of the proposed scheme over the no-RIS and random-RIS schemes is not yet pronounced. As the transmit power increases, the proposed scheme improves more rapidly and gradually outperforms both, indicating that it can better exploit the spatial degrees of freedom at higher transmit power. In the high-power regime, the proposed scheme approaches the perfect-CSI benchmark, demonstrating that the proposed robust optimization can effectively mitigate the performance loss caused by Eve's location uncertainty.
\begin{figure}[htbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{fig7.pdf}
	\caption {Secrecy Rate Versus Transmit Power Under Different Schemes.}
	\label{fig7}
\end{figure}
\section{Conclusion}
This paper has investigated robust secure transmission in RIS-assisted ISAC systems with Eve location uncertainty and proposed a sensing-assisted two-stage optimization framework. In the first stage, the sensing beamformer and RIS reflection coefficients have been jointly optimized for accurate Eve localization, based on which an ellipsoidal location uncertainty region has been constructed using the localization CRLB. In the second stage, the communication beamformer and RIS reflection coefficients have been jointly optimized to maximize the worst-case secrecy rate, where the robust constraints have been reformulated into tractable LMI forms. Simulation results have demonstrated the effectiveness of the proposed approach and revealed a tradeoff between the sensing time fraction and the effective secrecy rate. Moreover, increasing the BS antennas, transmit power, and RIS elements can improve the worst-case secrecy rate, while the proposed method outperforms the benchmark schemes. Future work will extend the proposed framework to more practical and complex scenarios, including multiple legitimate users, multiple non-colluding eavesdroppers, and multiple RISs.
