\abstract[ABSTRACT]{
  \textbf{Objective:} To develop the Cardiologist Annotation Tool (CAT)
  for frame-level cardiac view and image-quality annotation in fetal
  echocardiography and compare it with spreadsheet-based annotation.\par
  \textbf{Methods:} CAT provides cine playback, frame navigation, and
  keyboard-based labeling without manual frame-range entry. Experts 
  annotated 25 studies using both methods, and spreadsheet annotations
  were audited for predefined data-entry errors. In a randomized
  crossover study, six medical students annotated two normal studies,
  one with each method, against an expert reference.\par
  \textbf{Results:} Among 1,746 spreadsheet-annotated files, 6.2\
  contained major or critical data-entry errors, more frequently in
  congenital heart disease than normal studies (10.9\
  CAT yielded more annotated view segments per clip (3.6 vs 1.5).
  Rates of labeled and correctly labeled frames per minute were 1.77
  and 1.68 times those with spreadsheets, respectively
  (both \textit{P} = 0.031), with gains in all six students.
  No detectable difference in view-label accuracy was observed.\par
  \textbf{Conclusion:} CAT eliminates manual frame-range entry and 
  enables finer-grained, structured annotation of fetal
  echocardiography, providing a practical approach to preparing
  datasets for AI research.
}
