\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}
\subsection{Principal findings}
The expert audit found provable major or critical data-entry
errors in 6.2\
in CHD studies. CAT prevents these structural errors by design.
CAT annotations also contained more view segments per clip,
although this does not establish more accurate boundaries.
Agreement on image-quality grades was low, suggesting the need
for clearer scoring guidelines and annotator calibration.
In medical students, frame-based annotation throughput was
higher with CAT, without a detectable difference in view accuracy.
\subsection{Sources of spreadsheet errors}
Spreadsheet annotation requires manually transferring frame 
ranges and labels from a separate cine viewer. Without checks
for file length, overlapping ranges, or missing frames, invalid
and contradictory entries may go unnoticed. Even with restricted
label choices, student spreadsheets contained 10 provable major
or critical errors. CAT avoids manual frame-range entry by using 
fixed labeling keys and deriving ranges from annotation change
points, preventing these structural errors
(Table~\ref{tab:table3_error_prevention}). However, it cannot
prevent clinically incorrect but otherwise valid labels.
\subsection{Errors in CHD studies}
The higher error rate in CHD studies is concerning because
abnormal cases are relatively uncommon in screening datasets
and are important for training diagnostic models~\cite{Vega2025CARDIUM}.
This association may partly reflect
differences in file length, case complexity, or annotators.
Our error detectors identify logical and structural
inconsistencies but cannot detect incorrect labels that remain
internally consistent. Switching between a cine viewer and a
separate spreadsheet may introduce additional recording errors,
although this was not directly assessed. These findings
highlight the importance of validating spreadsheet annotations
before model training.
\subsection{Throughput and accuracy in students}
All three throughput measures favored CAT. Labeled and
correctly labeled frames per minute were higher for all six
students, while reference segments per minute was higher
for five of six students but not significantly
(\textit{P} = 0.094). The frame-based measures include
automatically assigned default labels, whereas reference
segments per minute is less affected by labeling style.
The crossover analysis yielded similar effect estimates,
although these did not reach statistical significance.
View accuracy did not differ detectably between methods.
Recall was lowest for outflow-tract and vessel views with
both methods (Figure~\ref{fig:figure5_student_crossover}b),
suggesting that recognizing these views remains challenging
regardless of the annotation method.
\subsection{Comparison with existing tools}
Existing tools support ultrasound video annotation but differ
in their scope and capabilities. The Oxford fetal heart
annotation tool supports frame-level cardiac view and
visibility labeling in AVI videos~\cite{Bridge2021HeartAnnotation}.
Annotation Web supports image-quality assessment alongside
other annotation tasks in ultrasound videos~\cite{Smistad2021AnnotationWeb}.
CAT combines direct DICOM
cine review with frame-level cardiac view and image-quality
labeling, using predefined labels and automatic frame-range
generation to reduce manual data entry. 
\subsection{Limitations}
The expert audit lacked an independent reference standard,
so disagreements between methods could not be adjudicated.
Spreadsheet and CAT annotations were performed in a fixed
order, 3--5 months apart, by partly different experts.
Consequently, differences may reflect both the annotation
method and the annotators. Expert annotation time was not
recorded, so potential time savings for cardiologists remain
unmeasured.
The student study included only six medical students who were
aware of the annotation method and used simplified scans
rather than DICOM files. Scan and period coincided within
each crossover group, limiting their separation in the
paired analysis. Annotation time was self-reported, with
event-log verification available only for CAT. The expert
reference was prepared using CAT, sharing its annotation
granularity. Finally, CAT was developed and evaluated by
the same research group.
\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
CAT streamlines frame-level annotation of fetal echocardiography,
allowing fetal cardiologists to focus on image interpretation rather
than manual spreadsheet entry. Our evaluations showed that CAT enabled 
finer-grained view annotations, avoided structural data-entry errors,
and improved annotation efficiency among medical students without a
detectable difference in view-label accuracy. These findings support
CAT as a practical tool for preparing fetal echocardiography datasets
for AI research. Future work will focus on two extensions: integrating a machine-learning
module that predicts standard cardiac views and adapts to annotator
corrections, and developing a study-level dashboard to track annotated
views and identify remaining annotation gaps.
