\title{Energy-Aware Vertically Layered Pinching-Antenna System Design with Probabilistic LoS Availability\vspace{-0.65em}}
\author{Ruotong Zhao and Derrick Wing Kwan Ng,~\IEEEmembership{Fellow,~IEEE} 
\IEEEauthorblockA{School of Electrical Engineering and Telecommunications, University of New South Wales, Sydney, NSW 2052, Australia
\vspace{-7mm}}}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
This paper investigates a vertically layered pinching antenna (PA) system, in which dielectric waveguides deployed at different heights provide additional vertical spatial degrees of freedom for line-of-sight (LoS)-favorable PA placement. We consider a practical energy model that captures in-waveguide attenuation, base station-to-feed-point optical-fiber loss, and PA repositioning energy, along with a statistical LoS-availability model that depends on elevation and distance. We then jointly optimize the transmit beamforming, PA positions, time allocation, and transmission rates to maximize the expected successfully delivered frame-effective sum rate under per-user outage and quality-of-service constraints. To avoid enumerating the exponentially many LoS states, we derive a certified conditional-moment outage envelope for outage-guaranteed rate selection and a differentiable safe approximation of the chance constraints to facilitate tractable optimization. Building on these results, a block coordinate descent algorithm with a penalty-aided majorization-minimization procedure attains a high-quality suboptimal solution. Simulation results confirm the gains of vertical layering and reveal a fundamental energy-LoS tradeoff: LoS-favorable PA placement generally enhances both performance and reliability, although it incurs higher attenuation, path loss, and energy costs.
\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}
Pinching antennas (PAs) have recently attracted significant attention for their spatial reconfigurability, which enables adaptive transmission in dynamic or obstructed scenarios within complex communication environments~\cite{zhao2026resource}.
Existing studies have investigated energy-constrained design for PA systems (PASS) in downlink~\cite{zeng2025energy} and uplink~\cite{zeng2025uplink} settings, demonstrating their advantages over conventional antenna architectures and characterizing the spectral--energy efficiency tradeoff~\cite{zhou2026spectral}.
In addition,~\cite{xu2025joint} minimized both the transmit and mechanical PA repositioning power. However, these energy-aware studies~\cite{zeng2025energy,zeng2025uplink,zhou2026spectral,xu2025joint} neglect position-dependent attenuation along the dielectric waveguide and the base station (BS)-to-feed-point optical-fiber loss. They also over-optimistically assume perfect line-of-sight (LoS) availability, ignoring inevitable blockage-induced randomness. As a result, they are unable to capture the fundamental non-trivial tradeoff among LoS-favorable PA placement, propagation conditions, and energy consumption.
On the other hand, recent studies have highlighted the potential of PASS to maintain LoS connectivity in blockage-prone environments~\cite{ding2025los,wang2025pin}, while largely neglecting PA repositioning energy costs. Although~\cite{li2026power} investigated a power-minimization problem under probabilistic LoS availability for PASS, it did not explicitly account for key practical energy consumption factors, e.g.,  motor-driven PA repositioning energy and the in-waveguide attenuation. In addition, these works predominantly adopt a conventional co-planar waveguide deployment, in which multiple waveguides are arranged in parallel at the same ceiling height~\cite{zeng2025energy,zeng2025uplink,zhou2026spectral,xu2025joint,blockage2025,ding2025los,wang2025pin,li2026power}, leaving the vertical deployment dimension unexploited. Specifically, higher PA elevations generally improve LoS availability by mitigating blockage, whereas lower elevations shorten the propagation distance and thereby reduce path loss~\cite{cai2022resource}. Moreover, moving a PA toward a LoS-favorable horizontal position can incur additional repositioning energy and in-waveguide attenuation.
This interplay among PA elevation, path loss, LoS availability, and energy consumption gives rise to a fundamental non-trivial resource allocation tradeoff that remains unexplored.
Motivated by the above observations, this work investigates a vertically layered multi-waveguide PASS under a practical energy model capturing in-waveguide attenuation, optical-fiber loss, and PA repositioning energy. We exploit statistical LoS availability for outage-guaranteed PASS design by jointly optimizing the transmit beamforming, PA positions, time allocation, and transmission rates to maximize the expected successfully delivered frame-effective sum rate, subject to maximum tolerable per-user outage probability constraints. 
Since exactly evaluating the outage probability of the formulated nonconvex chance-constrained problem requires enumerating exponentially many LoS states, we exploit the inherent link sparsity of PASS to derive a certified conditional-moment (CM) outage envelope for outage-guaranteed rate selection, and further construct a differentiable safe approximation of the chance constraints for tractable optimization. Capitalizing on these results, we develop an iterative block coordinate descent (BCD)-based algorithm incorporating a penalty-based method and majorization-minimization (MM), achieving a high-quality suboptimal solution. Numerical results show that the vertically layered PASS unlocks additional spatial degrees of freedom (DoF) and reveals a non-trivial tradeoff between energy consumption and LoS-favorable PA positioning under outage-guaranteed transmission.
\textbf{Notation:} 
Boldface capital and lowercase letters denote matrices and vectors, respectively. $\textbf{A}^\top$, $\textbf{A}^H$, $\text{Tr}(\textbf{A})$, and $\text{Rank}(\textbf{A})$ represent the transpose, Hermitian transpose, trace, and rank of $\textbf{A}$, respectively. 
The operator $\mathrm{diag}(\textbf{a})$ forms a diagonal matrix from vector $\textbf{a}$, while $\mathrm{Diag}(\textbf{A})$ extracts the main diagonal of matrix $\textbf{A}$.
$\mathbb{C}^{N \times M}$ ($\mathbb{R}^{N \times M}$),  $\mathbb{H}^{N}$, and $\mathbb{I}\{\cdot\}$ denote the spaces of $N \times M$ complex (real), $N \times N$ Hermitian matrices, and indicator function, respectively. The largest eigenvalue of $\textbf{A}$ and its associated eigenvector are $\lambda_{\rm max}\hspace{-1mm}\left(\hspace{-0.2mm}\textbf{A}\hspace{-0.2mm}\right)$ and $\bm{\lambda}_{\rm max}\hspace{-1mm}\left(\hspace{-0.2mm}\textbf{A}\hspace{-0.2mm}\right)$, respectively. We utilize $|\cdot|$, $\mathbb{E}\{\cdot\}$, $\textbf{1}$, $j = \sqrt{-1}$,  $\Re(a)$, and $\Im(a)$ to represent the absolute value, statistical expectation, the all-one vector, the imaginary unit, the real part, and the imaginary part of a complex number $a$, respectively. The circularly symmetric complex Gaussian (CSCG) distribution is denoted by $\mathcal{CN}(\mu, \sigma^2)$, with mean $\mu$ and variance $\sigma^2$.
\section{System Model and Problem Statement}\label{sec:System}
As illustrated in Fig. 1, we consider a downlink vertically layered PASS serving $K$ single-antenna users through $N$ dielectric waveguides mounted on a building surface, each supporting one movable PA. A central BS located at $\bm{\psi}^{\rm{BS}}=[0,0,z_{\rm BS}]^\top$ is connected via dedicated optical-fiber fronthaul links to phase-synchronized local feed modules colocated at the respective waveguide feed points. Unlike conventional co-planar PASS architectures~\cite{zeng2025energy,zeng2025uplink,zhou2026spectral,xu2025joint,blockage2025,ding2025los,wang2025pin,li2026power}, the waveguides are deployed at different heights, introducing an additional vertical dimension for effective PA placement. In a 3D Cartesian coordinate system, each waveguide has length $L$ meters, where the coordinates of the feed point and PA position on the $n$-th waveguide are $\bm{\psi}_n^{\mathrm{FP}} = [x_{\rm B},0,z_n]^\top$ and $\bm{\psi}_{n}^{\mathrm{Pin}} = [x^{\mathrm{Pin}}_{n},0,z_n]^\top$, respectively, with $z_n = z_1 + (n-1)d_z$ and $d_z > 0$ denoting the vertical separation between adjacent layers, $\forall n \in \mathcal{N} \triangleq \{1,\ldots,N\}$.
User $u_k$ is located at $\bm{\phi}_k = [x_k,y_k,0]^\top$, $\forall k \in \mathcal{K} \triangleq \{1,\ldots,K\}$. Let $s_k\in\mathbb{C}$, with $\mathbb{E}\{|s_k|^2\}=1$ and $\mathbb{E}\{s_j^{*}s_k\}=0$, $\forall j\neq k$, denote the information symbol for $u_k$ and let $\mathbf{w}_k=[w_{k,1},\ldots,w_{k,N}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{C}^{N\times 1}$ denote its beamforming vector, such that the transmit signal at the feed points is $\mathbf{s}\triangleq \sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}}\mathbf{w}_k s_k \in \mathbb{C}^{N\times 1}$.
