\subsection{Hybrid LoS Availability Model}
For each PA-user link, the horizontal and 3D PA-user distances are $l_{n,k} = \sqrt{(x_{n}^{\mathrm{Pin}} - x_k)^2 + y_k^2}$ and $d_{n,k} = \sqrt{l_{n,k}^2 + z_n^2}$, respectively, with the corresponding elevation angle $\theta_{n,k} = \tfrac{180}{\pi}\tan^{-1}(\tfrac{z_n}{l_{n,k}})$, $\forall n, k$, and $\theta_{n,k} = 90^{\circ}$ when $l_{n,k} = 0$. The LoS availability of each link is modeled by the multiplicative hybrid probability $q_{n,k}^{\mathrm{LoS}} = q_{n,k}^{\mathrm{ele}}q_{n,k}^{\mathrm{dis}}$ since $q_{n,k}^{\mathrm{LoS}} \le \min\{q_{n,k}^{\mathrm{ele}}, q_{n,k}^{\mathrm{dis}}\}$, where $q_{n,k}^{\mathrm{ele}} = \tfrac{1}{1+A_{\rm L}\exp[-B_{\rm L}(\theta_{n,k}-A_{\rm L})]}$~\cite{cai2022resource, bor2016efficient} and $q_{n,k}^{\mathrm{dis}} = \exp(-\wp_{\rm L}d_{n,k}^{2})$~\cite{li2026power} capture elevation-dependent LoS visibility and distance-dependent blockage, respectively, with LoS parameters $A_{\rm L}, B_{\rm L} \ge 0$ and blockage density $\wp_{\rm L} \ge 0$. The LoS indicators $\{\chi_{n,k}\}_{n,k}$ are modeled as independent Bernoulli variables with $\Pr\{\chi_{n,k}=1\}=q_{n,k}^{\mathrm{LoS}}$~\cite{li2026power}. Collecting $\bm{\chi}_k = [\chi_{1,k}, \ldots, \chi_{N,k}]^\top$, $\bm{\chi} \triangleq \{\bm{\chi}_{k}\}_{k \in \mathcal{K}}$, each realization occurs, conditioned on the PA-position vector $\textbf{x}^{\rm Pin}$, with probability $\pi_k(\bm{\chi}_k|\mathbf x^{\rm Pin},\bm\phi_k) =  \underset{{n \in \mathcal{N}}}{\Pi} (q_{n,k}^{\rm LoS})^{\chi_{n, k}} (1-q_{n,k}^{\rm LoS})^{1-\chi_{n, k}}$. Unlike conventional distance-based PASS blockage models~\cite{li2026power}, this hybrid model incorporates elevation-angle dependence, thereby capturing the dependence of the probabilistic quality-of-service (QoS) constraints on PA positioning.
