Three benchmarks are considered: \emph{Co-planar PASS} deploys all waveguides at $z=5$~m while retaining the proposed optimization framework; \emph{MRT-PA} retains the vertical deployment and PA positioning but adopts maximum ratio transmission with a fixed transmission time; and \emph{Conv.} employs a fixed antenna array, optimizing only the beamforming, coding rates, and transmission time.  We set $\delta_k=0.1$, $\forall k$, except for \emph{Conv.}, which is infeasible under this target and thus is evaluated at $\delta_k=0.4$ and assessed by the delivered sum rate in (5) without the $(1-\delta)$ weighting. 
Fig.~\ref{fig:energy}(a) shows that the successfully delivered sum rate first increases with the energy budget and eventually saturates as the transmit power and frame duration constraints become dominant.  The \emph{Proposed} scheme consistently outperforms the benchmarks, since the vertical DoF enables more effective joint optimization over propagation loss, attenuation, repositioning cost, and spatial channel dynamics.  Moreover, \emph{MRT-PA} exhibits a larger gap than the co-planar PASS, indicating that the loss from a prescribed beamformer and a fixed transmission time outweighs the gain from vertical deployment, which confirms the necessity of jointly optimizing the beamforming and time allocation.  As expected, \emph{Conv.} performs worst despite its relaxed outage target, as a fixed array can neither adapt its geometry nor exploit aperture mobility. The allocation strategy underpinning these gains is illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:energy}(b), where the waveguides (WGs) are indexed by height for the proposed scheme and by horizontal location for the co-planar PASS. It is observed that the proposed design concentrates $43\
This allocation reflects the favorable tradeoff offered by the upper layer: the LoS availability increases, e.g., from $(0.95, 0.57, 0.81)$ on WG1 to $(0.99, 0.97, 0.99)$ on WG8 across $(u_1, u_2, u_3)$. Thus, the LoS reliability gain from exploiting WG8 outweighs the associated repositioning energy costs and increased attenuation distance.
In contrast, the co-planar PASS lacks height diversity and thus relies more heavily on horizontal PA repositioning, whereas \emph{MRT-PA} spreads its power across all WGs, as its prescribed beamformer cannot suppress the multiuser interference.
