Since $T_{\rm tot}$ varies as an available-time budget, we report the time-integrated successfully delivered sum rate rather than the frame-normalized rate, avoiding an artificial normalization penalty for large deadlines.
Fig.~\ref{fig:TimeS} shows that the proposed scheme attains the highest sum rate for all considered $T_{\rm tot}$, with the largest gain in the short-deadline regime, as its height diversity provides LoS-favorable branches without relying heavily on horizontal repositioning, such that less positioning time is required and more of the frame is reserved for transmission. Lacking such diversity, the co-planar PASS depends more strongly on horizontal PA placement, such that additional time partially narrows the gap. In contrast, \emph{MRT-PA} exploits the additional time only for PA repositioning, and its fixed beamformer cannot accurately mitigate the multiuser interference, such that the gap persists across the entire range.
The fixed array remains the least effective and falls further behind. However, its sum rate keeps increasing as the additional time is devoted entirely to transmission without PA repositioning.
\section{Conclusion}
This paper investigated an energy-aware vertically layered PASS capturing in-waveguide attenuation, fronthaul loss, and repositioning energy under a hybrid elevation- and distance-dependent LoS availability model. The beamforming, PA positions, time allocation, and coding rates were jointly optimized to maximize the expected successfully delivered sum rate under probabilistic outage constraints. We derived a CM outage envelope and a differentiable safe approximation, and developed a penalty-aided MM-based BCD algorithm. Simulation results confirmed the vertical-layering gains and the reduced repositioning time over the co-planar PASS, and unveiled the fundamental energy–LoS tradeoff, despite LoS-favorable positions generally incurring higher path attenuation.
