\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}
\IEEEPARstart{T}{he} sixth-generation (6G) mobile networks are envisioned to provide ubiquitous connectivity for emerging applications, including autonomous vehicles, smart factories, and embodied intelligence~\cite{Vision6G}.
Conventional wireless networks primarily serve terrestrial devices, whereas 6G networks are expected to integrate terrestrial and aerial networks.
In particular, the low-altitude economy (LAE) is driving the deployment of a growing number of uncrewed aerial vehicles (UAVs) \cite{LAENet}. 
These UAVs can be integrated into cellular networks as aerial users~\cite{aerialuser} or aerial sensing platforms~\cite{MAISAC2} for applications such as surveillance, data collection, and aerial filming \cite{ZhanUAVSensing}. 
However, the broadcast nature of wireless channels and the high-quality links associated with aerial devices expose low-altitude transmissions to security risks such as eavesdropping, information leakage, and jamming \cite{AlHabobAerialPLS,XiuMAAntiJammingMEC}. 
These risks underscore the fundamental need for reliable and secure wireless transmission in low-altitude networks.
Physical-layer security (PLS) approaches leverage differences between legitimate and eavesdropping channels to reduce information leakage and enhance the secrecy capacity of wireless systems \cite{Wyner1975}.
Existing PLS techniques mainly include artificial noise (AN) \cite{DongFCLASecure}, directional modulation (DM) \cite{YangDiscreteRISSLP}, and null-space beamforming \cite{Qian6DMASecure2026}.
These PLS techniques have been widely adopted in various wireless communication scenarios, such as simultaneous wireless information and power transfer (SWIPT) \cite{DongMASecureSWIPT}, integrated sensing and communication (ISAC) \cite{XiuRISAAVISAC}, physical-layer service integration \cite{citeformeipls}, and airborne communications \cite{ZhuToMA,XiuSecureISACAAV2025}.
Although these measures can protect the confidentiality of transmitted message, they do not necessarily conceal the existence of a transmission, thereby increasing the risk of exposing transmission behavior.
In this context, covert communication, also known as low probability of detection (LPD) communication, has drawn wide
attention for its capability to conceal transmission behavior \cite{WangPLSCovert}.
The authors in \cite{Bash2013} pioneered to establish the fundamental square root law, which states that $\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{N})$ bits can be delivered reliably and covertly over $N$ uses of an additive white Gaussian noise (AWGN) channel.
In \cite{SobersJammer2017}, an uninformed jammer transmitting AN was proposed to increas the number of reliably and covertly transmitted bits from $\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{N})$ to $\mathcal{O}(N)$.
The authors in \cite{SoltaniAN} investigated a random network in which the friendly transmitter nearest the warden generates AN.
Covert communication has also been investigated in air-ground networks.
The authors in \cite{WangUAVSurveillance} investigated multi-hop secrecy and covert communication between terrestrial nodes under UAV surveillance.
In \cite{ChenUAVFlyingWarden}, a UAV relay was investigated to supported finite-blocklength covert communication against a flying warden.
In particular, multi-antenna techniques have been widely adopted in covert communications by offering spatial degrees of freedom (DoFs) in both detection and transmission.
From the detection perspective, the authors in \cite{HuBeamSweeping} derived the optimal number of beam-sweeping sectors that minimizes the detection error probability (DEP) of a UAV transmission.
For covert transmission, centralized and distributed multi-antenna architectures were proposed to improve covert throughput in random wireless networks \cite{ZhengMultiAntennaCovert}.
The authors in \cite{ForouzeshNull} combined three-dimensional transmit beamforming with multi-antenna jamming nulled at the intended receiver to improve the covert rate.
Although these optimization schemes have demonstrated the benefits of multi-antenna transmission, most prototypes and algorithm development are based on conventional fixed-position antenna (FPA) arrays. 
Such fixed array geometry leaves the spatial DoFs associated with antenna positions unexploited, leading to limited covertness performance improvement.
More importantly, when intended receiver and warden channels are highly correlated, the spatial discrimination capability becomes insufficient, thereby cannot strengthen the desired links while  effectively suppressing information leakage.
Recently, movable antennas (MAs) have emerged as an innovative solution to overcome this limitation by dynamically adjusting the antenna positions within a wavelength-scale region~\cite{MA4,YangFlexibleWMMSE2025}.
Unlike conventional FPA arrays, MA arrays can avoid deep fading and reshape the composite channel through changes in the propagation phases of the multipath components with antenna repositioning in real time~\cite{MA1}.
In particular, antenna movement can be realized using mechanically driven, liquid-based, or electronically reconfigurable architectures.
For instance, the authors in \cite{WeiMechanicalPower2026} developed a power consumption model for motor-driven MA systems by jointly optimizing the antenna positions, moving speeds, and transmit precoding to maximize energy efficiency.
The authors in \cite{DongMAPrototype} developed a motor-driven MA prototype with a slide track positioning accuracy of 0.05~mm and experimentally verified the received power gains of antenna movement at 3.5 and 27.5~GHz.
The ability of MA arrays to improve channel conditions has motivated further investigation of the benefits of jointly optimizing the MA positions and beamforming~\textcolor{red}{\cite{YangRobustRISMA2026}}.
For example, the authors in \cite{WangMNFBeamCoverage} used Fourier transform theory to construct a spatial multi-notch filter and select MA positions from a discrete set of sampling points for multi-region beam coverage.
The same filter-based view was applied to multi-beam forming by reducing the pass bands over continuous angular regions to impulses at the desired discrete angles~\cite{MeiGeneralAPO2026}.
Under the derived feasibility condition, the authors in \cite{zlp1} analytically showed that joint MA position and beamforming optimization can achieve full array gain in the desired direction while forming nulls in all specified undesired directions.
Furthermore, the authors in \cite{citeformeirobust} unveiled that antenna position optimization can reduce the performance loss due to imperfect CSI.
The aforementioned studies provided a theoretical basis for using MA arrays to suppress signal leakage toward undesired directions.
This ability has also been exploited in covert communication.
For example, the authors in \cite{WangWavelengthCovert} attained wavelength-scale beamforming resolution by adjusting the antenna positions, which preserves transmission covertness even when the warden is close to the intended receiver.
In \cite{LiuMACovert}, the transmit beamforming and MA positions were jointly optimized for a single covert user in the presence of multiple non-colluding wardens.
The authors in \cite{MaoMultiuserCovert} maximized the sum rate of multiple covert users in the presence of a single warden.
In \cite{ChengMFDACovert}, a movable frequency diverse array was adopted against multiple wardens with uncertain locations.
The authors in \cite{HuFDMACovert} employed a full-duplex receiver with MAs to transmit AN against an MA-equipped warden.
Although these works have demonstrated the effectiveness of MAs, they are typically based on the perfect knowledge of the warden channel state information (CSI).
In practice, perfect CSI of non-cooperative wardens is challenging for Alice to acquire~\cite{ForouzeshCDI,CovertSurvey}.
Therefore, ignoring the inherent CSI error may violate the covertness requirement, leading to degraded security performance~\cite{ZhouMixedFieldCovert}.
To address this issue, the authors in \cite{YangMAISACC2026} investigated robust beamforming and antenna position optimization for covert ISAC systems.
However, the proposed approach in \cite{YangMAISACC2026} was limited to scenarios with a single warden and cannot be applied to other error models beyond the bounded error model, i.e., the channel error is bounded by a prescribed value.
As presented in Table~\ref{tab:related_work_comparison}, the proposed framework is compared with related studies in terms of system models, CSI assumptions, joint optimization, and covertness guarantees.
To enhance data security and further unlock the synergy gain between MAs and covert transmission, a unified robust MA-based transmission framework is highly imperative. 
