In this paper, we study robust MA-assisted covert communications with multiple wardens under two typical warden CSI error models. The main contributions are summarized as follows.
\begin{itemize}[\setlength{\itemsep}{0pt plus 1pt}]
\item We introduce an MA-assisted covert communication system with Alice serving legitimate users in the presence of multiple wardens. To guarantee transmission covertness, we develop a unified optimization framework to maximize the weighted max-min signal-to-interference-plus-noise ratio (SINR) by jointly optimizing transmit beamforming, AN covariance matrix, and antenna placement, subject to a transmit power budget and a covertness constraint at each warden. 
The nonlinear coupling between MA positions and uncertain warden channels complicates the derivation of tractable constraint approximations that preserve the required covertness guarantees.
In addition to norm-bounded CSI errors, statistical warden CSI errors are also accounted for, assuming that they follow complex Gaussian distribution.
\item For the norm-bounded errors, we develop a worst-case optimization scheme that enforces the covertness constraints for every error realization in the channel uncertainty set. 
To address the challenging problem, we first apply the S-procedure to reformulate the intractable semi-infinite quadratic condition into an equivalent linear matrix inequality (LMI). 
Then, we develop a block coordinate descent (BCD) algorithm that integrates semidefinite relaxation (SDR) and bisection search for the covariance matrix optimization with a quadratic transform and successive convex approximation (SCA) for the position optimization.
\item For the statistical CSI error, we propose an outage-constrained optimization scheme to ensure covertness with a prescribed probability guarantee. 
A deterministic approximation of the covertness constraint is derived by using the Bernstein-type inequality (BTI) for complex Gaussian quadratic forms and is expressed as LMI and second-order cone (SOC) constraints that can be efficiently tackled by using the proposed BCD optimization framework.
\item Simulation results demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed schemes and provide insights into robust MA-assisted covert transmission.
In particular, the proposed MA schemes outperform the conventional FPA schemes in terms of weighted max-min SINR under both CSI error models, with further improvements observed for larger movable regions and richer multipath propagation.
Moreover, the statistical optimization scheme outperforms the worst-case scheme by allowing a prescribed covertness outage probability.
\end{itemize}
\section{System Model}\label{sys_model}
\subsection{Signal Model}
We consider a downlink multiuser multiple-input single-output (MU-MISO) covert communication system, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:system_model}.
\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{SystemModel.pdf}
  \caption{MA-assisted covert communication system.}
  \label{fig:system_model}
\end{figure}
It is assumed that Alice is equipped with $M$ MAs, and serves $K=K_o+K_c$ single-antenna legitimate users in the presence of $Q$ single-antenna wardens.
Let $\mathcal{M}\triangleq\{1,\ldots,M\}$ denote the set of transmit MA indices.
The legitimate users are divided into $K_o$ overt users and $K_c$ covert users. 
The overt signals can be exploited as a cover to enable the covert transmission of confidential information.
The set of overt users is denoted by $\mathcal{K}_o = \{1, \ldots, K_o\}$, the set of covert users is denoted by
$\mathcal{K}_c = \{K_o+1, \ldots, K_o+K_c\}$, and the set of all legitimate users is $\mathcal{K} = \mathcal{K}_o \cup \mathcal{K}_c$.
The set of wardens is $\mathcal{Q}=\{1,\dots,Q\}$. Each warden is a detector carried by a rotary-wing UAV that hovers over the served area, and it passively observes its received signal and tests whether a covert transmission is present. 
Let $\mathcal{H}_1$ and $\mathcal{H}_0$ denote the hypotheses that Alice does and does not transmit the covert signal, respectively. Under the two hypotheses, the signal transmitted by Alice is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:tx_signal}
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal{H}_1 :\;\boldsymbol{s}[n] &= \boldsymbol{W}_o \boldsymbol{c}_o[n] + \boldsymbol{W}_c \boldsymbol{c}_c[n] + \boldsymbol{r}[n],
\mathcal{H}_0 :\;\boldsymbol{s}[n] &= \boldsymbol{W}_o \boldsymbol{c}_o[n] + \boldsymbol{r}[n],
\end{aligned}
\right.
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol{s}[n] \in \mathbb{C}^{M\times 1}$ is the transmit signal in the $n$-th time slot, $n\in\{1,\ldots,N\}$, with $N$ being the transmission blocklength, $\boldsymbol{W}_o = [\boldsymbol{w}_1, \ldots, \boldsymbol{w}_{K_o}] \in \mathbb{C}^{M\times K_o}$ and $\boldsymbol{W}_c = [\boldsymbol{w}_{K_o+1}, \ldots, \boldsymbol{w}_{K_o+K_c}] \in \mathbb{C}^{M\times K_c}$ are the beamforming matrices for the overt and covert users, respectively, $\boldsymbol{c}_o[n] \in \mathbb{C}^{K_o\times 1}$ and $\boldsymbol{c}_c[n] \in \mathbb{C}^{K_c\times 1}$ are the corresponding overt and covert data streams whose entries are independent and identically distributed as $\mathcal{CN}(0,1)$, and $\boldsymbol{r}[n] \sim \mathcal{CN}(\bm{0}_M, \boldsymbol{R}_{\rm A})$ is the AN vector with covariance matrix $\boldsymbol{R}_{\rm A} \succeq \boldsymbol{0}$.
For brevity, we define $\boldsymbol{W}\triangleq[\boldsymbol{W}_o,\boldsymbol{W}_c]$. 
\subsection{Channel Model}
We assume that the MAs can move within a linear region $\mathcal{X}=[0,A]$, where $A$ denotes the region length. The position of the $m$-th MA is denoted by $x_m\in\mathcal{X}$, and the antenna position vector (APV) is defined as $\boldsymbol{x}=[x_1,\ldots,x_M]^T\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times1}$.
We consider a far-field channel model in which the size of the moving region is significantly smaller than the signal propagation distance~\cite{P_FAA}. 
Thus, the antenna movement does not affect the angles of departure (AoDs) \cite{zlp2}.
Let $L_k$ denote the number of channel paths between Alice and legitimate user $k$. The azimuth AoD of the $\ell$-th path to user $k$ is denoted by $\theta_{k}^{\ell} \in [0,\pi]$, where $1 \leq \ell \leq L_k$.
The signal propagation distance difference between the $m$-th transmitting MA position $x_m$ and the reference point $\boldsymbol{o}^t$ is given by
$\chi_{k}^{\ell}(x_m) = x_m\cos\theta_{k}^{\ell}$.
To incorporate the phase
differences across all $L_k$ transmit paths, the field response vector (FRV) at $x_m$ is then given by
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{g}_{k}(x_m) =
  \left[
 e^{\jmath\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}\chi^{1}_{k}(x_m)},\cdots,
 e^{\jmath\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}\chi^{L_k}_{k}(x_m)}
  \right]
  ^T \in \mathbb{C}^{L_k\times 1},
\end{equation}
where $\lambda$ is the wavelength of the carrier signal.
As a result, the field response matrix (FRM) from Alice to user $k$ over all transmit MAs is
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{G}_k(\boldsymbol{x}) =
  \left[
 \boldsymbol{g}_{k}(x_1), \cdots, \boldsymbol{g}_{k}(x_M)
  \right] \in \mathbb{C}^{L_k\times M}.
\end{equation}
Let $\boldsymbol{f}_k = [\beta_{k}^{*,1},\cdots,\beta_{k}^{*,L_k}]^T \in \mathbb{C}^{L_k\times 1}$ be the path response vector (PRV) from Alice to the $k$-th user. Then, the channel vector at the $k$-th user is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:user_channel}
  \boldsymbol{h}_k^H(\boldsymbol{x}) = \boldsymbol{f}_k^H\boldsymbol{G}_k(\boldsymbol{x}) \in \mathbb{C}^{1\times M},\forall k\in\mathcal{K}.
\end{equation}
As the warden is assumed to be a UAV, a line-of-sight (LoS) model is adopted for the warden channel, i.e.,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:warden_channel}
\boldsymbol{h}_{q}^H(\boldsymbol{x}) = \beta_q \, \boldsymbol{g}_{q}^H(\boldsymbol{x}), \forall q \in \mathcal{Q},
\end{equation}
where $\beta_q$ is the LoS complex gain between warden $q$ and Alice with AoD $\theta_{q}$, and $\boldsymbol{g}_{q}^H(\boldsymbol{x})$ is the corresponding FRV given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:warden_channel_FRV}
\boldsymbol{g}_{q}^H(\boldsymbol{x}) =
  \left[
 e^{\jmath\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}\chi_{q}(x_1)},\cdots,
 e^{\jmath\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}\chi_{q}(x_M)}
  \right] \in \mathbb{C}^{1\times M},
\end{equation}
with $\chi_{q}(x_m) = x_m\cos\theta_{q}$ denoting the propagation distance difference between the $m$-th transmitting MA position and the reference point $\boldsymbol{o}^t$ for warden $q$.
\subsection{Communication Performance}
The received signal at the $k$-th legitimate user under $\mathcal{H}_1$ is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rx_user}
\begin{aligned}
y_k[n] = 
&\underbrace{\boldsymbol{h}_k^H(\boldsymbol{x})\, \boldsymbol{w}_k c_k[n]}_{\text{desired signal}} 
+ \underbrace{\sum_{i\in\mathcal{K}\setminus\{k\}} \boldsymbol{h}_k^H(\boldsymbol{x})\, \boldsymbol{w}_i c_i[n]}_{\text{multiuser interference}}
&
+ \underbrace{\boldsymbol{h}_k^H(\boldsymbol{x})\, \boldsymbol{r}[n]}_{\text{AN interference}}
+ z_k[n],
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $z_k[n] \sim \mathcal{CN}(0,\sigma_k^2)$ denotes the AWGN at the $k$-th legitimate user.
The corresponding SINR at the $k$-th legitimate user under $\mathcal{H}_1$ is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sinr_user}
\begin{aligned}
&\gamma_{1,k}^C(\boldsymbol{W},\boldsymbol{R}_{\rm A},\boldsymbol{x}) = &
\frac{\boldsymbol{h}_k^H(\boldsymbol{x}) \boldsymbol{w}_k \boldsymbol{w}_k^H \boldsymbol{h}_k(\boldsymbol{x})}
{\sum_{i\in\mathcal{K}\setminus\{k\}} \!\boldsymbol{h}_k^H(\boldsymbol{x}) \boldsymbol{w}_i \boldsymbol{w}_i^H \boldsymbol{h}_k(\boldsymbol{x})
\!+\! \boldsymbol{h}_k^H(\boldsymbol{x}) \boldsymbol{R}_{\rm A}\boldsymbol{h}_k(\boldsymbol{x}) \!+\! \sigma_k^2}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Likewise, the SINR of overt user $k\in\mathcal K_o$ under $\mathcal{H}_0$ is given by\footnote{Note that due to the additional interference from the covert users that is present under $\mathcal{H}_1$ but absent under $\mathcal{H}_0$, we can verify that $\gamma_{1,k}^C(\boldsymbol{x}) \le \gamma_{0,k}^C(\boldsymbol{x})$ for all $k\in\mathcal K_o$.}
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\gamma_{0,k}^C(\boldsymbol{W},\boldsymbol{R}_{\rm A},\boldsymbol{x}) = &
\frac{\boldsymbol{h}_k^H(\boldsymbol{x}) \boldsymbol{w}_k \boldsymbol{w}_k^H \boldsymbol{h}_k(\boldsymbol{x})}
{\sum_{i\in\mathcal{K}_o\!\setminus\!\{k\}}\! \boldsymbol{h}_k^H(\boldsymbol{x}) \boldsymbol{w}_i \boldsymbol{w}_i^H \boldsymbol{h}_k(\boldsymbol{x})
\!+\! \boldsymbol{h}_k^H(\boldsymbol{x}) \boldsymbol{R}_{\rm A}\boldsymbol{h}_k(\boldsymbol{x}) \!+\! \sigma_k^2}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
\subsection{Detection Performance and Covertness Constraint}
We consider a non-colluding warden scenario, where each warden independently detect the communication behaviour between Alice and the covert users based on the Neyman--Pearson test~\cite{ZhouMixedFieldCovert}. 
Specifically, the received signal at warden $q$ in time slot $n$ can be given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rx_warden}
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal{H}_1: y_{q}[n] & = \boldsymbol{h}_{q}^H(\boldsymbol{x}) \big( \boldsymbol{W}_o \boldsymbol{c}_o[n] + \boldsymbol{W}_c \boldsymbol{c}_c[n] + \boldsymbol{r}[n] \big) + z_{q}[n], 
\mathcal{H}_0: y_{q}[n] & = \boldsymbol{h}_{q}^H(\boldsymbol{x}) \big( \boldsymbol{W}_o \boldsymbol{c}_o[n] + \boldsymbol{r}[n] \big) + z_{q}[n],
\end{aligned}
\right.
\end{equation}
where $z_{q}[n] \sim \mathcal{CN}(0, \sigma_{q}^2)$ denotes the AWGN at warden $q$.
The signals received at warden $q$ over the $N$ time slots are stacked into the observation vector $\boldsymbol{y}_{q} = [y_{q}[1], \ldots, y_{q}[N]]^T \in \mathbb{C}^{N\times 1}$.
Given that the received samples are independent and identically distributed across time slots, the probability density functions (PDFs) of $\boldsymbol{y}_{q}$ under two hypotheses are respectively given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:warden_pdf1}
\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_1\right) \!=\! \frac{\exp\!\left(- \frac{\|\boldsymbol{y}_{q}\|^2}{\Lambda_{1,q}(\boldsymbol{x})}\right)}{\pi^N \Lambda_{1,q}^N(\boldsymbol{x})},
\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_0\right) \!=\! \frac{\exp\!\left(- \frac{\|\boldsymbol{y}_{q}\|^2}{\Lambda_{0,q}(\boldsymbol{x})}\right)}{\pi^N \Lambda_{0,q}^N(\boldsymbol{x})},
\end{equation}
where
$
\Lambda_{1,q}(\boldsymbol{x}) \triangleq \boldsymbol{h}_{q}^H(\boldsymbol{x}) \!\left(\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}} \boldsymbol{w}_k\boldsymbol{w}_k^H + \boldsymbol{R}_{\rm A}\right) \boldsymbol{h}_{q}(\boldsymbol{x}) + \sigma_{q}^2$, 
and $\Lambda_{0,q}(\boldsymbol{x}) \triangleq \boldsymbol{h}_{q}^H(\boldsymbol{x}) \!\left(\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_o} \boldsymbol{w}_k\boldsymbol{w}_k^H + \boldsymbol{R}_{\rm A}\right) \boldsymbol{h}_{q}(\boldsymbol{x}) + \sigma_{q}^2$.
Denote by $\mathcal{D}_{1,q}$ and $\mathcal{D}_{0,q}$ the binary decisions of warden $q$ in support of $\mathcal{H}_1$ and $\mathcal{H}_0$, respectively.
Following the work in \cite{Ran1}, we assume that each warden uses classical hypothesis testing with equal prior probabilities for the two hypotheses.
The DEP at warden $q$ is then given by $\xi_q = \mathbb{P}_{{\rm MD},q} + \mathbb{P}_{{\rm FA},q}$, where
$\mathbb{P}_{{\rm MD},q} \triangleq \mathbb{P}\left(\mathcal{D}_{0,q}|\mathcal{H}_1\right)$ and
$\mathbb{P}_{{\rm FA},q} \triangleq \mathbb{P}\left(\mathcal{D}_{1,q}|\mathcal{H}_0\right)$
are the missed detection and false alarm probabilities, respectively.
To minimize the DEP $\xi_q$, warden $q$ performs the likelihood ratio test between $\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_1\right)$ and $\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_0\right)$.
The likelihood ratio test therefore compares the received power with a threshold, i.e.,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:LRT}
\frac{\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_1\right)}{\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_0\right)}
\underset{\mathcal{D}_{0,q}}{\overset{\mathcal{D}_{1,q}}{\gtrless}} 1
\Rightarrow
\|\boldsymbol{y}_{q}\|^2
\underset{\mathcal{D}_{0,q}}{\overset{\mathcal{D}_{1,q}}{\gtrless}}
N\frac{\Lambda_{0,q}\Lambda_{1,q}}{\Lambda_{1,q}-\Lambda_{0,q}}
\ln\frac{\Lambda_{1,q}}{\Lambda_{0,q}}
\triangleq \vartheta_q^{*}.
\end{equation}
Accordingly, the optimal detection rule in \eqref{eq:LRT} can be implemented using an energy detector.
Under hypothesis $\mathcal{H}_i$, $\|\boldsymbol{y}_{q}\|^2$ is the sum of $N$ independent exponential random variables, each with mean $\Lambda_{i,q}$, and thus follows a Gamma distribution. The two error probabilities are obtained from its cumulative distribution function, and the minimum DEP is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:min_DEP}
\begin{aligned}
\xi_q^*
&= \mathbb{P}\!\left\{\|\boldsymbol{y}_{q}\|^2 \le \vartheta_q^{*}\big|\mathcal{H}_1\right\}
+ \mathbb{P}\!\left\{\|\boldsymbol{y}_{q}\|^2 \ge \vartheta_q^{*}\big|\mathcal{H}_0\right\}

&= 1 - \underbrace{\left[
\frac{\gamma_{\rm inc}\!\left(N,\vartheta_q^{*}/\Lambda_{0,q}\right)}{\Gamma(N)}
-
\frac{\gamma_{\rm inc}\!\left(N,\vartheta_q^{*}/\Lambda_{1,q}\right)}{\Gamma(N)}
\right]}_{
=\,\mathcal{V}_{T}(\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_0\right),\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_1\right))},
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\gamma_{\rm inc}(N,x) \triangleq \int_0^x e^{-t}t^{N-1}\mathrm{d}t$ is the lower incomplete Gamma function, $\Gamma(N) = (N-1)!$ is the Gamma function, and $\mathcal{V}_{T}(\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_0\right),\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_1\right))$ is the total variation distance between the PDFs $\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_0\right)$ and $\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_1\right)$.
Based on the detection performance in \eqref{eq:min_DEP}, the covertness requirement at warden $q$ is expressed as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:DEP_constraint}
\xi_q^* = 1 - \mathcal{V}_{T}(\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_0\right),\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_1\right))\ge 1 - \epsilon, \forall q \in \mathcal{Q},
\end{equation}
where $\epsilon \in [0, 1]$ is the given covertness level, and a smaller $\epsilon$ means a stricter covertness requirement. In particular, when $\epsilon = 0$, we have $\xi_q^* = 1$, in which case warden $q$ cannot do better than a random guess.
The expression for $\xi_q^*$ is difficult to handle directly because it involves the lower incomplete Gamma functions in \eqref{eq:min_DEP}.
Although the exact DEP constraint can be expressed using a numerically computed power-ratio threshold, we follow~\cite{Yan2019Gaussian} and adopt the KL-based sufficient condition for its simple analytical form and explicit covertness guarantee.
Specifically, we use a lower bound on $\xi_q^*$ based on the Kullback--Leibler (KL) divergence, given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:KL_div}
\begin{aligned}
&\mathcal{D}(\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_1\right)\!\|\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_0\right)) \!=\! \int \!\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_1\right)
\ln\!\left(\frac{\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_1\right)}{\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_0\right)}\right)\mathrm{d}\boldsymbol{y}_{q}
&=\! N\!\left[\!\frac{\Lambda_{1,q}(\boldsymbol{x})}{\Lambda_{0,q}(\boldsymbol{x})} \!-\! 1 \!-\! \ln\!\frac{\Lambda_{1,q}(\boldsymbol{x})}{\Lambda_{0,q}(\boldsymbol{x})}\!\right]\!=\! N\!f\!\left(\!\frac{\Lambda_{1,q}(\boldsymbol{x}) - \Lambda_{0,q}(\boldsymbol{x})}{\Lambda_{0,q}(\boldsymbol{x})}\!\right)\!,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $f(x) \triangleq x - \ln(1+x)$ is monotonically increasing for $x \ge 0$.
According to \emph{Pinsker's inequality}, we have
\begin{equation}\label{eq:pinsker}
\mathcal{V}_{T}(\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_0\right),\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_1\right))\le \sqrt{\frac{\mathcal{D}(\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_1\right)\|\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_0\right))}{2}}.
\end{equation}
Therefore, \eqref{eq:DEP_constraint} holds as long as $\mathcal{D}(\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_1\right)\|\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_0\right)) \le 2\epsilon^2$.
By substituting the expressions of $\Lambda_{1,q}(\boldsymbol{x})$ and $\Lambda_{0,q}(\boldsymbol{x})$ into the argument of $f$, we have
\begin{equation}
\frac{\Lambda_{1,q}(\boldsymbol{x}) - \Lambda_{0,q}(\boldsymbol{x})}{\Lambda_{0,q}(\boldsymbol{x})}
= \frac{\boldsymbol{h}_{q}^H(\boldsymbol{x}) \!\left(\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_c}\boldsymbol{w}_k \boldsymbol{w}_k^H\right)\! \boldsymbol{h}_{q}(\boldsymbol{x})}
{\Lambda_{0,q}(\boldsymbol{x})}.
\end{equation}
Based on the monotonicity of $f(x)$, the KL divergence condition
\(
\mathcal{D}(\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_1\right)\|\mathbb{P}\left(\boldsymbol{y}_{q}|\mathcal{H}_0\right)) \le 2\epsilon^2
\)
can be equivalently expressed as $(\Lambda_{1,q}(\boldsymbol{x})-\Lambda_{0,q}(\boldsymbol{x}))/\Lambda_{0,q}(\boldsymbol{x}) \le \eta$, where $\eta \ge 0$ is the unique solution of $f(\eta) = 2\epsilon^2/N$.
Rearranging the resulting inequality, we obtain the following equivalent quadratic form of the KL divergence condition at each warden, given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:covert_compact}
\boldsymbol{h}_{q}^H(\boldsymbol{x}) \, \bm\Xi \, \boldsymbol{h}_{q}(\boldsymbol{x}) \le \eta \, \sigma_{q}^2, \forall q \in \mathcal{Q},
\end{equation}
where
$\bm\Xi\!\triangleq\!\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_c}\!\!\boldsymbol{w}_k \boldsymbol{w}_k^H\!-\!\eta\!\left(\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_o}\!\!\boldsymbol{w}_k\boldsymbol{w}_k^H \!+\! \boldsymbol{R}_{\rm A}\right)\!\in\!\mathbb{C}^{M\times M}.$
\subsection{Imperfect CSI Models}
Due to the absence of pilot transmissions from warden $q$, Alice cannot estimate $\boldsymbol{h}_{q}(\boldsymbol{x})$ from reference signals~\cite{ForouzeshCDI}. In practice, the AoD $\theta_q$ is unlikely to be perfectly known, and the channel gain $\beta_q$ is also subject to estimation errors. Both factors lead to imperfect warden CSI in \eqref{eq:warden_channel}. Accordingly, the warden channel is expressed as the sum of its estimated value and estimation error, i.e.,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:CSI_error_model}
\boldsymbol{h}_{q}(\boldsymbol{x}) = \bar{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol{x}) + \Delta\boldsymbol h_q, \forall q\in\mathcal{Q},
\end{equation}
where $\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol{x})$ denotes the nominal warden channel determined by the estimated AoD and channel gain, and $\Delta\boldsymbol h_q$ is the corresponding CSI error.
In the following, we introduce two CSI error models.
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Bounded CSI error}: The squared norm of the CSI error is bounded by the known constant $e_q^2$, i.e.,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bounded_CSI_error}
\Delta\boldsymbol h_q \in \mathcal{W}_q, \forall q\in\mathcal Q,
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal W_q \triangleq \{\Delta\boldsymbol h_q | \|\Delta\boldsymbol h_q\|^2 \le e_q^2\}$.
\item \emph{Statistical CSI error}: The CSI error vector is characterized by a zero-mean complex Gaussian distribution with a known covariance matrix, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
\Delta\boldsymbol h_q \sim \mathcal{CN}(\bm{0}, \boldsymbol{E}_{q}), \forall q\in\mathcal Q,
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol{E}_{q}\succeq \boldsymbol{0}$ denotes the CSI error covariance matrix known at Alice.
\end{itemize}
\section{Worst-case Robust Beamforming}\label{sec:wc_csi_design}
In this section, we first formulate the robust optimization problem under the bounded CSI error model and develop a BCD-based algorithm incorporating bisection search, SDR, and SCA methods to solve the intractable problem.
\subsection{Problem Formulation}
We aim to maximize the minimum weighted SINR among all legitimate users subject to the covertness constraints at all wardens, the transmit power budget, and the MA position constraints. Specifically, the optimization problem can be given by
\begin{subequations}\label{p1}
\begin{align}
\mathmakebox[2em][r]{\underset{\mathclap{\tau,\boldsymbol{w}_k,\boldsymbol{R}_{\rm A},\boldsymbol{x}}}{\max}}\; & \quad\tau 
\mathmakebox[2em][r]{\text{s.t.}}\;
&\quad \gamma_{1,k}^C(\boldsymbol{w}_k,\boldsymbol{R}_{\rm A},\boldsymbol{x}) \geq \nu_k\tau,\forall k\in\mathcal{K} \label{Const_1}
&\quad \boldsymbol{h}_{q}^H(\boldsymbol{x}) \, \bm\Xi \, \boldsymbol{h}_{q}(\boldsymbol{x}) \le \eta \, \sigma_{q}^2,\Delta\boldsymbol h_q \in \mathcal{W}_q, \forall q\in\mathcal{Q},\label{Const_2}
&\quad \sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}}(\boldsymbol{w}_k^H\boldsymbol{w}_k) + \mathrm{Tr}(\boldsymbol{R}_{\rm A}) \leq P, \label{Const_3}
&\quad x_{m+1}-x_m \geq x_{th}, \forall m\in\mathcal{M}\setminus\{M\}, \label{Const_4}
&\quad x_m\in \mathcal{X},\forall m\in\mathcal{M}, \label{Const_5}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
where $\nu_k>0$ in \eqref{Const_1} is the priority weight of user $k$, $P$ in \eqref{Const_3} denotes Alice's transmit power budget, and \eqref{Const_4} enforces a minimum spacing of $x_{th} \ge 0$ between transmit MAs to avoid coupling.
Since overt transmission also provides cover for covert transmission, we set $\nu_k=1$ for overt users $k\in\mathcal K_o$ and $\nu_k=\nu$ for covert users $k\in\mathcal K_c$, where $\nu>0$ controls the priority of covert users relative to overt users.
For a given $\tau$, a larger $\nu$ requires a higher SINR for covert users.
The formulated problem is difficult to solve directly due to the coupling among the beamforming, AN covariance matrix, and MA positions in the quality-of-service (QoS) and covertness constraints.
To this end, we propose a BCD-based algorithm by alternately optimizing the beamforming and AN covariance matrix via semidefinite relaxation and a bisection search over the SINR threshold, and updating MA positions via the SCA technique.
\subsection{Optimizing Beamforming and AN Covariance Matrix}
With the MA positions being fixed, we focus on optimizing the transmit beamforming vectors $\boldsymbol{w}_k$ and the AN covariance matrix $\boldsymbol{R}_{\rm A}$. The non-convexity of problem \eqref{p1} lies in the quadratic terms with respect to (w.r.t.) $\boldsymbol{w}_k$ in \eqref{Const_1} and \eqref{Const_2}. 
To deal with this issue, we employ the SDR method~\cite{wtl_noma_isac}. 
Towards this end, we define $\boldsymbol{W}_k \triangleq \boldsymbol{w}_k\boldsymbol{w}_k^H$ and impose $\boldsymbol{W}_k\succeq\boldsymbol0$ and $\mathrm{rank}(\boldsymbol{W}_k)=1$, $\forall k\in\mathcal K$. Then, \eqref{Const_1} can be reformulated as
\begin{equation}
\frac{\boldsymbol{h}_k^H(\boldsymbol{x}) \boldsymbol{W}_k \boldsymbol{h}_k(\boldsymbol{x})}
{\boldsymbol{h}_k^H(\boldsymbol{x})(\boldsymbol{R} - \boldsymbol{W}_k)\boldsymbol{h}_k(\boldsymbol{x}) + \sigma_k^2} \ge \nu_k\tau,
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol{R} = \sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}} \boldsymbol{W}_k + \boldsymbol{R}_{\rm A}$, and $\nu_k$ remain constants on the right-hand side (RHS).
To tackle semi-infinite constraints introduced by channel uncertainty in \eqref{Const_2}, we introduce the following lemma.
\begin{lemma}[S-Procedure]\phantomsection\label{lem:S_procedure}
\it Define the quadratic function $f_m(\bm x)$ w.r.t.\ $\bm x\in\mathbb{C}^{M\times 1}$ as
\begin{equation*}
f_m(\bm x) = \bm x^H \mathbf{A}_m \bm x + 2\,\Re\{\mathbf{b}_m^H \bm x\} + c_m,\; m=1,2,
\end{equation*}
where $\mathbf{A}_m \in \mathbb{C}^{M\times M}$, $\mathbf{b}_m \in \mathbb{C}^{M\times 1}$, and $c_m \in \mathbb{R}$.
The condition $f_1(\bm x) \le 0 \Rightarrow f_2(\bm x)\le 0$ holds if and only if there exists $\omega \ge 0$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:S_proc_LMI}
\omega\!\begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{A}_1 & \mathbf{b}_1 \mathbf{b}_1^H & c_1\end{bmatrix}
- \begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{A}_2 & \mathbf{b}_2 \mathbf{b}_2^H & c_2\end{bmatrix}
\succeq \mathbf{0}_{M+1}.
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}
By substituting \eqref{eq:CSI_error_model} into (\ref{Const_2}), we have 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:covert_constraint_bounded_expanded}
\begin{aligned}
\Delta\boldsymbol h_q^H \bm\Xi \Delta\boldsymbol h_q +& 2\Re\{\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q^H \bm\Xi \Delta\boldsymbol h_q\} 
&\quad+ \bar{\boldsymbol h}_q^H \bm\Xi \bar{\boldsymbol h}_q - \eta \sigma_q^2 \le 0, \forall q\in\mathcal{Q}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Note that \eqref{eq:bounded_CSI_error} leads to $\Delta\boldsymbol h_q^H \Delta\boldsymbol h_q \le e_q^2$, $\forall q\in\mathcal Q$.
Lemma~\ref{lem:S_procedure} is employed to tackle the CSI uncertainty in the covertness constraint in~\eqref{eq:covert_constraint_bounded_expanded}. 
Specifically, the constraint corresponding to each warden in \eqref{Const_2} can be recast as follows
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\mathbf{A}_1 = \mathbf{I}_M, \mathbf{b}_1 = \mathbf{0}, c_1 = -e_q^2, & \mathbf{A}_2 = \bm\Xi, \mathbf{b}_2 = \bm\Xi\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol x),
c_2 = \bar{\boldsymbol h}_q^H(\boldsymbol x)\bm\Xi\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol x) - \eta\sigma_{q}^2.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Then, \eqref{Const_2} is equivalently expressed as the following LMI
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Sproc_lmi}
\begin{aligned}
&\bm\Theta_q(\boldsymbol x,\{\boldsymbol W_k\},\boldsymbol R,\omega_q)
\triangleq &
\begin{bmatrix}
\omega_q\mathbf{I}_M-\bm\Xi
&-\bm\Xi\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol x)
-\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q^H(\boldsymbol x)\bm\Xi
&\eta\sigma_{q}^2-
\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q^H(\boldsymbol x)\bm\Xi\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol x)-\omega_q e_{q}^2
\end{bmatrix}
\succeq\boldsymbol0,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol{\omega}=[\omega_1,\ldots,\omega_Q]^{\mathrm T}\ge\boldsymbol0$ is the vector of slack variables and $\bm\Xi=(1+\eta)\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_c}\boldsymbol{W}_k-\eta\boldsymbol{R}$.
Then, for any given $\tau\ge0$, the beamforming and AN covariance matrix subproblem in~\eqref{p1} can be reformulated as the following feasibility problem, i.e.,
\begin{subequations}\label{p2}
\begin{align}
\mathmakebox[0pt][r]{\text{find}}\; & \quad\boldsymbol{W}_k,\boldsymbol{R},\boldsymbol{\omega}
\mathmakebox[0pt][r]{\text{s.t.}}\;
&\quad\frac{\boldsymbol{h}_k^H(\boldsymbol{x}) \boldsymbol{W}_k \boldsymbol{h}_k(\boldsymbol{x})}
{\boldsymbol{h}_k^H(\boldsymbol{x}) (\boldsymbol{R} \!-\!  \boldsymbol{W}_k) \boldsymbol{h}_k(\boldsymbol{x}) \!+\! \sigma_k^2} \geq \nu_k\tau, \forall k\in\mathcal{K},\label{Const_p2_sinr}
&\quad\bm\Theta_q(\boldsymbol x,\boldsymbol W_k,\boldsymbol R,\boldsymbol\omega) \succeq \boldsymbol0,\boldsymbol\omega\ge 0,\label{Const_p2_covert_lmi}
&\quad\mathrm{Tr}(\boldsymbol{R}) \le P,\label{Const_p2_power}
&\quad\boldsymbol{R}-\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}}\boldsymbol{W}_k\succeq \boldsymbol{0},\boldsymbol{W}_k \succeq \boldsymbol{0}, \forall k\in\mathcal{K},\label{Const_p2_split}
&\quad\mathrm{rank}(\boldsymbol{W}_k) = 1, \forall k\in\mathcal{K}.\label{Const_p2_rank}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
Omitting the non-convex rank-one constraints \eqref{Const_p2_rank}, we obtain a relaxed version of the above problem.
Thus, the beamforming and AN covariance matrix optimization reduces to a series of semidefinite programming (SDP) feasibility subproblems, which can be solved using the CVX toolbox~\cite{GrantCVX2014}.
Note that for any fixed $\tau\ge0$, the relaxed problem~\eqref{p2} is an SDP.
Therefore, the corresponding relaxed weighted max-min SINR problem is quasi-concave and can be solved by a bisection search over $\tau$.
The details of the bisection search method are summarized in Algorithm~\ref{alg:robust_bisection}.
Let $\{\overline{\boldsymbol{W}}_k\}_{k=1}^{K}$ and $\overline{\boldsymbol{R}}$ denote a feasible solution of the relaxed SDP for a given $\tau>0$.
Here, we note that if $\mathrm{rank}(\overline{\boldsymbol{W}}_k)=1$ holds for all $k\in\mathcal K$, the solution to the relaxed problem is also feasible for the original rank-constrained problem~\eqref{p2}.
Otherwise, we can reconstruct a rank-one feasible solution in closed form that attains the same SINR threshold.
Specifically, the beamforming vectors $\boldsymbol{w}_k^\star$, the rank-one matrices $\boldsymbol{W}_k^\star$, and the corresponding AN covariance matrix $\boldsymbol{R}_{\rm A}^\star$ can be reconstructed as follows
\begin{equation}\label{SDR_optimal}
\begin{aligned}
&\boldsymbol{R}^\star = \overline{\boldsymbol{R}},\boldsymbol{w}_k^\star = (\boldsymbol{h}_k^H(\boldsymbol{x})\overline{\boldsymbol{W}}_k\boldsymbol{h}_k(\boldsymbol{x}))^{-1/2}\overline{\boldsymbol{W}}_k\boldsymbol{h}_k(\boldsymbol{x}), 
  &\boldsymbol{W}_k^\star = \boldsymbol{w}_k^\star\boldsymbol{w}_k^{\star H}, \boldsymbol{R}_{\rm A}^\star = \boldsymbol{R}^\star - \sum_{k=1}^{K}\boldsymbol{W}_k^\star.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The proof is provided in Appendix \ref{AppSDR}.
\subsection{Optimizing Auxiliary Variables}\label{subsec:auxiliary_update}
With other variables being fixed, we focus on the optimization of the auxiliary variables.
For each user, we introduce an auxiliary variable $\alpha_k\in\mathbb C$ and apply the quadratic transform as follows
\begin{equation}\label{eq:qt_position_sinr}
\begin{aligned}
\tilde\gamma_{1,k}^C(\boldsymbol x,\alpha_k)
=&2\Re\{\alpha_k^*\boldsymbol h_k^H(\boldsymbol x)\boldsymbol w_k\}
&-|\alpha_k|^2\left(\boldsymbol h_k^H(\boldsymbol x)\boldsymbol\Xi_k\boldsymbol h_k(\boldsymbol x)+\sigma_k^2\right),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol\Xi_k\triangleq \sum_{i\in\mathcal K\setminus\{k\}}\boldsymbol W_i+\boldsymbol{R}_{\rm A}$.
$\tilde\gamma_{1,k}^C(\boldsymbol x,\alpha_k)$ is concave w.r.t.\ $\alpha_k$, and its optimal auxiliary variable has the closed-form solution
\begin{equation}\label{eq:alpha_update_position}
\alpha_k^\star
=\frac{\boldsymbol h_k^H(\boldsymbol x)\boldsymbol w_k}
{\boldsymbol h_k^H(\boldsymbol x)\boldsymbol\Xi_k\boldsymbol h_k(\boldsymbol x)+\sigma_k^2},
\quad\forall k\in\mathcal K.
\end{equation}
\subsection{Optimizing Transmit Antenna Positions}\label{subsec:MA_position_update_wc}
With $\boldsymbol W_k$, $\boldsymbol{R}_{\rm A}$, and $\alpha_k$ fixed, we jointly optimize the MA positions $\boldsymbol x$ and the SINR threshold $\tau$.
Accordingly, the MA position subproblem can be formulated as
\begin{subequations}\label{p3_raw}
\begin{align}
\mathmakebox[2em][r]{\underset{\mathclap{\boldsymbol x,\tau}}{\max}}\;
&\quad\tau

\mathmakebox[2em][r]{\text{s.t.}}\;
&\quad\gamma_{1,k}^C(\boldsymbol x)\ge \nu_k\tau,\quad \forall k\in\mathcal K,
\label{Const_p3_raw_sinr}
&\quad\left(\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol x)+\bm\delta_q\right)^H
\bm\Xi
\left(\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol x)+\bm\delta_q\right)
\le \eta\sigma_q^2, \notag
&\quad\bm\delta_q^H\bm\delta_q \le e_q^2,\ \forall q\in\mathcal Q,
\label{Const_p3_raw_covert}
&\quad(\ref{Const_4}),(\ref{Const_5})
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
where $\bm\Xi
=(1+\eta)\sum_{k\in\mathcal K_c}\boldsymbol W_k
-\eta\boldsymbol{R}$ and $\bm\delta_q$ denotes the channel estimation error $\Delta\boldsymbol h_q$ under the bounded CSI error model in \eqref{eq:bounded_CSI_error}.
It is worth noting that the non-convexity of problem~\eqref{p3_raw} lies in the SINR constraint \eqref{Const_p3_raw_sinr} and the covertness constraint \eqref{Const_p3_raw_covert}.
To tackle this challenge, we employ SCA to construct a sequence of convex subproblems.

To approximate the two terms involving $\boldsymbol x$ in \eqref{eq:qt_position_sinr} separately, we define $a_k(\boldsymbol x)$ and $b_k(\boldsymbol x)$ as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ak_position_def}
a_k(\boldsymbol x)
\triangleq
2\Re\{\alpha_k^*\boldsymbol h_k^H(\boldsymbol x)\boldsymbol w_k\},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bk_position_def}
b_k(\boldsymbol x)
\triangleq
|\alpha_k|^2\boldsymbol h_k^H(\boldsymbol x)\boldsymbol\Xi_k\boldsymbol h_k(\boldsymbol x).
\end{equation}
To construct a concave surrogate function for the SINR in \eqref{eq:qt_position_sinr}, we introduce the following lemma.
\begin{lemma}[Global Lower Bound Approximation]\phantomsection\label{lem:global_lower_bound}
\it Let $f(\boldsymbol x):\mathbb R^N\to\mathbb R$ denote a continuously differentiable function with bounded curvature, i.e., there exists a positive real number $\mu$ such that $\mu\ge\|\nabla^2 f(\boldsymbol x)\|_2$ for any $\boldsymbol x$.
Define $f_2(\boldsymbol x)=-f(\boldsymbol x)$.
Then, for all $\boldsymbol x,\boldsymbol y\in\mathbb R^N$, the following inequality holds
\begin{equation}\label{eq:global_lower_bound}
f_2(\boldsymbol x)
\ge{} f_2(\boldsymbol y)+\nabla f_2(\boldsymbol y)^T(\boldsymbol x-\boldsymbol y)
-\frac{1}{2}\mu(\boldsymbol x-\boldsymbol y)^T(\boldsymbol x-\boldsymbol y).
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}
\textit{Proof:} See Appendix~\ref{AppGlobalLowerBound} for the proof.
Based on Lemma~\ref{lem:global_lower_bound}, we approximate $a_k(\boldsymbol x)$ and $b_k(\boldsymbol x)$ at $\boldsymbol x^{(t)}$ by constructing a concave lower bound and a convex upper bound, respectively.
Specifically, by applying the lemma with $f_2=a_k$ and $f_2=-b_k$, we can obtain
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ak_under_position}
\begin{aligned}
a_k(\boldsymbol x)
\ge{} a_k(\boldsymbol x^{(t)})
&+\nabla a_k(\boldsymbol x^{(t)})^T(\boldsymbol x-\boldsymbol x^{(t)})

&-\frac{\varpi_{a,k}}{2}\|\boldsymbol x-\boldsymbol x^{(t)}\|_2^2
\triangleq
\underline a_k(\boldsymbol x),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bk_over_position}
\begin{aligned}
b_k(\boldsymbol x)
\le{} b_k(\boldsymbol x^{(t)})
&+\nabla b_k(\boldsymbol x^{(t)})^T(\boldsymbol x-\boldsymbol x^{(t)})

&+\frac{\varpi_{b,k}}{2}\|\boldsymbol x-\boldsymbol x^{(t)}\|_2^2
\triangleq \bar b_k(\boldsymbol x),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\nabla a_k(\boldsymbol x)$ and $\nabla b_k(\boldsymbol x)$ denote the gradient vectors of $a_k(\boldsymbol x)$ and $b_k(\boldsymbol x)$ w.r.t.\ $\boldsymbol x$, respectively.
The nonnegative constants $\varpi_{a,k}$ and $\varpi_{b,k}$ satisfy $\varpi_{a,k}\ge\|\nabla^2a_k(\boldsymbol x)\|_2$ and $\varpi_{b,k}\ge\|\nabla^2b_k(\boldsymbol x)\|_2$ for all $\boldsymbol x$, where $\nabla^2a_k(\boldsymbol x)$ and $\nabla^2b_k(\boldsymbol x)$ are the corresponding Hessian matrices.
At $\boldsymbol x^{(t)}$, $\underline a_k$ and $\bar b_k$ match the values and gradients of $a_k$ and $b_k$, respectively, and the curvature bounds ensure their global validity.
The expressions of $\varpi_{a,k}$ and $\varpi_{b,k}$ are derived in Appendix~\ref{AppSINR_a}.
Combining \eqref{eq:ak_under_position} and \eqref{eq:bk_over_position}, we obtain the concave SINR lower bound as
$
\widehat\gamma_{1,k}^C(\boldsymbol x)
\triangleq
\underline a_k(\boldsymbol x)
-
\bar b_k(\boldsymbol x)
-|\alpha_k|^2\sigma_k^2
\le
\tilde\gamma_{1,k}^C(\boldsymbol x,\alpha_k).
$
The resulting conservative convex constraint is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sinr_sca_constraint_position}
\widehat\gamma_{1,k}^C(\boldsymbol x)\ge\nu_k\tau,
\quad \forall k\in\mathcal K.
\end{equation}
Next, we construct a convex approximation of the covertness constraint~\eqref{Const_2}. By substituting $\boldsymbol h_q(\boldsymbol x)=\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol x)+\bm\delta_q$ into the left-hand side (LHS) of \eqref{Const_2}, we can obtain the equivalent form
\begin{equation}\label{eq:covert_constraint_expanded_position}
\bm\delta_q^H\bm\Xi\bm\delta_q
+
\underbrace{\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q^H(\boldsymbol x)\bm\Xi\bm\delta_q
+
\bm\delta_q^H\bm\Xi\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol x)}_{\triangleq\mu_q(\boldsymbol x,\bm\delta_q)}
+
\underbrace{\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q^H(\boldsymbol x)\bm\Xi\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol x)}_{\triangleq\varkappa_q(\boldsymbol x)}
\le \eta\sigma_q^2.
\end{equation}
Using the uniform Hessian bound $\varpi_{\mu,q}$, we construct the following convex quadratic upper bound on $\mu_q(\boldsymbol x,\bm\delta_q)$
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mu_upper_two_part_app}
\begin{aligned}
\mu_q(\boldsymbol{x},\bm\delta_q)
\le
\mu_q&(\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)},\bm\delta_q)
+ \nabla_{\boldsymbol{x}}\mu_q(\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)},\bm\delta_q)^T
(\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)})

& + \frac{\varpi_{\mu,q}}{2}
\|\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)}\|_2^2 \triangleq \bar{\mu}_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol{x},\bm\delta_q),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\varpi_{\mu,q}$ is given in Appendix~\ref{AppCovertSCA}.
Similarly, $\varkappa_q(\boldsymbol x)$ can be upper-bounded as follows
\begin{equation}\label{eq:varkappa_q_upper_bound}
\begin{aligned}
\varkappa_q(\boldsymbol x)\le
\varkappa_q(\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)})
&+ \nabla_{\boldsymbol{x}}\varkappa_q(\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)})^T
(\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)})
&
+ \frac{\varpi_{\varkappa,q}}{2}
\|\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)}\|_2^2 \triangleq \bar{\varkappa}_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol{x}),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\varpi_{\varkappa,q}$ is given in Appendix~\ref{AppSINR_a}.
Then, substituting \eqref{eq:mu_upper_two_part_app} and \eqref{eq:varkappa_q_upper_bound} into the LHS of \eqref{eq:covert_constraint_expanded_position}, we have
\begin{equation}\label{eq:covert_sca_expanded_position}
\bm\delta_q^H\bm\Xi\bm\delta_q
+
\bar{\mu}_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol{x},\bm\delta_q)
+\bar{\varkappa}_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol{x})
-\eta\sigma_q^2
\le 0.
\end{equation}
We rewrite $\nabla_{\boldsymbol{x}}\mu_q(\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)},\bm\delta_q)^T(\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)})$ as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mu_linear_compact_app}
\nabla_{\boldsymbol{x}}\mu_q(\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)},\bm\delta_q)^T
(\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)}) \!=\!
\boldsymbol z_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol{x})^H\bm\delta_q
\!+\!
\bm\delta_q^H\boldsymbol z_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol{x}),
\end{equation}
where
$ \boldsymbol z_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol{x})
\triangleq
\left[
\boldsymbol r_{q,1}^H(x_1^{(t)}),\,
\ldots,\,
\boldsymbol r_{q,M}^H(x_M^{(t)})
\right]
(\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)}).$
Hence, \eqref{eq:covert_sca_expanded_position} can be written in the following tractable form
\begin{equation}\label{eq:covert_sca_explicit_app}
\bm\delta_q^H\bm\Xi\bm\delta_q
\!+\!
\bm\delta_q^H
\boldsymbol c_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol{x})
\!+\!
\boldsymbol c_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol{x})^H\bm\delta_q
\!+\! \upsilon_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol{x}) \!-\! \eta\sigma_q^2
\le 0,
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol c_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol{x}) \triangleq \bm\Xi\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)}) + \boldsymbol z_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol{x})$, and
$\upsilon_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol{x})
= \varkappa_q(\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)})
+ \nabla_{\boldsymbol{x}}\varkappa_q(\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)})^T
(\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)})
+\frac{\varpi_{\varkappa,q}+\varpi_{\mu,q}}{2}\|\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)}\|_2^2$.
The upper bounds in \eqref{eq:mu_upper_two_part_app} and \eqref{eq:varkappa_q_upper_bound} are globally valid and match the function values and position gradients at $\boldsymbol x^{(t)}$. Thus, \eqref{eq:covert_sca_explicit_app} implies \eqref{eq:covert_constraint_expanded_position} for every $\|\bm\delta_q\|_2\le e_q$ and retains the current feasible position.
By the S-procedure, \eqref{eq:covert_sca_explicit_app} is equivalent to the existence of $\omega_q\ge0$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:position_covert_sca_lmi_main}
\begin{bmatrix}
\omega_q\mathbf{I}_M-\bm\Xi
&
-\boldsymbol c_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol x)
-\boldsymbol c_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol x)^H
&
\eta\sigma_q^2-\upsilon_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol x)-\omega_q e_q^2
\end{bmatrix}
\succeq\boldsymbol0.
\end{equation}
The matrix inequality in \eqref{eq:position_covert_sca_lmi_main} is still nonlinear w.r.t.\ $\boldsymbol x$ due to the quadratic term in $\upsilon_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol x)$.
To obtain an LMI formulation, we introduce the following lemma.
\begin{lemma}\phantomsection\label{lem:lmi_brc_relaxation}
\it For any complex Hermitian matrix $\boldsymbol A\in\mathbb{C}^{N\times N}$, $\boldsymbol b\in\mathbb{C}^{N\times 1}$, $c\in\mathbb R$, and $d\in\mathbb R$, if
\begin{equation}
\begin{bmatrix}
\boldsymbol A & \boldsymbol b
\boldsymbol b^H & c
\end{bmatrix}\succeq\boldsymbol 0
\quad\text{and}\quad d\ge c,
\end{equation}
then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:brc_lmi_impl}
\begin{bmatrix}
\boldsymbol A & \boldsymbol b
\boldsymbol b^H & d
\end{bmatrix}\succeq\boldsymbol 0
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}
By applying Lemma~\ref{lem:lmi_brc_relaxation}, the matrix inequality \eqref{eq:position_covert_sca_lmi_main} is equivalently reformulated as the following LMI together with the auxiliary constraint \eqref{eq:48}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:position_covert_sca_lmi_relaxed}
\begin{aligned}
\bm\Theta_{q,x}^{(t)}(\boldsymbol x,\omega_q,\iota_q)
\!\triangleq\!  
\begin{bmatrix}
\omega_q\mathbf{I}_M-\bm\Xi & -\boldsymbol c_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol x)
-\boldsymbol c_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol x)^H & \eta\sigma_q^2-\iota_q-\omega_q e_q^2
\end{bmatrix}\!\succeq\!\boldsymbol 0,
\forall q\in\mathcal Q,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\iota_q$ is an auxiliary variable satisfying 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:48}
\iota_q \ge \upsilon_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol x).
\end{equation}
Based on the above derivations, a convex approximation of problem~\eqref{p3_raw} is formulated as
\begin{subequations}\label{p3_sca}
\begin{align}
\mathmakebox[2em][r]{\underset{\mathclap{\boldsymbol x,\tau,\{\omega_q,\iota_q\}_{q\in\mathcal Q}}}{\max}}\;
& \quad\tau

\mathmakebox[2em][r]{\text{s.t.}}\;
&\quad (\ref{Const_4}),(\ref{Const_5}),\eqref{eq:sinr_sca_constraint_position},\eqref{eq:position_covert_sca_lmi_relaxed},\eqref{eq:48},
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
which is convex and can be optimally solved using the CVX toolbox.
\begin{proposition}\phantomsection\label{prop:bcd_wc_monotonicity}
The value of the objective function in Problem~\eqref{p3_sca} is non-decreasing with iterations, and Algorithm~\ref{alg:robust_bcd} converges.
\end{proposition}
\noindent\textit{Proof.} Please refer to Appendix~\ref{AppBCDWC}.
\section{Outage-Constrained Robust Beamforming under Statistical CSI Errors}\label{sec:stat_csi_design}
In practice, the worst-case optimization scheme may be overly conservative, since it requires the covertness constraint to hold for all CSI error realizations in the uncertainty set regardless of their statistical distribution. To address this issue, in this section, we focus on the statistical warden CSI error model and investigate the corresponding outage-constrained robust beamforming optimization. In the following, we first formulate the design problem and derive a deterministic approximation to the outage-constrained covertness constraint using the Bernstein-type inequality.
Then, we adopt the BCD framework to alternately optimize the beamforming, AN covariance matrix, and MA positions.
\subsection{Problem Formulation}
As in the worst-case design, we lift the transmit beamforming vectors into matrix variables. Let $\tau$ denote the SINR threshold. The optimization problem to maximize the minimum weighted SINR among all legitimate users is formulated as
\begin{subequations}\label{p2_stat}
\begin{align}
\mathmakebox[2em][r]{\underset{\mathclap{\tau,\boldsymbol W_k,\boldsymbol{R},\boldsymbol x}}{\max}}\; & \quad\tau

\mathmakebox[2em][r]{\text{s.t.}}\;
&\quad
\frac{\boldsymbol h_k^H(\boldsymbol{x}) \boldsymbol{W}_k \boldsymbol h_k(\boldsymbol{x})}
{\boldsymbol h_k^H(\boldsymbol{x}) (\boldsymbol{R}\!-\!\boldsymbol W_k)\boldsymbol h_k(\boldsymbol{x})+\sigma_k^2}
\ge \nu_k\tau,\forall k\in\mathcal K,
\label{Const_stat_sinr}
&\quad\mathrm{Pr}\!\left\{
{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol x)^H
\bm\Xi
{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol x)
\le \eta\sigma_q^2
\right\}
\ge 1-\varrho_q,\notag
&\quad\Delta\boldsymbol h_q \sim\mathcal{CN}(\boldsymbol 0,\boldsymbol E_q),
\forall q\in\mathcal Q,
\label{Const_stat_covert}
&\quad\mathrm{Tr}(\boldsymbol{R})\le P,
\label{Const_stat_power}
&\quad\boldsymbol{R}-\sum_{k\in\mathcal K}\boldsymbol W_k\succeq\boldsymbol 0,
\boldsymbol W_k\succeq\boldsymbol 0,
\forall k\in\mathcal K,
\label{Const_stat_RA_split}
&\quad\mathrm{rank}(\boldsymbol W_k)=1,
\quad \forall k\in\mathcal K,
\label{Const_stat_rank}
&\quad\eqref{Const_4},\eqref{Const_5}.
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
where $\varrho_q\in(0,1)$ denotes the maximum tolerable outage probability for the covertness requirement at warden $q$. Constraint \eqref{Const_stat_covert} represents the outage-constrained covertness requirement, ensuring that \eqref{eq:covert_compact} holds with probability at least $1-\varrho_q$. The probabilistic quadratic constraint~\eqref{Const_stat_covert} renders problem~\eqref{p2_stat} intractable.
\subsection{Optimizing Beamforming and AN Covariance Matrix}
To obtain a tractable formulation of \eqref{Const_stat_covert}, we use the following Bernstein-type inequality.
\begin{lemma}[Bernstein-Type Inequality]\phantomsection\label{lem:BTI_statCSI}
\it For any complex Hermitian matrix $\mathbf{A}\in\mathbb{C}^{M\times M}$, vector $\mathbf{b}\in\mathbb{C}^{M\times1}$, scalar $c\in\mathbb{R}$, random vector $\mathbf{x}\sim\mathcal{CN}(\bm0_{M\times1},\mathbf{I}_M)$, and $\rho\in(0,1)$, if there exist $x$ and $y$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:BTI_generic}
\begin{aligned}
&{\rm Tr}(\mathbf{A}) - \sqrt {2\ln \left(\frac{1}{\rho }\right)}x  + \ln(\rho )y + c \ge 0,&
\sqrt {\left\|\mathbf{A} \right\|_F^2 + 2{{\left\| \mathbf{b} \right\|}^2}}  \le x, &
y\mathbf{I}_M + \mathbf{A} \succeq \boldsymbol 0,y \ge 0,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
then
\begin{equation}
{\rm Pr}({\mathbf{x}^H}{\mathbf{A}}\mathbf{x} + 2{\mathop{\rm Re}\nolimits} \{ \mathbf{x}^H\mathbf{b}\}  + {c} \ge 0)\ge 1-\rho.
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}
Let $\boldsymbol d_q\sim\mathcal{CN}(\boldsymbol 0_{M \times 1},\mathbf{I}_M)$ and
represent the CSI error as
$\Delta\boldsymbol h_q=\boldsymbol E_q^{1/2}\boldsymbol d_q$,
which is valid since $\boldsymbol E_q\succeq\boldsymbol{0}_{M\times M}$ admits a
Hermitian positive-semidefinite square root $\boldsymbol E_q^{1/2}$.
Then, \eqref{Const_stat_covert} can be expressed as
\eqref{eq:transform_outage} at the bottom of this page.
\begin{figure*}[!b]
\hrulefill
\begin{equation}\label{eq:transform_outage}
\begin{aligned}
&\mathrm{Pr}\Bigl\{
- \left(\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol x)+\boldsymbol E_q^{1/2}\boldsymbol d_q\right)^H
\bm\Xi
\left(\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol x)+\boldsymbol E_q^{1/2}\boldsymbol d_q\right)
+\eta\sigma_q^2 \ge0
\Bigr\}
&=\mathrm{Pr}\Bigl\{-\boldsymbol d_q^H\left(\boldsymbol E_q^{1/2}\right)^H\bm\Xi\boldsymbol E_q^{1/2}\boldsymbol d_q
-2\Re\left\{\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q^H(\boldsymbol x)\bm\Xi\boldsymbol E_q^{1/2}\boldsymbol d_q\right\}
+\eta\sigma_q^2-
\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q^H(\boldsymbol x)\bm\Xi\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol x)\ge0\Bigr\}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
\end{figure*}
Therefore, the outage-constrained covertness requirement \eqref{Const_stat_covert} is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:stat_quad_form}
\mathrm{Pr}\left\{
\boldsymbol d_q^H\boldsymbol U_q\boldsymbol d_q
\!+\!2\Re\{\boldsymbol u_q^H(\boldsymbol x)\boldsymbol d_q\}
\!+\!u_q(\boldsymbol x)
\!\ge\!0
\right\}
\!\ge\!1\!-\!\varrho_q,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
&\boldsymbol U_q
\triangleq
-\left(\boldsymbol E_q^{1/2}\right)^H\bm\Xi\boldsymbol E_q^{1/2},\label{eq:stat_Uq_def}
&\boldsymbol u_q(\boldsymbol x)
\triangleq
-\left(\boldsymbol E_q^{1/2}\right)^H\bm\Xi\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol x),\label{eq:stat_uq_def}
&u_q(\boldsymbol x)
\triangleq
\eta\sigma_q^2-
\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q^H(\boldsymbol x)\bm\Xi\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol x) = 
\eta\sigma_q^2-\varkappa_q(\boldsymbol x).\label{eq:stat_scalar_def}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
According to Lemma~\ref{lem:BTI_statCSI}, sufficient deterministic conditions for the outage-constrained covertness requirement in \eqref{eq:stat_quad_form} are given by
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
&\mathrm{Tr}(\boldsymbol U_q)
\!-\!\sqrt{2\ln(1/\varrho_q)}\,\ell_q
\!-\!\ln(1/\varrho_q)\,\psi_q
\!+\!u_q(\boldsymbol x)
\ge0,\label{eq:stat_covert_BTI_a}
&\sqrt{\|\boldsymbol U_q\|_F^2+2\|\boldsymbol u_q(\boldsymbol x)\|_2^2}
\le \ell_q,\label{eq:stat_covert_BTI_b}
&\psi_q\mathbf{I}_M+\boldsymbol U_q\succeq\boldsymbol 0,
\psi_q\ge0,\label{eq:stat_covert_BTI_c}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
where $\boldsymbol{\ell}=[\ell_1,\ldots,\ell_Q]^{\mathrm T}$ and $\boldsymbol{\psi}=[\psi_1,\ldots,\psi_Q]^{\mathrm T}$ are vectors of auxiliary variables. Due to the affine dependence of $\bm\Xi$ on $\boldsymbol W_k$ and $\boldsymbol{R}$, constraints \eqref{eq:stat_covert_BTI_a}--\eqref{eq:stat_covert_BTI_c} are convex in the beamforming covariance matrices when the antenna positions are fixed.
Therefore, for a fixed SINR threshold $\tau$ and a fixed APV $\boldsymbol x$, the beamforming feasibility problem under statistical CSI errors is formulated as
\begin{subequations}\label{p2_stat_feas}
\begin{align}
\mathmakebox[2em][r]{\text{find}}\;
&\quad \boldsymbol W_k,\boldsymbol{R},\boldsymbol{\ell},\boldsymbol{\psi}

\mathmakebox[2em][r]{\text{s.t.}}\;
&\quad\eqref{Const_stat_sinr},\eqref{eq:stat_covert_BTI_a},\eqref{eq:stat_covert_BTI_b},\eqref{eq:stat_covert_BTI_c},\eqref{Const_stat_power},\eqref{Const_stat_RA_split},\eqref{Const_stat_rank}.
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
Similarly, by dropping the rank-one constraint in \eqref{Const_stat_rank}, Problem~\eqref{p2_stat_feas} is transformed into an SDP with second-order cone constraints and can be solved by standard convex solvers. 
Note that, for a feasible solution with $\tau>0$, the rank-one recovery in \eqref{SDR_optimal} preserves the user SINRs, total covariance matrix, and original outage-constrained covertness constraint.
\subsection{Optimizing MA Positions}\label{subsec:MA_position_update_statCSI}
In each outer iteration, we retain the relaxed covariance matrices $\{\overline{\boldsymbol W}_k\}_{k\in\mathcal K}$ and $\overline{\boldsymbol R}$, together with their feasible BTI auxiliary variables, after rank-one recovery. The recovered beamforming vectors and AN covariance matrix are held fixed for evaluating the user SINRs. At each inner SCA iteration $t$, the auxiliary variables $\alpha_k^{(t)}$ are updated via \eqref{eq:alpha_update_position} at the current position $\boldsymbol x^{(t)}$, using $\boldsymbol w_k=\boldsymbol w_k^\star$ and $\boldsymbol\Xi_k=\overline{\boldsymbol R}-\boldsymbol W_k^\star$. The SINR constraint is then handled by \eqref{eq:sinr_sca_constraint_position}.
For the covertness constraints in this subsection, we use the fixed matrix $\overline{\bm\Xi}\triangleq(1+\eta)\sum_{k\in\mathcal K_c}\overline{\boldsymbol W}_k-\eta\overline{\boldsymbol R}$. Specifically, $\bm\Xi$ is replaced by $\overline{\bm\Xi}$ in \eqref{eq:stat_Uq_def}--\eqref{eq:stat_scalar_def}, in $\varkappa_q(\boldsymbol x)$, and in its upper bound \eqref{eq:varkappa_q_upper_bound} and Hessian bound in Appendix~\ref{AppSINR_a}. All covertness approximations below use this same matrix throughout the inner SCA loop. Note that $\boldsymbol U_q$ is independent of $\boldsymbol x$, whereas $\boldsymbol u_q(\boldsymbol x)$ and $u_q(\boldsymbol x)$ depend on $\boldsymbol x$ through $\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol x)$.
For constraint \eqref{eq:stat_covert_BTI_b}, define
\begin{equation}\label{eq:varsigma_def}
\varsigma_q(\boldsymbol x)
\triangleq
\|\boldsymbol u_q(\boldsymbol x)\|_2^2
=
\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q^H(\boldsymbol x)\,
\boldsymbol A_q\,
\bar{\boldsymbol h}_q(\boldsymbol x),
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol A_q\triangleq\overline{\bm\Xi}\boldsymbol E_q\overline{\bm\Xi}
=\left(\boldsymbol E_q^{1/2}\overline{\bm\Xi}\right)^H\!\left(\boldsymbol E_q^{1/2}\overline{\bm\Xi}\right)
\succeq\boldsymbol 0$ is a constant Hermitian matrix with the beamforming
covariance matrices fixed. Since $\varsigma_q(\boldsymbol x)$ has the same
quadratic form as $\varkappa_q(\boldsymbol x)$, its Hessian bound follows
from Appendix~\ref{AppSINR_a} by replacing $\bm\Xi$ with
$\boldsymbol A_q$, i.e.,
$\nabla_{\boldsymbol{x}}^2 \varsigma_q(\boldsymbol{x})
\preceq\varpi_{\varsigma,q}\mathbf{I}_M$ with
\begin{equation}
\varpi_{\varsigma,q}
=
\frac{16\pi^2 (M-1)}{\lambda^2}|\beta_q|^2\max_{m,n\in\mathcal{M}}\left|[\boldsymbol A_q]_{m,n}\right|.
\end{equation}
Based on the above Hessian bound, a global quadratic upper bound on $\varsigma_q(\boldsymbol x)$ is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:varsigma_q_upper_bound}
\begin{aligned}
\varsigma_q(\boldsymbol x)\le\,
\varsigma_q(\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)})
& + \nabla_{\boldsymbol{x}}\varsigma_q(\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)})^T
(\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)})
&
+ \frac{\varpi_{\varsigma,q}}{2}
\|\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)}\|_2^2 \triangleq \bar{\varsigma}_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol{x}),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where equality holds at $\boldsymbol x=\boldsymbol x^{(t)}$.
For $\varpi_{\varsigma,q}>0$, the quadratic upper bound in \eqref{eq:varsigma_q_upper_bound} can be equivalently rewritten as
$\bar{\varsigma}_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol{x})
=\frac{\varpi_{\varsigma,q}}{2}\|\boldsymbol x-\boldsymbol v_q^{(t)}\|_2^2
+d_q^{(t)}$, where
$\boldsymbol v_q^{(t)}
\triangleq
\boldsymbol x^{(t)}-\frac{1}{\varpi_{\varsigma,q}}
\nabla_{\boldsymbol{x}}\varsigma_q(\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)})$ and
$d_q^{(t)}
\triangleq
\varsigma_q(\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)})
-\frac{\|\nabla_{\boldsymbol{x}}\varsigma_q(\boldsymbol{x}^{(t)})\|_2^2}
{2\varpi_{\varsigma,q}}$.
Moreover, since \eqref{eq:varsigma_q_upper_bound} holds for all $\boldsymbol x$
and $\varsigma_q(\boldsymbol x)\ge0$, we have
$d_q^{(t)}=\min_{\boldsymbol x}\bar{\varsigma}_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol{x})
\ge\varsigma_q(\boldsymbol v_q^{(t)})\ge0$.
Therefore, a sufficient second-order cone condition for
constraint \eqref{eq:stat_covert_BTI_b} is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:stat_position_BTI_b_soc}
\left\|
\left[
\|\boldsymbol U_q\|_F,\,
\sqrt{\varpi_{\varsigma,q}}\,(\boldsymbol x-\boldsymbol v_q^{(t)})^T,\,
\sqrt{2d_q^{(t)}}
\right]^T
\right\|_2
\le \ell_q,
\forall q\in\mathcal Q.
\end{equation}
Note that if $\varpi_{\varsigma,q}=0$, $\varsigma_q(\boldsymbol x)$ is constant, and \eqref{eq:stat_position_BTI_b_soc} is replaced by $\sqrt{\|\boldsymbol U_q\|_F^2+2\varsigma_q(\boldsymbol x^{(t)})}\le\ell_q$.
For constraint \eqref{eq:stat_covert_BTI_a}, by applying the upper bound in \eqref{eq:varkappa_q_upper_bound}, we have
\begin{equation}\label{eq:stat_position_BTI_a_sca}
\begin{aligned}
&\mathrm{Tr}(\boldsymbol U_q)
-\sqrt{2\ln(1/\varrho_q)}\,\ell_q
-\ln(1/\varrho_q)\,\psi_q
&\quad+\eta\sigma_q^2-\bar{\varkappa}_q^{(t)}(\boldsymbol x)
\ge0,
\quad\forall q\in\mathcal Q.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Thus, the resulting subproblem for MA position optimization can be formulated as
\begin{subequations}\label{p3_stat_sca}
\begin{align}
\mathmakebox[2em][r]{\underset{\mathclap{\boldsymbol x,\tau,\boldsymbol{\ell},\boldsymbol{\psi}}}{\max}}\; &\quad
\tau

\mathmakebox[2em][r]{\text{s.t.}}\;
&\quad\eqref{eq:sinr_sca_constraint_position},
\eqref{eq:stat_position_BTI_a_sca},
\eqref{eq:stat_position_BTI_b_soc},
\eqref{eq:stat_covert_BTI_c},\eqref{Const_4},\eqref{Const_5},
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
which is convex and can be optimally solved using the CVX toolbox.
\subsection{Algorithm Framework and Complexity Analysis}\label{subsec:algorithm_complexity}
The overall BCD procedure is summarized in Algorithm~\ref{alg:robust_bcd}.
The bisection search is detailed in Algorithm~\ref{alg:robust_bisection}. 
We next analyze the complexity of the convex subproblems using the LMI and SOC counting method in~\cite{WangOutageConic2014}. The beamforming and AN covariance matrix subproblem has complexity $\mathcal C_{{\rm B},{\rm WC}}=\mathcal O\bigl({(K+Q)^{1.5}}\allowbreak M^{2.5}\allowbreak{(KM^2+Q)^2}\bigr)$ for the worst-case optimization scheme and $\mathcal C_{{\rm B},{\rm ST}}=\mathcal O\bigl({(K+Q)^{1.5}}\allowbreak M^{2.5}\allowbreak{(KM^2+2Q)^2}\bigr)$ for the statistical optimization scheme. The MA position subproblem has complexity $\mathcal C_{{\rm X},{\rm WC}}=\mathcal O\bigl(M^2(M+2Q)\allowbreak(QM+K)^{0.5}\allowbreak(QM+2Q^2+K)\bigr)$ for the worst-case optimization scheme and $\mathcal C_{{\rm X},{\rm ST}}=\mathcal C_{{\rm X},{\rm WC}}$ for the statistical optimization scheme.
To assess the computational cost in practice, we conduct a runtime test using the default parameters in Section~V. The test is performed on a computer equipped with an Intel Core i7-12700 processor. The average runtimes per outer BCD iteration are $3.42$ s and $4.17$ s for the worst-case and statistical optimization schemes, respectively.
