Fig.~\ref{fig:transmit_power} illustrates the weighted max-min SINR versus the maximum transmit power $P$ for different transmission blocklengths.
It can be observed that the weighted max-min SINRs of all schemes increase as $P$ increases. 
This is because a larger transmit-power budget provides more power for the transmit signal while satisfying the covertness constraints. Moreover, the proposed MA schemes outperform the corresponding FPA schemes by properly optimizing the antenna positions to enhance the desired channels and mitigate interference. It can also be observed that the weighted max-min SINR achieved with $N=128$ is lower than that with $N=32$.
This is because a larger $N$ provides each warden with more observations, improving the accuracy of received power estimation and the reliability of covert transmission detection.
For a fixed received power ratio, the KL divergence in \eqref{eq:KL_div} increases linearly with $N$.
For a fixed $\epsilon$, the threshold $\eta$ therefore decreases, imposing more stringent constraints on covert signal leakage.
These constraints restrict the feasible region and limit the achievable weighted max-min SINR.
