Fig.~\ref{fig:willie_number} illustrates the effect of the number of wardens $Q$ on the weighted max-min SINR under the two CSI error models. To more clearly illustrate the impact of increasing $Q$, we consider a scenario with one overt user and one covert user. The azimuth angle from Alice to the center of the user region is $90^\circ$. It can be observed that the weighted max-min SINR decreases as $Q$ increases. This is because each additional warden attempts to detect the covert transmission, making the overall covertness requirements stricter. It can also be observed that, for the same number of antennas, the statistical optimization scheme achieves higher weighted max-min SINR than the worst-case scheme.
For instance, at $Q=3$, the statistical scheme outperforms the worst-case scheme by approximately $0.86$ dB and $1.05$ dB for $M=5$ and $M=6$, respectively.
This is because the worst-case scheme requires the covertness constraints to hold for all channel errors within the uncertainty set.
These more stringent constraints reduce the flexibility in power allocation and antenna positioning, leading to a lower weighted max-min SINR.
